  { id:'opqb-t13-513', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Retinal detachment** is a result of:',
    options:['Separation between the external limiting membrane and the outer nuclear layer.','Separation between the outer nuclear layer and the outer plexiform layer.','Separation between the RPE and the neurosensory retina.','Separation between the outer plexiform layer and the inner nuclear layer.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The retina detaches at one plane and only one: between the retinal pigment epithelium and everything in front of it.**\n\n`L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` never says \"cleavage plane\" in those words, but every one of its three mechanisms puts the fluid in the same place — the **subretinal space**, which is by definition the space between the neurosensory retina and the RPE:\n\n| Type | `L20`'s own wording |\n|---|---|\n| **Rhegmatogenous** | \"Vitreous fluid … seeps through the tear and collects in the **subretinal space**, physically pushing the retina away from its underlying blood supply.\" |\n| **Tractional** | \"Scar tissue contracts on the surface of the retina, creating 'traction' that **lifts it**.\" |\n| **Exudative** | \"Fluid from the choroid … seep into the **subretinal space**.\" |\n\nThe deck's slide-10 cross-section labels the layers in order — \"Retinal Break (Tear/Hole) · Retina · **Subretinal Space** · **Retinal Pigment Epithelium (RPE)** · Choroid · Sclera\" — and the gap that opens in the drawing sits exactly between *Retina* and *RPE*.\n\n**Why that plane and not another.** The nine layers listed on `L20` slide 4 (nerve fibre → ganglion cell → inner plexiform → inner nuclear → outer plexiform → outer nuclear → rods and cones → RPE) are not held together equally. Everything from the internal limiting membrane down to the photoreceptor outer segments is bound into one sheet by Müller cells, whose processes span the whole thickness and form the external and internal limiting membranes the deck labels on the same slide. The RPE, by contrast, only *touches* the photoreceptor outer segments — it is a separate epithelium held against them by the pump that keeps the subretinal space dry, with no cell junctions crossing the interface. Take the pump away, or push fluid in faster than it can be cleared, and the sheet lifts cleanly off. *(That the apposition is maintained by RPE pumping and by interphotoreceptor matrix adhesion rather than by junctions is **not taken from the course material**; the decks give the anatomy and the three mechanisms, not the adhesion physiology.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** each names a split *inside* the neurosensory retina. Options A, B and D would separate the outer nuclear layer from the external limiting membrane, from the outer plexiform layer, or split the outer plexiform from the inner nuclear layer respectively. A split within the sensory retina itself has a different name — retinoschisis — and `L20` does not cover it (its own NOT-COVERED list records \"RETINOSCHISIS (degenerative or juvenile X-linked) — not named\"). None of the three is a detachment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retinal detachment is separation of the neurosensory retina from the RPE, so subretinal fluid collects at that single plane; a split within the sensory retina is not a detachment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q513), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-514', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Central retinal vein occlusion** is characterized by all of these, **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Retinal veins are dilated and tortuous','Retinal hemorrhages','Optic disc edema','Cherry red spot'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"CRVO shows dilated tortuous veins, retinal hemorrhages, optic disc edema. **Cherry red spot is seen in central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO).**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints both sign lists on facing slides, and the box above is simply the difference between them.**\n\n| | **CRVO** (`L19.2`, both types) | **CRAO** (`L19.2`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Veins | \"Marked venous tortuosity and **engorgement**\" (ischaemic) / \"Mild venous tortuosity and dilatation\" (non-ischaemic) | \"Arteriolar **and venular narrowing**\" |\n| Haemorrhage | \"**Extensive** retinal haemorrhages\" / \"Mild to moderate retinal haemorrhages\" | none listed |\n| Disc | \"**Severe disc oedema**\" / \"Mild to moderate disc oedema\" | none listed |\n| Macula | \"Macular ischaemia\", \"Chronic macular oedema\" | **\"'Cherry-red spot' at macula\"** |\n| Acuity, APD | VA < 6/60, APD marked (ischaemic) · VA > CF, APD mild (non-ischaemic) | VA < 6/60, APD marked |\n\n**Why a cherry-red spot cannot happen in a vein occlusion.** The spot is not a lesion. It is the *normal* macula seen against an abnormal background. When the central retinal artery is blocked, the inner retina infarcts and turns opaque white — `L19.2` prints \"Retinal whitening\" — but at the fovea the inner layers are absent (`L20` slide 5, panel D: \"the retina thins to a pit with the inner layers absent\"), so there is nothing there to swell and go white. The intact choroidal circulation shows through that thin window as its normal orange-red, and the contrast against the surrounding white retina makes it look like a red cherry sitting in cream. A vein occlusion does not infarct the inner retina in that uniform way — it floods it with blood and oedema — so the background never whitens and the contrast never appears.\n\n**Why the other three are all true of CRVO:** venous stagnation is the primary event (`L19.2`'s pathophysiology flow chart runs \"Venous Occlusion → Stagnation → increased extravascular pressure and hypoxia → **Oedema and haemorrhage**\"), which produces the dilated tortuous veins directly, the haemorrhages from the raised pressure in the venous bed, and the disc oedema from stasis at the point where the vein leaves the eye through the lamina cribrosa.\n\n**Read alongside `opqb-t13-519`**, which asks nearly the same question with the same option A and a different distractor set — and therefore a different key. A stem match is not a question match.",
    objective:'A cherry-red spot belongs to arterial occlusion, not venous: CRVO gives dilated tortuous veins, haemorrhages and disc oedema, and never whitens the retina behind the fovea.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q514), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-515', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'The causes of a **marked drop of vision** in patients suffering from **diabetic retinopathy** are due to all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Diabetic macular edema','Tractional retinal detachment affecting the macula','Vitreous hemorrhage','Retinal drusen'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Drusen is associated with AMD,** not diabetic retinopathy.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` defines drusen and puts them somewhere diabetes never reaches:** \"Drusen are hyaline nodules (or colloid bodies) deposited in **Bruch's membrane**, which separates the inner choroidal vessels from the retinal pigment epithelium.\" Bruch's membrane sits *behind* the RPE, on the choroidal side. Diabetic retinopathy is a disease of the **retinal capillary bed**, in front of the RPE — `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` gives its whole pathogenesis as \"Microvascular occlusion\" and \"Microvascular leakage\". The two diseases work on opposite sides of the pigment epithelium, which is why drusen are the odd one out. The same deck adds that drusen alone barely affect vision at all: \"Patients with drusen alone tend to have **normal or near normal visual acuity**, with minimal metamorphopsia\" — so even in the eye where they do belong, they are not a cause of a *marked* drop.\n\n**The other three are the three ways diabetes actually takes vision, and `L19.1` prints all three:**\n\n| Option | Where the deck puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Diabetic macular edema** | The whole \"Clinically significant macular oedema\" slide, with its 500 µm and one-disc-diameter criteria, plus the three maculopathies — focal (\"good prognosis\"), diffuse (\"guarded prognosis\") and **ischaemic** (\"Macula appears relatively normal · **Poor visual acuity** · Treatment not appropriate\"). |\n| **Tractional retinal detachment affecting the macula** | Listed among the indications for vitreoretinal surgery: \"**Retinal detachment involving macula**\". `L20` names \"Advanced diabetic retinopathy (**THE MOST COMMON CAUSE**)\" of tractional detachment, and its slide-10 figure specifies *proliferative* diabetic retinopathy. |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage** | Also a surgical indication: \"**Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage**\" and \"Dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage\". This bank builds the vignette at `opqb-t13-549` — the diabetic who loses vision overnight. |\n\n**A useful way to hold the three together:** diabetes blurs vision *slowly* through the macula, and blinds it *suddenly* through blood in the vitreous or through the retina being pulled off. Drusen do neither, and belong to a different patient — the one in `opqb-t13-537` and `opqb-t13-559`.",
    objective:'Drusen sit in Bruch membrane and belong to AMD; diabetic vision loss comes from macular oedema, tractional detachment at the macula, or vitreous haemorrhage.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q515), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-516', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'In some patients with **central retinal artery occlusion** the **central visual field may be spared**. This may be explained by:',
    options:['Patent retinal veins','Patent retinal arteries','Patent cilioretinal artery','Patent external carotid artery'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"20% of people have additional supply from **cilioretinal artery → supply macula)**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, unmatched bracket included. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives the cilioretinal artery its own slide and its own two facts:** \"Cilioretinal artery **derived from posterior ciliary circulation**\" and \"**Present in about 30% of individuals**\".\n\n⚠️ **The bank's box says 20 %, the deck says about 30 %.** Both figures are in circulation in the literature; the discrepancy is recorded, not resolved, and it does not touch the key — the question turns on *which vessel*, not on how common it is.\n\n**The whole answer is in the word \"cilioretinal\".** The central retinal artery and the posterior ciliary arteries are two separate branches of the ophthalmic artery. In most eyes the entire inner retina, macula included, is fed by the central retinal artery alone, so occluding it infarcts everything. In a minority, one small vessel comes off the **posterior ciliary** circulation instead, emerges at the temporal edge of the disc and runs to the papillomacular bundle. When the central retinal artery blocks, that vessel is on the other side of the obstruction and keeps flowing — so the macula it supplies stays perfused, stays pink, and keeps seeing. The patient loses the whole peripheral field and retains a small central island. `L19.2` shows the same thing on fluorescein: under \"FA of central retinal artery occlusion\" it captions the three phases \"**Early filling of cilioretinal artery** · Non-filling of other vessels · Late staining of vessel walls\" — the one vessel that fills is the one that is spared.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it cannot spare the macula |\n|---|---|\n| **Patent retinal veins** | Veins carry blood *away*. A patent vein downstream of a blocked artery drains an unperfused retina; it delivers no oxygen. `L19.2` in fact prints \"Arteriolar **and venular** narrowing\" as a CRAO sign — the veins collapse too, and the blood in them breaks into segments (\"Sludging and segmentation of blood column — **cattle-trucking**\"). |\n| **Patent retinal arteries** | Circular. The branch retinal arteries all arise *from* the central retinal artery, so if it is occluded they are unfilled by definition. This option describes branch occlusion, a different disease (`L19.2` gives BRAO its own slide: \"VA - variable · APD mild or absent · Retina whitening · Arteriolar narrowing\"). |\n| **Patent external carotid artery** | The wrong carotid. The eye is supplied by the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the **internal** carotid; the external carotid supplies the face and scalp. It is patent in almost every CRAO patient and spares nothing. *(The internal-carotid origin of the ophthalmic artery is **not taken from the course material** — `L19.2` covers carotid imaging and endarterectomy but never states which carotid feeds the eye.)* |\n\n**Consequence worth carrying:** this is the same anatomy that makes `opqb-t13-572`'s option C — CRAO *with* a cilioretinal artery — a cause of a tubular field. The peripheral retina is gone and a central island survives, which is what a tubular field is.",
    objective:'A cilioretinal artery arises from the posterior ciliary circulation, so it is upstream of a central retinal artery block and can keep the macula perfused.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q516), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-517', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'**Stimulation of one retina** will result into electric activity in:',
    options:['Contralateral visual cortex','Ipsilateral visual cortex','Both visual cortices','Contralateral sensory cortex'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Retina projects bilaterally: nasal fibers cross at the optic chiasm, temporal fibers remain ipsilateral → **stimulation of one retina activates both visual cortices**.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**One retina, two destinations.** Every retina is split down a vertical line through the fovea:\n\n| Half of the retina | Where its fibres go at the chiasm | Which cortex receives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nasal** half | **Decussate** — cross to the opposite optic tract | **Contralateral** occipital cortex |\n| **Temporal** half | Do **not** cross — stay in the tract of the same side | **Ipsilateral** occipital cortex |\n\nSo a flash delivered to the whole of one retina lights up nasal and temporal fibres together, and the signal arrives at both occipital lobes. Only a *half*-retinal stimulus would go to one cortex alone.\n\n**Do not confuse the retina with the visual field.** The two are mirrored, and the question is asked about the retina, which is the less familiar way round. The left half of each visual **field** is seen by the right half of each retina and processed in the **right** occipital lobe — that is the rule behind every homonymous hemianopia. But an entire **retina** is not a half-field; it carries both halves of its own eye's field, so it must project to both hemispheres. A candidate who reads \"retina\" as \"field\" answers A and is wrong for a reason worth understanding.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Contralateral visual cortex** | True of the **nasal** half only. It is the half-answer, and the commonest wrong answer, because it is what the chiasmal decussation is usually taught for. |\n| **Ipsilateral visual cortex** | True of the **temporal** half only — the fibres that stay uncrossed. Again half the retina. |\n| **Contralateral sensory cortex** | Wrong lobe entirely. Vision terminates in the **occipital** cortex around the calcarine sulcus; the primary somatosensory cortex is in the parietal lobe and receives nothing from the eye. |\n\n*(The decussation itself is the module's own material — `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` is the deck named for it. The calcarine/parietal distinction in the last row is general anatomy and is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why the fact is not merely academic:** it is the reason an electrophysiological recording over one occipital lobe cannot localise a monocular retinal problem, and the reason a full-field VEP — the test keyed at `opqb-t13-581` — tests a pathway rather than a hemisphere.",
    objective:'Nasal fibres cross and temporal fibres do not, so whole-retina stimulation reaches both occipital cortices; only a half-retina projects to one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q517), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-518', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'**Retinitis pigmentosa** is characterized by:',
    options:['Pigmentary degeneration of the retina','Chronic inflammation of the retina','Secondary optic atrophy','Single focus of retinal pigmentation'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The name is a misnomer and the question is testing whether you know it.** Retinitis pigmentosa is not a retinitis. It is a **hereditary, progressive, degenerative** disease of the photoreceptors and the retinal pigment epithelium — a *dystrophy* — that was named in the nineteenth century when any retinal disease with pigment in it was assumed to be inflammatory. The pigment is the tombstone of dead retina, not the product of inflammation.\n\n**What actually happens.** Rods die first and in the greatest numbers, and they are concentrated in the **mid-peripheral** retina. As they go, the RPE overlying them proliferates and migrates into the sensory retina along the perivascular spaces, producing the **bone-spicule** pigmentation that gives the disease its name — scattered, bilateral, symmetrical and peripheral, not one patch. The retinal arterioles attenuate as the retina they supplied stops needing blood, and the disc eventually goes waxy pale. The clinical consequences follow the anatomy exactly, and this bank prints them in `opqb-t13-527`'s explanation box: night blindness first (rods), then \"tunnel vision (**due to rod atrophy**)\", then central loss when the macula is finally reached.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Chronic inflammation of the retina** | The trap the name sets. There is no inflammatory infiltrate, no cells in the vitreous, no response to steroids, and the disease is inherited — autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive or X-linked. A true chronic retinitis (toxoplasma, CMV, syphilis) is usually unilateral or focal, has active borders and shows vitritis. |\n| **Secondary optic atrophy** | Gets the direction of the arrow backwards. Optic atrophy in RP is **consecutive** — it follows the loss of the retinal ganglion cells whose axons make the nerve, and the disc goes *waxy pale*. \"Secondary\" optic atrophy is the atrophy that follows a **swollen** disc (papilloedema, papillitis), which is not what happens here. This bank keys the same distinction in its Neuro-Ophthalmology topic. It is also, incidentally, the reason `opqb-t13-527` keys option B **false**: visual acuity in RP falls because the *macula* is involved, not because of an optic atrophy. |\n| **Single focus of retinal pigmentation** | A solitary pigmented patch is a different lesion entirely — congenital hypertrophy of the RPE, an old chorioretinal scar, or a naevus. RP is diffuse, bilateral and symmetrical; a single focus argues against it. |\n\n**⚠️ Source note — this entry is a gap-fill, and the gap was measured, not assumed.** \"Retinitis pigmentosa\" returns exactly **two hits across all thirty cached lecture decks**, and both were opened: `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 96 lists it as one of seven items on a **family-history** bar chart, and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` line 167 lists it among the **causes of congenital cataract**. Neither teaches the disease. There is no cached book chapter for the retina either (`content\\ophtho\\book\\` holds chapters 1, 3–7, 10, 14–17 and 19 only). **The pathology above is therefore not taken from the course material**, apart from the symptom list, which this bank prints itself at `opqb-t13-527`. Six questions in this topic — `opqb-t13-518`, `opqb-t13-527`, `opqb-t13-542`, `opqb-t13-553`, `opqb-t13-572` and `opqb-t13-579` — stand on it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited pigmentary degeneration of photoreceptors and RPE, diffuse and bilateral — not an inflammation, not a single focus, and its optic atrophy is consecutive rather than secondary.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q518), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-519', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Central retinal vein thrombosis** manifestations do **NOT** include:',
    options:['Retinal veins are dilated and tortuous','Severe pain & headache','Superficial hemorrhage all over the fundus','Impairment of vision'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A vein occlusion is a painless event, and that is the single most useful thing about it clinically.**\n\n`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the sign lists for both forms of CRVO, and **neither contains pain, headache, redness or any other symptom of ocular discomfort**:\n\n| | Non-ischaemic CRVO | Ischaemic CRVO |\n|---|---|---|\n| Acuity | VA > CF | **VA < 6/60** |\n| Pupil | APD mild | **APD marked** |\n| Veins | Mild venous tortuosity and dilatation | **Marked venous tortuosity and engorgement** |\n| Haemorrhage | Mild to moderate retinal haemorrhages | **Extensive retinal haemorrhages** |\n| Cotton-wool spots | Variable | Variable |\n| Disc | Mild to moderate disc oedema | **Severe disc oedema** |\n| Macula | Chronic macular oedema | Macular ischaemia |\n| Prognosis | Guarded; may convert to ischaemic | Very poor; **rubeosis iridis in 50 %** |\n\n**Why there is no pain.** The retina has no pain fibres at all — this bank states it itself at `opqb-t13-565`, whose option A reads \"the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light\". The pain-sensitive structures of the eye are the cornea, the iris and ciliary body, and the sclera; a thrombosis of the retinal vein touches none of them. The whole of the classical presentation is a patient who notices, often on waking, that one eye has gone blurred, with nothing to feel and nothing to see from outside.\n\n**What the pain option is really there for.** \"Severe pain & headache\" is the profile of **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, and it is the distractor the bank uses to test whether a sudden painless loss is being separated from a sudden painful one. `opqb-t13-522` — the same disease as a vignette — offers acute angle-closure as its option D for exactly the same reason. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` opens with a two-line classification, \"1. Painful / 2. Painless\", and then never populates it; this pair of questions is where that unfinished slide gets used.\n\n**⚠️ There is one late CRVO complication that IS painful, and it is not what the option describes.** Ischaemic CRVO produces \"**Rubeosis irides in 50%**\" (`L19.2`), and new vessels on the iris can shut the angle and cause neovascular glaucoma — a red, painful, high-pressure eye, weeks to months later. That is a complication of the occlusion, not a manifestation of it, and the stem asks about manifestations. `opqb-t13-578` puts the relationship the other way round: open-angle glaucoma is a *predisposing factor* for CRVO.\n\n**Why the other three are true:** dilated tortuous veins are the direct mechanical result of the block; \"superficial hemorrhage all over the fundus\" is the *extensive* haemorrhage of the table above, and the word **all over** matters — a CRVO bleeds in all four quadrants, where a branch occlusion bleeds in one sector (`opqb-t13-576` uses that sectorial logic); and impairment of vision is why the patient came.\n\n**Near-repeat inside this topic:** `opqb-t13-514` asks the same disease with the **same option A** and a different distractor set, and therefore keys differently (cherry-red spot). Reading one and assuming the other is the trap.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Central retinal vein occlusion is painless — dilated tortuous veins, four-quadrant haemorrhages and visual loss; pain and headache point to angle-closure instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q519), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-520', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Simple retinal detachment** is characterized by the following criteria **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Low intraocular pressure','Presence of retinal tears','History of trauma may be present','High intraocular pressure','Eye is usually myopic.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Simple RD** usually has **low or normal IOP;** high IOP is not typical.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**\"Simple\" retinal detachment is this bank's term for the rhegmatogenous type** — the detachment that begins with a hole. Every one of the four true options is a property of that type, and the fifth contradicts the first.\n\n**⚠️ Note the shape of the question before anything else: it prints FIVE options, A to E.** It is the only five-option question in the topic, and **options A and D are direct opposites**. When a list contains a statement and its negation, one of the two must be the answer — the work is deciding which.\n\n**Why the pressure falls.** A retinal break is a hole in a membrane that separates two fluid compartments. Vitreous fluid passes through it into the subretinal space, and from there it is absorbed by the choroid, so the eye is losing volume through a leak that the ciliary body has to keep replacing. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` describes the flow without naming the pressure: \"Vitreous fluid … seeps through the tear and collects in the subretinal space, physically pushing the retina away from its underlying blood supply.\" The detached, ischaemic retina and the disturbed ciliary body also reduce aqueous production. The result is the classically **soft eye** — a hypotonic globe, sometimes several millimetres of mercury below the fellow eye, which is a genuinely useful bedside sign. ⚠️ **`L20` never prints an IOP figure of any kind** — its own NOT-COVERED list records \"IOP in retinal detachment (the classically LOW IOP)\" as absent — so **the mechanism above is not taken from the course material**; the bank's box supplies the direction of the change and the reasoning is added here.\n\n**Why the other three are true of a rhegmatogenous detachment:**\n\n| Option | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|\n| **Presence of retinal tears** | Definitional. `L20`: \"Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (RRD) … occurs when a physical **TEAR OR BREAK** develops in the retina.\" `opqb-t13-555` keys the same fact directly. |\n| **History of trauma may be present** | `L20` lists the RRD causes as \"Aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia (nearsightedness), or **eye trauma**.\" Note the printed hedge — *may* be present. It is one cause among three, not a requirement. This bank builds two trauma vignettes on it, `opqb-t13-569` and `opqb-t13-571`. |\n| **Eye is usually myopic.** | The same list: \"**high myopia**\". The myopic eye is longer, its vitreous liquefies earlier, and its peripheral retina is thinner — which is why every flashes-and-floaters vignette in this topic (`opqb-t13-545`, `opqb-t13-558`, `opqb-t13-569`, `opqb-t13-571`) specifies a myope. |\n\n**Where a detached eye CAN have a high pressure**, so that the exception is understood rather than memorised: a long-standing detachment can shed photoreceptor outer segments that clog the trabecular meshwork (Schwartz–Matsuo syndrome), and an eye that has had gas or oil put into it at surgery may run high afterwards. Both are unusual, and neither is a *characteristic* of the untreated disease. `opqb-t13-521` keys option D, \"IOP may be elevated\", as one of its **true** statements for exactly that reason — the bank draws the line between *may* and *is characteristically*, and the two questions must be read together or they look contradictory.",
    objective:'A rhegmatogenous (simple) detachment softens the eye: low or normal IOP, a retinal break, possible trauma and usually myopia — a high pressure is the exception, not a criterion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q520), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-521', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'In **retinal detachment (RD)**, the following are true **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['The red reflex is always grey, whatever the size of the pupil, or the position of gaze','In rhegmatogenous RD, the retina is corrugated, mobile, with wavy vessels.','The retina is anteriorly convex in exudative RD','IOP may be elevated'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Red reflex may be normal if **pupil is wide or detachment is peripheral;**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, trailing semicolon included. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The word that makes option A false is \"always\".** A red reflex is light returning from whatever lies behind the pupil at that moment. A detachment that involves only the peripheral retina leaves the posterior pole attached and normally pigmented, so an undilated axial view still comes back red. Dilate the pupil, or ask the patient to look towards the detached quadrant, and the grey elevated retina swings into view — which is why the reflex in a real detachment is **variable with pupil size and direction of gaze**, the exact two conditions the option denies. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` makes the same point from the other side in its RRD case: \"red reex [sic] is clear in the left eye, but the red reflex in the right eye reveals **mobile opacities**\" — an abnormality that is *found* on looking, not one that fills the pupil.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Where the deck puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Corrugated, mobile, wavy vessels in RRD** | `L20`'s differentiation table gives the retinal surface as the discriminator between the three types: \"Rhegmatogenous — **Corrugated/Wrinkled** · Tractional — Smooth/Taut · Exudative — Smooth/Bullous\". A retina held only at the disc and ora floats freely in subretinal fluid, so it ripples with eye movement and its vessels ride over the folds and look wavy. `opqb-t13-543` uses the same word for a *fresh* detachment and keys demarcation lines for an old one. |\n| **Anteriorly convex in exudative RD** | The same table's third column, \"Smooth/**Bullous**\", and `L20` slide 2's photograph of \"a large BULLOUS RETINAL DETACHMENT, the detached retina lifted as a smooth **dome** over the vessels\". Exudative fluid comes from the choroid under hydrostatic pressure and heaps up smoothly, so the surface bulges forward towards the observer. It also **shifts with posture**, though `L20` does not print that sign. |\n| **IOP may be elevated** | Note the hedge, and read it against `opqb-t13-520`, which keys a *high* IOP as NOT characteristic. The two are consistent: the usual pressure in a detachment is low, but it *may* rise — in Schwartz–Matsuo syndrome, where photoreceptor outer segments obstruct the trabecular meshwork; after tamponade with gas or silicone oil; or where the detachment is exudative and the underlying disease (uveitis, tumour) raises the pressure itself. \"May be\" is a much weaker claim than \"is characterized by\". |\n\n*(The Schwartz–Matsuo mechanism, the shifting-fluid sign and the effect of pupil dilatation on the reflex are **not taken from the course material** — `L20` names no eponym anywhere and its NOT-COVERED list records \"SHIFTING SUBRETINAL FLUID as the classic sign of exudative detachment\" as absent. The corrugated/taut/bullous row and the mobile-opacity reflex are printed.)*\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option C as \"The **retinal** is anteriorly convex in exudative RD\" and option A's odd comma placement is its own. The word is repaired to *retina* here; nothing else is changed.",
    objective:'The red reflex in retinal detachment varies with pupil size and gaze and can be normal in a peripheral detachment; corrugation, a bullous exudative dome and an occasionally raised IOP are all real.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.82 (Q521), key p.90 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-522', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **30-year female** patient on **contraceptive pills for 3 years** developed **rapid marked painless diminution of vision in one eye**, the most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Central Retinal Artery Occlusion','Branch retinal vein occlusion','Central retinal vein occlusion','Acute angle closure glaucoma'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This vignette is printed three times in this topic — here, at `opqb-t13-568` and at `opqb-t13-574` — and the full account is written once, here.**\n\n**Four words in the stem do all the work: young, female, on the pill, painless.**\n\n`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` lists the predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion under two headings:\n\n| Systemic | Ocular |\n|---|---|\n| Increasing age · Hypertension · Diabetes · **Abnormalities of coagulation** | Raised intraocular pressure · Periphlebitis |\n\n**The combined oral contraceptive is a prothrombotic state**, and it is the only thing in this woman's history that can put her in the fourth systemic row. She is thirty, so \"increasing age\" does not apply; nothing suggests hypertension or diabetes. The deck also gives the mechanism as a flow chart — \"**Venous Occlusion → Stagnation →** increased extravascular pressure **and** hypoxia **→ Oedema and haemorrhage**\" — which is why the fundus fills with blood and the vision goes in hours to days rather than in seconds.\n\n**Why not each of the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Central Retinal Artery Occlusion** | Wrong tempo and wrong patient. `L19.2` gives arterial occlusion two causes: \"Embolism\" (cardiac or carotid — cholesterol, fibrinoplatelet or calcific) and \"Vaso-obliteration\", of which \"**Atherosclerosis** — most common cause of central artery occlusion\". Both are diseases of older arteries. An arterial occlusion drops vision to counting fingers or worse **in seconds**, not \"rapidly\", and it leaves the fundus white with a cherry-red spot. The deck does allow young arterial occlusion, but through a different door: \"Haematological disorders may cause recurrent occlusions in **young individuals** — protein S deficiency, protein C deficiency, antithrombin III deficiency, sticky platelet syndromes and antiphospholipid antibody syndrome\", which is a thrombophilia work-up, not an oral contraceptive. |\n| **Branch retinal vein occlusion** | Right disease, wrong extent. A branch occlusion damages **one sector** and, because it usually spares the macula, gives a *field defect* or mild blurring rather than a marked drop — `L19.2` gives its prognosis as \"VA 6/12 or better after 6 months in **50%**\". \"Marked diminution\" of the whole eye's vision points to the central vein. `opqb-t13-576` shows what a sectorial vascular occlusion actually does to the field. |\n| **Acute angle closure glaucoma** | Excluded by one printed word: **painless**. Angle closure is the archetypal *painful* sudden loss, with a red eye, a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, nausea and headache. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` opens on the painful/painless split, and this is the one option that sits on the other side of it. It is also the wrong demographic — angle closure favours hypermetropic older women, not thirty-year-olds. |\n\n**Which CRVO is it?** The stem gives \"marked\" visual loss but no acuity figure and no pupil finding, so the ischaemic/non-ischaemic split cannot be made from what is printed. `L19.2` divides them on two numbers — **VA < 6/60 with a marked APD** for ischaemic, **VA > CF with a mild APD** for non-ischaemic — and the difference matters: the ischaemic form carries \"**Rubeosis irides in 50%**\" and needs monthly review for six months, \"Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels\", with panretinal photocoagulation if they appear.\n\n**Siblings in this bank:** `opqb-t13-574` asks the examination (fundoscopy) and `opqb-t13-568` asks the sign — and `opqb-t13-568`'s printed key is defective, which is discussed there.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The combined pill is a coagulation abnormality, the fourth systemic predisposing factor for retinal vein occlusion — so rapid painless monocular loss in a young woman on it is central retinal vein occlusion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q522), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-523', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'**High ICP due to Pseudotumor cerebri** can affect the eye by one of the following:',
    options:['Optic neuritis','Iridocyclitis','Lateral rectus palsy','High intraocular pressure'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The sixth nerve is the longest and the most tethered of the ocular motor nerves, and raised intracranial pressure squeezes it before it squeezes anything else.**\n\nAfter leaving the pons the abducent nerve runs a long upward course over the petrous ridge, where it is held down by the petroclinoid (Gruber's) ligament before entering the cavernous sinus. When the whole brain is displaced downwards by raised pressure, that fixed point becomes a fulcrum and the nerve is stretched against it. The result is a **lateral rectus palsy** — the eye fails to abduct, and the patient gets horizontal diplopia that is worse looking towards the affected side.\n\n**This is the classical false localising sign.** It is called that because a sixth-nerve palsy normally means a lesion at the sixth nucleus or along the nerve, whereas here it means nothing more than that the pressure is high — it points nowhere. It can be unilateral or bilateral. In idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumour cerebri), the other eye finding is **papilloedema**, and the two together are the whole ocular picture: a swollen disc in each eye and a lateral rectus that will not work.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Optic neuritis** | Right nerve, wrong pathology, and the two look different at the disc. Papilloedema from raised ICP is **bilateral**, spares acuity until late, gives an enlarged blind spot and **no relative afferent pupillary defect**; optic neuritis is typically **unilateral**, drops acuity early and severely, hurts on eye movement and gives a marked RAPD. `L21) Optic Nerve` prints a papilloedema-versus-papillitis table for exactly this distinction. Raised ICP swells the disc mechanically by damming axoplasmic flow; it does not inflame the nerve. |\n| **Iridocyclitis** | Anterior segment inflammation has no route from the cerebrospinal fluid. Uveitis is not a consequence of intracranial pressure at any level. |\n| **High intraocular pressure** | The two pressures are separate compartments and do not equilibrate. If anything the relationship runs the other way in clinical thinking: a *low* intraocular pressure against a normal intracranial pressure widens the translaminar gradient, which is one theory of normal-tension glaucoma. Raised ICP does not raise IOP. |\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** This is a neuro-ophthalmology question that happens to be printed in the Retina topic, and the retinal decks do not touch it. `L21) Optic Nerve` covers papilloedema and its distinction from papillitis, and that part is sourced. **The sixth-nerve anatomy, the false-localising-sign concept and the pseudotumour cerebri picture are not taken from the course material.** Note that this bank asks the papilloedema half of the same disease at `opqb-t13-575`, where a headache plus swollen discs plus near-normal acuity keys raised intracranial pressure.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Raised intracranial pressure stretches the long, tethered sixth nerve and causes a lateral rectus palsy — the classical false localising sign, alongside papilloedema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q523), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-524', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'All are used in treating **CRAO EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Ocular massage','Intravitreal triamcinolone','5% CO2','AC paracentesis/tapping'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every treatment in central retinal artery occlusion is an attempt to move an embolus onwards or to widen the vessel it is stuck in. A steroid injected into the vitreous does neither.**\n\nThe retina downstream of the block is infarcting from the moment the vessel closes, so the only useful interventions are the ones that might restore flow within the first hours. Three of the four options are exactly that:\n\n| Option | What it is trying to do |\n|---|---|\n| **Ocular massage** | Rhythmic digital pressure on the globe raises the intraocular pressure and then lets it fall abruptly. The pressure swing shakes the arterial column and may dislodge the embolus into a smaller, more peripheral branch, converting a total occlusion into a partial one. |\n| **5% CO2** | Carbogen — oxygen with about 5 % carbon dioxide — inhaled from a mask. The CO₂ is a powerful retinal **vasodilator**, and the oxygen raises the amount dissolved in whatever plasma still reaches the tissue. The aim is to widen the vessel around the obstruction and to oxygenate by diffusion from the choroid. |\n| **AC paracentesis/tapping** | Draining a small volume of aqueous through a limbal needle drops the intraocular pressure sharply and immediately. The perfusion pressure of the retina is arterial pressure *minus* intraocular pressure, so lowering the IOP raises the pressure gradient across the block — the same reasoning as intravenous acetazolamide, applied faster. |\n\n**Why intravitreal triamcinolone is the exception.** Triamcinolone is a depot corticosteroid, and steroids in the eye do one thing: they suppress **inflammation and vascular leakage**. Their real indications are the oedematous diseases — macular oedema from diabetes or from a vein occlusion, and uveitis. CRAO is neither inflammatory nor oedematous in that sense; it is an ischaemic infarction, and the retina is white because it is dying, not because it is leaking. A steroid does not move a clot, does not dilate an artery and does not lower the intraocular pressure — in fact intravitreal steroid characteristically **raises** IOP in a proportion of eyes, which in this disease is the wrong direction. It would also delay the treatments that might work while the therapeutic window closes.\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE NOTE — THIS ENTIRE ENTRY IS A GAP-FILL, AND THE GAP IS UNUSUALLY EXPLICIT.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` presents CRAO as its Case 1 and reaches the diagnosis on slide 9 — and then slide 10 is headed **\"Management\" and is otherwise completely blank**. The deck's own cached note records that three of its four management slides render byte-identical and that \"the management of central retinal artery occlusion is not taught anywhere in this file\", listing by name what is missing: \"ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, IOP-lowering drops or acetazolamide, rebreathing/carbogen, the 'retina survives ~90 minutes' style time window, thrombolysis, and the stroke/carotid/cardiac work-up\". `L19.2` covers the *causes* of arterial occlusion and the carotid work-up in detail but prints no ocular treatment either. **All four rows above are therefore not taken from the course material.** What *is* sourced is the prognosis, and this bank prints it in its own box at `opqb-t13-567`: \"CRAO has poor prognosis; focus on prophylaxis.\"\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'CRAO treatment aims to dislodge the embolus or improve perfusion — massage, carbogen, paracentesis; intravitreal steroid treats oedema and inflammation and has no role.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q524), key p.90' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-525', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'You **cannot examine the retinal details** by:',
    options:['Direct ophthalmoscopy','Indirect ophthalmoscopy','Retinoscopy','Slit lamp'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Retinoscopy is not a way of looking at the retina. It is a way of measuring the eye's refraction, and it uses the retina only as a screen to bounce light off.**\n\nThe retinoscope throws a streak of light into the pupil and the examiner watches the *reflex* that comes back out as the streak is swept across. Whether the reflex moves with the streak or against it, and how fast, tells you the sign and size of the refractive error, which is then neutralised with trial lenses. The instrument is defocused by design and has no magnification of the fundus at all — a patient with a dense retinal lesion and a patient without one give the same retinoscopy result if their refraction is the same. It is a **refraction test**, and it appears as the wrong answer three more times in this topic for the same reason (`opqb-t13-541`, `opqb-t13-548`, `opqb-t13-556`).\n\n**Why the other three do show retinal detail:**\n\n| Option | What it gives you |\n|---|---|\n| **Direct ophthalmoscopy** | The most magnified view of the three (about ×15), upright and monocular, but with a field of only a few degrees and no stereopsis. Excellent for the disc and macula, poor for the periphery. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` uses it in Case 1: \"The **direct ophthalmoscope** shows that the red reflex is clear in both eyes.\" |\n| **Indirect ophthalmoscopy** | Lower magnification (about ×3–5) and an inverted image, but a wide field, binocular stereopsis and much better penetration of hazy media — which is why it is the instrument for detachment, where the whole periphery matters. |\n| **Slit lamp** | With an auxiliary lens (a +78 D or +90 D held in front of the eye, or a contact lens such as the Goldmann three-mirror), the slit lamp gives a stereoscopic, optically sectioned view of the retina. That is what makes it the clinical instrument for **retinal thickening** — `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints \"Retinal oedema seen as thickening **on biomicroscopy**\" under mild NPDR — and `opqb-t13-536` names the Goldmann three-mirror lens by name for viewing the periphery. |\n\n**The distinction the question is really drawing** is between an instrument that *forms an image of the retina* and one that *analyses light returning from it*. Ophthalmoscopes and the slit lamp with a lens do the first; retinoscopy, and the electrophysiological tests, do the second.\n\n*(The magnification figures and the +78/+90 D lens powers are **not taken from the course material**; the decks name the instruments and the three-mirror lens but print no optics.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retinoscopy measures refraction from the reflex and forms no image of the fundus; direct and indirect ophthalmoscopy and slit-lamp biomicroscopy with a lens all show retinal detail.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q525), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-526', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'All of the following statements are true about **tractional retinal detachment EXCEPT**:',
    options:['It may be caused by trauma','The treatment is always medical','There is always a pathology in the vitreous','Diabetes is the most common cause'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Traction is a mechanical problem, and a mechanical problem needs a mechanical answer.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives tractional detachment its own management slide and there is no medicine on it:\n\n> \"**2. Tractional Detachment (Relieving Pull).** Treatment focuses on removing the mechanical force pulling the retina away: **Vitrectomy & Membrane Peeling** — surgeons remove the vitreous and carefully peel away **fibrovascular membranes** or scar tissue that is pulling on the retina. **Underlying Disease Control** — long-term management involves strict control of the causative condition, most commonly diabetic retinopathy.\"\n\nControlling the diabetes is essential and it is the second half of that slide, but it is **long-term management to stop the process recurring** — it does not release a membrane that is already contracting on the macula. Once the retina is off, only surgery puts it back. The word that makes option B false is **always**, and it would be false even without it.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Where `L20` prints it |\n|---|---|\n| **It may be caused by trauma** | Slide 7's causes: \"Advanced diabetic retinopathy (the most common cause), sickle cell disease, or **previous ocular trauma**.\" |\n| **There is always a pathology in the vitreous** | Slide 7's mechanism: \"**Scar tissue contracts on the surface of the retina**, creating 'traction' that lifts it. **Unlike RRD, there is typically no initial tear.**\" The pulling structure — fibrovascular membrane, fibrous scar, contracting vitreous — is by definition vitreous-side pathology. Slide 10's cross-section labels it: \"Fibrovascular Membranes / Scar Tissue · **Mechanical Pulling Force**\". This is the feature that separates TRD from RRD, where the primary lesion is a hole in the retina. |\n| **Diabetes is the most common cause** | Printed in capitals on slide 7 — \"Advanced diabetic retinopathy (**THE MOST COMMON CAUSE**)\" — repeated on the management slide, and specified as *proliferative* diabetic retinopathy on the slide-10 figure. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists \"**Retinal detachment involving macula**\" among the indications for vitreoretinal surgery. |\n\n**How to recognise it at the slit lamp:** `L20`'s differentiation table gives the retinal surface as the discriminator — rhegmatogenous is \"Corrugated/Wrinkled\", tractional is \"**Smooth/Taut**\", exudative is \"Smooth/Bullous\". A taut, concave, immobile retina drawn towards a fibrovascular stalk is the tractional picture, and it usually stops short of the ora because the pull is focal. Its symptoms differ too: \"Gradual vision loss and **distorted** or wavy vision\", progressing slowly, where a rhegmatogenous detachment is \"often sudden and rapid\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Tractional detachment is released surgically by vitrectomy and membrane peeling; medical control of the diabetes prevents recurrence but never reattaches a retina.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q526), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-527', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'In **retinitis pigmentosa**:',
    options:['Ultrasonography is of great help in diagnosis','Visual acuity usually diminishes due to secondary optic atrophy','It most commonly leads to symptoms after the age of 60','It may lead to complicated cataract'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Clinical features of retinitis pigmentosa;\n\nA. Symptoms:\n1. Night blindness (the main and the earliest complaint of the patient).\n2. Decreased peripheral vision \"tunnel vision\" (due to rod atrophy).\n3. Decreased central vision (macular affection).\n4. **Glare (decreased visual performance in bright light) from posterior subcapsular cataracts. (Common in longstanding cases)**\n\n---\n\n*(The block above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box answers the question in its own fourth line.** \"Posterior subcapsular cataract\" in a longstanding case *is* the complicated cataract of option D — a cataract secondary to another intraocular disease rather than to age. Chronic retinal degeneration disturbs the metabolic environment of the posterior lens surface, and the opacity that forms there is characteristically posterior subcapsular. It scatters light badly for its size, which is why the symptom it produces is **glare** rather than a simple dimming.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Ultrasonography is of great help in diagnosis** | Ultrasound shows *shape and structure* — it is the test for an eye whose media are opaque, for a detachment behind a mature cataract (`opqb-t13-550`, `opqb-t13-536`) or for an intraocular mass. Retinitis pigmentosa changes neither the contour of the globe nor its acoustic interfaces; the diagnosis rests on **fundoscopy** (bone-spicule pigment, attenuated arterioles, waxy disc pallor), the **field**, and the **ERG**, which is flat or grossly reduced early. `opqb-t13-560` keys the ERG for exactly this disease. |\n| **Visual acuity usually diminishes due to secondary optic atrophy** | Right observation, wrong cause. Acuity falls because the **macula** is eventually involved — the box's own third line, \"Decreased central vision (**macular affection**)\". The disc does go pale, but that pallor is *consecutive* to the loss of the ganglion cells whose axons form the nerve, and it follows the visual loss rather than causing it. \"Secondary\" optic atrophy specifically means the atrophy that follows a **swollen** disc, which is not what happens here. `opqb-t13-518` keys the same distinction as its option C. |\n| **It most commonly leads to symptoms after the age of 60** | Far too late. Typical RP declares itself with night blindness in **childhood or adolescence**, with field loss following through early adult life. `opqb-t13-560` builds a whole vignette on this: a man about to marry his cousin, whose prospective father-in-law already has night blindness, annular scotoma and marked defective vision — a pre-marital genetic question that only makes sense in a disease that presents young. And `opqb-t13-542` keys the sex-linked form's visual loss at 50–60 as **false**, because the X-linked variety is the *most severe and earliest*, not the latest. |\n\n**⚠️ The bank contradicts itself between two questions on one point.** This box says \"posterior **subcapsular** cataracts\"; `opqb-t13-542` prints \"posterior **cortical** cataract\" and keys that statement TRUE. Posterior subcapsular is the correct term. Both are keyed as printed, and neither key moves.\n\n**⚠️ Source note:** the symptom list above is the bank's own; the disease is taught in none of the thirty cached decks (measured — \"retinitis pigmentosa\" returns exactly two hits, a family-history bar chart in `L1,2` and a congenital-cataract cause list in `L35,36`, neither of them teaching). The pathology, the ERG and the age of onset are **not taken from the course material**.",
    objective:'Retinitis pigmentosa causes a complicated posterior subcapsular cataract; its acuity loss is macular rather than from secondary optic atrophy, it presents young, and ultrasound has no diagnostic role.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q527), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-528', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Superficial hemorrhages**, which are **flame shaped**, lie in the retinal:',
    options:['Nerve fiber layer.','Ganglion cell layer.','Outer plexiform layer.','Outer nuclear layer.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"All retinal layers are arranged vertically except the nerve fibers layer; arranged horizontally. So, if -for any reason- **hemorrhage occur in the nerve fibers layer, it will be within the fibers and appear in the form of \"Flame-shaped** hemorrhage\". If hemorrhage occur in the other layer, it will appear in the form of \"Dot and plot hemorrhage \".\n\n---\n\n*(The block above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — \"Dot and plot\" for dot-and-blot, and the stray space before the closing quotation mark, are the bank's own. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The shape of a retinal haemorrhage is a map of the tissue it is trapped in.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 4 lists the layers from inner to outer — nerve fibre, ganglion cell, inner plexiform, inner nuclear, outer plexiform, outer nuclear, rods and cones, RPE. Only the first of them is built of axons running **parallel to the retinal surface**, sweeping from the periphery towards the disc in arcuate bundles. Blood escaping into that layer is confined between the fibres and spreads along them, so it takes the form of a **striated, feathery streak that fans out from the vessel** — a flame. Because the fibres are packed nearest the surface, these are also the most *superficial* haemorrhages, and they lie in front of the retinal vessels rather than behind them.\n\nDeeper, the cells and their processes are stacked **vertically**, so blood released there is boxed into a small round or oval pocket by the surrounding columns — the **dot-and-blot** haemorrhage. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints exactly this under mild NPDR: \"Intraretinal **dot and blot** haemorrhages\", and under severe NPDR, \"**Dark blot** haemorrhages\".\n\n**What the shape tells you clinically, which is why it is worth knowing:**\n\n| Shape | Layer | Diseases that produce it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Flame** | Nerve fibre layer | Hypertensive retinopathy (`opqb-t13-532`); vein occlusion (`L19.2`: \"**Flame-shaped** and 'dot-blot' haemorrhages\" in BRVO); disc swelling of any cause (`L31,32` slide 22: \"flame-shaped hemorrhages around the disc\") |\n| **Dot / blot** | Deeper — around the inner nuclear and outer plexiform layers | Diabetic retinopathy, the classical deep haemorrhage |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** the ganglion cell, outer plexiform and outer nuclear layers all have vertically arranged cell bodies and processes, so a haemorrhage in any of them is round, not linear. The **outer plexiform** layer in particular is where the deepest intraretinal haemorrhages sit, and blood there sometimes tracks laterally along Henle's fibres at the macula to give a star or petal pattern — the same architecture that produces the **macular star** of hard exudates in hypertension (`opqb-t13-539`). *(The Henle's-fibre detail is **not taken from the course material**.)*",
    objective:'Nerve fibre layer axons run horizontally, so superficial haemorrhages spread along them as flames; the vertically arranged deeper layers box blood into dots and blots.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q528), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-529', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'**Fluorescein angiography** is used for studying:',
    options:['Orbital vessels.','Retinal vessels.','Lid vessels.','Conjunctival vessels.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Fluorescein angiography exists because the retinal circulation is the only vascular bed in the body that can be photographed directly through a natural window.** Sodium fluorescein is injected into an arm vein, reaches the eye in about ten seconds, and is photographed through a blue exciter filter and a yellow-green barrier filter as it fills the choroid, then the retinal arterioles, then the capillaries, then the veins.\n\n**What it shows, and why nothing else does it as well:** the dye is largely bound to plasma protein and does not cross healthy **tight junctions**, so it stays inside normal retinal vessels. Wherever it escapes, leaks or fails to arrive, the photograph says so. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints an FA slide for every one of its diseases and the captions are the whole teaching:\n\n| Disease | What `L19.2` says the angiogram shows |\n|---|---|\n| BRVO | \"Early - **blocked background fluorescence** due to haemorrhage\" · \"Late - **hyperfluorescence** due to diffuse oedema\" |\n| Non-ischaemic CRVO | \"Good retinal capillary perfusion\" |\n| Ischaemic CRVO | \"**Extensive capillary non-perfusion**\" |\n| BRAO | \"Early masking · **Extreme delay of arterial phase** · Late staining of arterial walls\" |\n| CRAO | \"**Early filling of cilioretinal artery** · Non-filling of other vessels · Late staining of vessel walls\" |\n| Macroaneurysm | \"Immediate uniform filling of macroaneurysm · **Late leakage**\" |\n\n`L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` uses it the same way to separate the three maculopathies — \"**Focal** leakage on FA\", \"**Generalized** leakage on FA\", \"**Capillary non-perfusion** on FA\" — which is a distinction no other test can make and which changes the treatment.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.** All three are opaque or awkward beds that fluorescein cannot usefully image:\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Orbital vessels** | Behind the globe, inside bone and fat, with no optical access. Orbital vasculature is studied by CT, MRI or angiography. |\n| **Lid vessels** | Skin and muscle in the way; there is nothing to photograph and no clinical question that needs it. |\n| **Conjunctival vessels** | Directly visible at the slit lamp already, so nothing is gained; and the conjunctival vessels leak fluorescein freely because they are *fenestrated*, so the study would be uninterpretable. Fluorescein is used on the conjunctiva and cornea, but **topically**, as a stain for epithelial defects — a completely different use of the same dye. |\n\n**Cited elsewhere in this topic:** `opqb-t13-541` keys FA as the best way to examine the retinal *circulation*, and `opqb-t13-551` and `opqb-t13-563` build clinical decisions on it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fluorescein angiography images the retinal (and choroidal) circulation through the pupil, showing leakage, blockage and non-perfusion; the orbit, lids and conjunctiva are studied other ways.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q529), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-530', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Fundus picture of central retinal artery occlusion** includes all of the following manifestations **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Narrowing of retinal arterioles.','Flame-shaped hemorrhages.','Opaque white retina.','Cherry-red spot.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The CRAO fundus is written out in full here; `opqb-t13-554` and `opqb-t13-570` point at this entry.**\n\n`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the whole picture as one list:\n\n> **Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)** — VA < 6/60 · APD marked · **Retinal whitening** · **'Cherry-red spot' at macula** · **Arteriolar and venular narrowing** · Sludging and segmentation of blood column (**cattle-trucking**) · Very poor prognosis\n\nThree of the four options are on that list. **Haemorrhage is not, and cannot be.**\n\n**Why an arterial occlusion does not bleed.** A haemorrhage needs blood arriving under pressure at a vessel that then leaks. In CRAO the supply is cut off upstream, so the vascular bed downstream is empty and collapsing — `L19.2`'s \"arteriolar **and venular** narrowing\" and \"cattle-trucking\" describe a circulation running dry, with the residual column breaking into separated segments that shunt slowly along like trucks in a train. There is no head of pressure to force blood into the tissue. Flame haemorrhages belong to the opposite hydraulics: a **venous** obstruction, where blood keeps arriving and cannot leave, so pressure rises in the capillary bed until it ruptures. `L19.2` gives them to BRVO — \"**Flame-shaped** and 'dot-blot' haemorrhages\" — and to hypertensive retinopathy, and this bank keys them to the nerve fibre layer at `opqb-t13-528` and to hypertension at `opqb-t13-532`.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Sign | Why it appears |\n|---|---|\n| **Opaque white retina** | The inner retina, which the central retinal artery alone supplies, infarcts. Ischaemic cells swell with intracellular oedema and lose transparency, so the normally see-through retina turns cloudy white and the orange choroid can no longer be seen through it. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` puts it in one sentence in its Case 1: \"Retinal examination reveals a **white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot**.\" |\n| **Cherry-red spot** | Not a lesion — the *normal* macula seen against that white background. At the fovea the inner layers are absent (`L20` slide 5: \"the retina thins to a pit with the inner layers absent\"), so there is nothing there to swell and whiten, and the intact **choroidal** circulation shows through the thin window as normal orange-red. The contrast makes it look like a cherry. This is why the sign disappears over weeks as the whitening resolves. |\n| **Narrowing of retinal arterioles** | Empty vessels collapse. Printed as \"Arteriolar and venular narrowing\" — note that the **veins narrow too**, which is a useful discriminator from a vein occlusion, where they engorge. |\n\n**The one exception to the whitening**, worth carrying because this bank asks it at `opqb-t13-516`: if a **cilioretinal artery** is present (`L19.2`: \"present in about 30% of individuals\", derived from the posterior ciliary circulation) it lies upstream of the block, keeps a wedge of macula perfused, and that wedge stays pink and keeps seeing.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'CRAO gives a white infarcted retina, a cherry-red fovea and narrowed arterioles and venules; it does not bleed, because flame haemorrhages need the raised pressure of a venous block.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q530), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-531', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Symptoms of rhegmatogenous retinal detachment** include all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Photopsia.','Floaters.','Sudden pain.','Field defect.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A detaching retina is silent, and that is exactly why it is dangerous — nothing hurts, so nothing forces the patient to come in.**\n\nThe retina contains no pain fibres. This bank states the fact itself in the option it keys correct at `opqb-t13-565`: \"the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light\". Everything it can report, it reports as a **visual** symptom — which is why traction on it is felt as a flash rather than as an ache.\n\n`L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` prints the symptom table, and the rhegmatogenous row contains the other three options and nothing else:\n\n| Type | Key symptoms (`L20` slide 11) | Progression |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rhegmatogenous** | \"**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a **'curtain' or shadow** in the peripheral vision.\" | \"Often sudden and rapid.\" |\n| Tractional | \"Gradual vision loss and **distorted** or wavy vision.\" | Slow or progressive |\n| Exudative | \"Decreased central vision and general blurriness.\" | Varies with the underlying disease |\n\n**How the three true symptoms are generated, in the order the patient gets them:**\n\n| Symptom | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Photopsia** (flashes) | Vitreous traction tugs on the retina at the point of adhesion. The retina answers every stimulus in the only language it has, so mechanical pull is perceived as light. This bank keys the mechanism at `opqb-t13-565`, whose printed box reads \"Flashes = **traction on photoreceptors**\". The flashes are typically **temporal** and worse in the dark. |\n| **Floaters** | The tear opens a retinal vessel and red cells enter the vitreous, throwing shadows on the retina. Keyed at `opqb-t13-564`, box: \"Floaters = **vitreous hemorrhage from tear**\". A sudden shower of new floaters is the change that matters. |\n| **Field defect** | Subretinal fluid tracks under the retina and the detached area stops transducing light. The patient describes it as `L20` does — a \"**curtain**\" coming across from one side — and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` reproduces the wording in its RRD case: \"a disturbance in the temporal field of vision of his right eye, '**like a curtain coming down**'\". Because the retinal image is inverted, a *superior* detachment produces an *inferior* field defect. |\n\n**Why \"sudden pain\" is the exception.** A painful sudden visual loss sends you to a different list altogether — acute angle-closure glaucoma, keratitis, uveitis, endophthalmitis, scleritis, or optic neuritis hurting on eye movement. `L31,32` opens on that split (\"1. Painful / 2. Painless\") and never fills it in; this question, `opqb-t13-519` and `opqb-t13-522` are where it gets used.\n\n**⚠️ Read the stem's word \"symptoms\" carefully.** Painless does not mean sensationless — flashes are a sensation, and so is the curtain. What is absent is *nociception*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Rhegmatogenous detachment announces itself with flashes, floaters and a curtain-like field defect, all painless — the retina has no pain fibres.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.83 (Q531), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-532', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Retinal hemorrhages** seen in **hypertensive retinopathy** are mainly:',
    options:['White centered.','Dot shaped.','Flame shaped.','Sub hyaloid.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints it as the first of the three extravascular signs of hypertensive retinopathy:** \"**Flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages** · Cotton-wool spots and macular star · Disc oedema\".\n\n**Why hypertension bleeds superficially.** Raised arterial pressure damages the arteriolar wall — the deck gives the arteriolar changes as \"Focal\" and \"Generalized\" constriction, and then \"Arteriolosclerosis (A-V changes)\". When such a vessel finally leaks, it leaks near the surface, where the **precapillary arterioles and the radial peripapillary capillaries run inside the nerve fibre layer**. Blood escaping there is trapped between axons that run parallel to the retinal surface and spreads along them as a striated streak. `opqb-t13-528`'s printed box gives the rule in full: \"All retinal layers are arranged vertically except the nerve fibers layer; arranged horizontally … hemorrhage occur in the nerve fibers layer … will appear in the form of 'Flame-shaped hemorrhage'.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually indicates |\n|---|---|\n| **White centered.** | A Roth spot — a haemorrhage with a pale core of fibrin, leucocytes or septic material. `L19.2` gives it to a different group entirely, under \"Retinopathy in blood dyscrasias → Anaemias and Leukaemias: haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and venous tortuosity · Occasionally **Roth spots**\". Classically it also suggests infective endocarditis. Not a hypertensive sign. |\n| **Dot shaped.** | The *deep* haemorrhage, boxed into a round pocket by vertically arranged cells. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints \"Intraretinal **dot and blot** haemorrhages\" as a sign of mild NPDR and \"**Dark blot** haemorrhages\" in severe NPDR. Dot means diabetes far more often than it means hypertension. |\n| **Sub hyaloid.** | Blood in the potential space between the posterior vitreous face and the retina, which pools under gravity into a **boat-shaped** collection with a flat upper fluid level. It signals bleeding of a different order — proliferative diabetic retinopathy (`L19.1` lists \"Dense, persistent **premacular** haemorrhage\" as an indication for vitrectomy), a ruptured macroaneurysm (`L19.2`: \"**Pre-retinal** and retinal haemorrhage\"), or subarachnoid haemorrhage. |\n\n**The rest of the hypertensive fundus, so the sign is placed rather than isolated.** `L19.2` builds it in two stages — first the **arteriolar** response (focal then generalised constriction, then arteriolosclerosis with its arteriovenous crossing changes), then the **extravascular** consequences (flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots with a macular star, and disc oedema in the malignant phase). The deck's own \"Grading of arteriolosclerosis\" slide is a bare title over a raster and its grades are **not in the text layer**, so no grading system is available from the source. `opqb-t13-539` asks the macular star, `opqb-t13-547` and `opqb-t13-573` build hypertensive vignettes, and `opqb-t13-553` asks the cotton-wool spot.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hypertensive haemorrhages are flame-shaped because damaged superficial arterioles bleed into the horizontally running nerve fibre layer; white-centred, dot and subhyaloid haemorrhages point elsewhere.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q532), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-533', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'**Retinal ischemia & hypoxia in diabetic retinopathy** commonly affects the:',
    options:['Peripapillary area.','Macular area.','Midperipheral retina.','Ora serrata.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Retinal ischemia in diabetes mainly affects **mid-peripheral retina, causing neovascularization.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box names the region and, in the same breath, says why it matters.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` gives the pathogenesis as two processes — \"**Microvascular occlusion**\" and \"**Microvascular leakage**\" — and the mid-periphery is where the occlusive half does its damage. Capillaries close, large areas of retina are left without perfusion, and the hypoxic tissue releases vasoproliferative factor. New vessels then grow where the deck says they grow: \"**Neovascularization of disc = NVD**\" and \"**Neovascularization elsewhere = NVE**\".\n\n**Why this is the anatomical basis of panretinal photocoagulation.** PRP does not treat the new vessels; it treats the retina that is *asking* for them. `L19.1` prescribes \"Initial treatment is **2000-3000 burns**\", spot size 200–500 µm, over \"the area covered by complete PRP\" — thousands of burns laid across the mid-peripheral retina, deliberately sparing the macula and the disc. Ablating hypoxic peripheral retina lowers the total oxygen demand and removes the source of the angiogenic signal, so the new vessels regress. The deck's assessment slide describes what success looks like: \"Good involution — **regression of neovascularization** · residual 'ghost' vessels or fibrous tissue · disc pallor\". `opqb-t13-544` keys PRP for precisely this picture.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not where the ischaemia sits |\n|---|---|\n| **Peripapillary area.** | The disc is where new vessels *appear* (NVD), not where the ischaemia that drives them lies. Confusing the site of the consequence with the site of the cause is the trap — and it is the reason PRP is applied everywhere except here. |\n| **Macular area.** | The macula is damaged in diabetes, but by the **leakage** half of the pathogenesis, not the occlusive half: oedema and exudate, with the 500 µm and one-disc-diameter criteria of \"clinically significant macular oedema\". There *is* an ischaemic maculopathy — `L19.1` prints \"Macula appears relatively normal · **Poor visual acuity** · Capillary non-perfusion on FA · **Treatment not appropriate**\" — but it is a distinct and less common entity, deliberately excluded from laser treatment, and it is not the ischaemia that drives proliferation. |\n| **Ora serrata.** | The extreme anterior edge of the retina, at the junction with the ciliary body. It is thin, sparsely vascularised and metabolically quiet, so there is little there to become ischaemic and little to proliferate. Diabetic neovascularisation is a **posterior and mid-peripheral** phenomenon. |\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option D as \"**Orra serrata**\". Repaired here; the misspelling is recorded.",
    objective:'Diabetic capillary occlusion strikes the mid-peripheral retina, and the hypoxia there drives neovascularisation — which is why panretinal photocoagulation ablates that region and spares the macula and disc.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q533), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-534', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'The **macular edema** is **best detected clinically** by:',
    options:['OCT.','Direct ophthalmoscope.','Slit lamp biomicroscope.','Fluorescein angiography.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS A, AND IT IS KEYED AS PRINTED — but the word \"clinically\" in the stem points at option C, and the difference is worth understanding rather than glossing.**\n\n**What the source says.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` names the clinical detection of macular oedema exactly once, under the signs of mild NPDR: \"Retinal oedema seen as **thickening on biomicroscopy**.\" *Biomicroscopy* is slit-lamp examination with a fundus lens — option C. The deck never mentions OCT at all; nor does any of the four retinal decks (\"OCT\" is absent from `L19.1`, `L19.2`, `L20` and `L31,32`, and `L20`'s own NOT-COVERED list records \"no OCT\" by name).\n\n**Why OCT is nevertheless the right answer in modern practice, which is presumably what the bank means.** Optical coherence tomography produces a micrometre-resolution cross-section of the retina and *measures* its thickness in microns, mapping it over the macula. It detects oedema that is too subtle to see, quantifies it, distinguishes intraretinal cysts from subretinal fluid, and — because it is a number — tracks the response to treatment between visits. It is the standard of care for diabetic macular oedema. The tension is only that OCT is an **imaging investigation** rather than a clinical examination, and the stem says \"clinically\".\n\n**How the four compare:**\n\n| Option | What it detects | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OCT.** | Retinal thickness in cross-section, quantified in microns; cysts and subretinal fluid seen directly. | The most sensitive by a wide margin — the printed key. |\n| **Direct ophthalmoscope.** | A magnified but **monocular** view. Thickening is a depth change, and one eye cannot see depth. This is the option that is unambiguously worst. | Wrong. |\n| **Slit lamp biomicroscope.** | With a +78/+90 D or contact lens, a **stereoscopic** optically-sectioned view — thickening is seen as a raised, dull, ill-defined area. This is the deck's own answer and the true *clinical* one. | The strongest challenger. |\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | **Leakage**, not thickness. `L19.1` uses it to classify maculopathy — \"Focal leakage on FA\", \"Generalized leakage on FA\", \"Capillary non-perfusion on FA\" — which is a different question. An ischaemic macula can be thin and still leak nothing. | Wrong tool for thickness. |\n\n**The clinical point the two candidate answers share:** macular oedema is a **thickening**, so any test that only shows *colour* misses it. That is why the direct ophthalmoscope loses and why both the slit lamp (stereopsis) and OCT (cross-section) win — each recovers the depth axis in its own way. The bank keys the machine; the deck teaches the lens.\n\n*(The OCT description, the +78/+90 D lens powers and the stereopsis argument are **not taken from the course material**; the biomicroscopy line and the FA captions are printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Macular oedema is a thickening, so it needs a depth-resolving test — OCT (the printed key) measures it in cross-section and slit-lamp biomicroscopy sees it stereoscopically; a monocular ophthalmoscope and an angiogram both miss it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q534), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-535', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Exudative retinal detachment due to eclampsia** is treated by:',
    options:['Retinal photocoagulation.','Retinal cryopexy.','Vitrectomy & retinal tamponade.','Termination of pregnancy.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**An exudative detachment is treated by treating the disease that is producing the fluid, and in eclampsia that means delivering the pregnancy.**\n\n`L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` states the principle without knowing the case:\n\n> \"**3. Exudative Detachment (Treating the Cause).** **SURGERY IS RARELY THE FIRST STEP**; the focus is on stopping fluid leakage: Medical Therapy — corticosteroids or immunosuppressants for inflammatory conditions like uveitis or VKH syndrome. Injections — anti-VEGF drugs to stop leaking from abnormal blood vessels. **Specific Treatments — targeted therapy for infections, hypertension control**, or radiation/surgery for ocular tumors.\"\n\nThe deck also names \"systemic conditions like **severe hypertension**\" among the causes of exudative detachment, and \"**hypertension control**\" among its specific treatments. **Eclampsia is severe hypertension whose only definitive treatment is delivery.** Everything else — magnesium, antihypertensives — is temporising until the placenta is out.\n\n**Why the mechanism forces this answer.** In eclampsia the choroidal arterioles constrict intensely, the choroid becomes ischaemic, and the overlying retinal pigment epithelium — whose pump normally keeps the subretinal space dry — fails. Fluid crosses from the choroid into the subretinal space and the retina lifts as a **smooth bullous dome** with no tear anywhere in it (`L20`'s differentiation table: exudative = \"**Fluid leakage** · Smooth/Bullous · Inflammation, Tumors\"). There is nothing to seal, nothing to buckle and nothing to peel. Deliver the baby, the blood pressure falls, choroidal perfusion recovers, the RPE resumes pumping, and the detachment **resolves spontaneously**, usually within days to weeks and usually with full recovery of vision. *(The choroidal-ischaemia mechanism and the spontaneous resolution are **not taken from the course material**; `L20` gives the principle and names hypertension, not the obstetric detail.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and note that all three are the treatments for the OTHER two types of detachment:**\n\n| Option | Which detachment it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Retinal photocoagulation.** | Sealing a **break** — a rhegmatogenous problem. `L20` gives \"laser or cryotherapy\" after pneumatic retinopexy, and `opqb-t13-571` keys laser for a tear without detachment. There is no break here to surround. |\n| **Retinal cryopexy.** | Same purpose by freezing, and the same objection. Worse than useless in an exudative detachment: it inflames the choroid, and inflammation is what is producing the fluid. |\n| **Vitrectomy & retinal tamponade.** | The rhegmatogenous operation — `L20`: \"the surgeon removes the vitreous gel and replaces it with a **gas or oil bubble** to push the retina back\". Nothing is pulling and nothing is torn; opening an eclamptic patient's eye achieves nothing and risks a great deal. |\n\n**Recognise the pattern:** when a question offers three surgical options and one systemic one for an *exudative* detachment, the systemic one wins. The detachment is a symptom.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Exudative detachment is treated by treating its cause; in eclampsia that is delivery, after which the choroidal leak stops and the retina reattaches by itself.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q535), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-536', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A **64-year-old male** came complaining of **sudden photopsia & floaters** in front of his right eye. He is **diabetic since 15 years** & has **moderate myopia**. **BCVA is 6/6** in this eye & **the fundus could be seen clearly**. All the following is true regarding his condition **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['It is possible to be a case of posterior vitreous detachment.','Fundus examination could be performed using the Goldmann 3 mirror lens to visualize the retinal periphery.','Ultrasonography must be done in such a case.','Laser photocoagulation could be done in management of such a case.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The stem contains its own discriminator and it is easy to read past: \"the fundus could be seen clearly\".**\n\n**Ultrasound is the test for an eye you cannot look into.** Its whole indication is opaque media — a dense cataract, a vitreous haemorrhage, a corneal scar — where the retina has to be assessed through sound because light will not get there. This bank keys exactly that use twice: at `opqb-t13-550`, where a diabetic loses vision overnight from a vitreous haemorrhage, and in the Lens topic at `opqb-t9-371`, where the question is how to check for detachment behind a mature cataract. Here the media are clear, the acuity is **6/6**, and the examiner can see the fundus directly at higher resolution than any B-scan offers. Ultrasound is not wrong; it is simply not **must**, and that is the word the stem uses.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Why it holds |\n|---|---|\n| **Possible posterior vitreous detachment** | The single most likely cause of new flashes and floaters at 64. The vitreous liquefies with age and separates from the retina; where it is adherent it tugs, giving photopsia, and the separation itself throws a floater. `L20` names the process only as \"Aging (**vitreous shrinkage**)\" among the causes of RRD, and its NOT-COVERED list records that PVD as a named entity and the Weiss ring are absent from the deck — so **the term itself is not taken from the course material**. `opqb-t1-18` and `opqb-t1-19` in this corpus ask the same process directly. |\n| **Goldmann 3 mirror lens for the periphery** | A contact lens with three angled mirrors used at the slit lamp; each mirror reflects a different zone, so it reaches from the posterior pole to the ora serrata under stereoscopic view. That is precisely what a flashes-and-floaters patient needs, because the break — if there is one — will be peripheral, not at the pole. |\n| **Laser photocoagulation could be done** | Two separate reasons in one patient. If the search finds a **retinal break**, prophylactic laser retinopexy walls it off before it detaches — the treatment this bank keys at `opqb-t13-571`. And he is a fifteen-year diabetic, so if the examination reveals proliferative disease, `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prescribes panretinal photocoagulation, \"2000-3000 burns\". Note the printed hedge: \"**could** be done\". |\n\n**⚠️ The real teaching point of the vignette is that this man needs a DILATED PERIPHERAL EXAMINATION TODAY, not an investigation.** New photopsia and floaters in a myope of 64 is a retinal break until the periphery has been looked at; 6/6 acuity is reassuring about the macula and says nothing at all about the far periphery. Ordering a scan instead of picking up the indirect ophthalmoscope is the mistake the question is built around.\n\n**Note also what the stem does NOT say:** there is no curtain, no field defect and no drop in acuity, so the retina is still attached. Compare `opqb-t13-558`, where a lower black veil in a high myope makes the diagnosis, and `opqb-t13-569`, where the answer to the same symptoms is to look at the fundus.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ultrasound is for eyes whose media are opaque; with 6/6 vision and a clearly visible fundus the answer is a dilated peripheral examination, not a scan.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q536), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-537', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'Concerning **dry type AMD**:',
    options:['Associated with a disease of the retinal pigment epithelium.','Caused by a hole formation at the macula.','Associated with growth of blood vessels beneath the macula.','Is commonly treated with retinal surgery.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` splits age-related macular degeneration in one line each, and the word \"RPE\" appears in the dry half:**\n\n> \"**Types.** **Dry type 90 %** — slow progressive **atrophy of RPE and photoreceptors**. **Wet type 10 %** — RPE detachment and **choroidal neovascularization**.\"\n\nThat single slide keys three of the four options.\n\n**Dry AMD is a disease of the pigment epithelium, and drusen are the visible evidence.** The same deck defines them: \"Drusen are hyaline nodules (or colloid bodies) deposited in **Bruch's membrane**, which separates the inner choroidal vessels from the retinal pigment epithelium.\" The RPE sits on Bruch's membrane and depends on it for the exchange of nutrients and waste with the choroid; as debris accumulates in that membrane the RPE cells above it fail, and the photoreceptors that depend on the RPE fail with them. The end stage is **geographic atrophy** — sharply outlined patches where RPE, photoreceptors and choriocapillaris have all disappeared together. The deck adds the prognostic note: \"Patients with drusen alone tend to have normal or near normal visual acuity, with **minimal metamorphopsia**\", which is why dry AMD blurs slowly over years rather than suddenly.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Which disease it actually describes |\n|---|---|\n| **Caused by a hole formation at the macula.** | A macular hole — a full-thickness dehiscence at the fovea from vitreomacular traction, which is a mechanical disease with its own surgery (vitrectomy with membrane peel and gas). Unrelated to AMD. ⚠️ \"Macular hole\" returns **0 hits across all thirty cached lecture decks**, so it is not taught; this bank uses it as a distractor three times (here, `opqb-t13-539` and `opqb-t13-573`). |\n| **Associated with growth of blood vessels beneath the macula.** | **Wet** AMD, by definition — \"RPE detachment and **choroidal neovascularization**\". Choroidal vessels break through Bruch's membrane and grow under the retina, where they leak and bleed. That is the 10 %, not the 90 %, and it is the half that loses vision suddenly and severely. |\n| **Is commonly treated with retinal surgery.** | Neither type is treated surgically. `L35,36`: \"For **dry AMD** dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins especially **Vit. E**. For **wet AMD** injections of **Anti VEGF**.\" Supplements for one, intravitreal injections for the other; no operation for either. |\n\n**Two numbers from the same deck worth keeping**, both local to this course: AMD is \"the leading cause of **irreversible central visual loss** (20/200 or worse) among people aged 52 or older\", and \"the overall prevalence of AMD in **Alexandria** was 6.6 %\" (early 5.3 %, late 2.4 %, rising sharply after 75). `opqb-t13-559` asks the same disease as the senile change that permanently costs vision, and `opqb-t13-515` uses drusen as the sign that a fundus is AMD rather than diabetic.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Dry AMD is 90 % of cases and is progressive atrophy of the RPE and photoreceptors with drusen in Bruch membrane; choroidal neovascularisation defines the wet type, and neither is treated by surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q537), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-538', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'All is true concerning **macular edema EXCEPT**:',
    options:['May be seen following intraocular surgery.','Causes blurring of vision.','Is usually associated with growth of abnormal vessels in the retina.','It may take a cystoid appearance'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Macular oedema is a LEAKAGE phenomenon. Neovascularisation is an ISCHAEMIA phenomenon. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` keeps them in separate columns from its first pathogenesis slide onward:**\n\n| Pathogenetic route | What it produces |\n|---|---|\n| **Microvascular leakage** | Retinal oedema, hard exudates, **maculopathy** — focal, diffuse, cystoid |\n| **Microvascular occlusion** | Hypoxia → **neovascularization** (NVD, NVE), and proliferative disease |\n\nThe two can coexist in the same diabetic eye, which is why the word **usually** in option C is what makes it false: oedema is far commoner than proliferation, occurs in many diseases that never neovascularise at all, and does not depend on new vessels for its existence.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Support |\n|---|---|\n| **May be seen following intraocular surgery.** | Pseudophakic cystoid macular oedema — the Irvine–Gass syndrome — is a well-recognised cause of blurring some weeks after an otherwise uneventful cataract operation. `L18) Cataract II` lists it among the postoperative complications of cataract surgery. |\n| **Causes blurring of vision.** | The macula is where fine vision is made. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` gives its functions as \"Sharp clear vision · Color perception · Form vision · Point to point discrimination\", and its symptoms as \"**Diminution of vision** · Color vision defects · Micropsia · Macropsia · **Metamorphopsia** · Scotoma\". Fluid separating photoreceptors distorts the image as well as dimming it — straight lines bend. |\n| **It may take a cystoid appearance** | Printed twice in `L19.1`: \"Leakage — focal, diffuse, **cystoid**\", and under diffuse maculopathy, \"Frequent **cystoid macular oedema**\". Fluid collects in the outer plexiform layer, where Henle's fibres run radially from the fovea, so it pools into petal-shaped cysts around the centre. |\n\n**What macular oedema is, in numbers, from the deck that defines it.** `L19.1`'s \"clinically significant macular oedema\" slide gives three criteria, any one of which suffices: retinal oedema within **500 µm** of the centre of the fovea; hard exudates within 500 µm of the centre with adjacent oedema (which may extend beyond the limit); or retinal oedema **one disc area or larger, any part of it within one disc diameter (1500 µm)** of the foveal centre. Treatment follows the geometry — \"**Grid** treatment for diffuse retinal thickening located more than 500 µm from centre of fovea\", \"**Focal** treatment for microaneurysms in centre of hard exudate rings located 500–3000 µm from centre of fovea\", with \"intra vitreal injection of anti VEGF\" in both.\n\n**Transcription note:** option D is printed \"It may take a **cystoids** appearance\". Repaired here.\n\n**Elsewhere in this topic:** `opqb-t13-534` asks how to detect it and `opqb-t13-562` asks which vascular disease does *not* cause it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Macular oedema comes from microvascular leakage — post-surgical, blurring, often cystoid — while neovascularisation comes from microvascular occlusion; the two are separate pathways.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q538), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-539', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **macular star** is seen in:',
    options:['Wet age-related macular degeneration.','Malignant hypertension.','Macular hole.','Retinitis pigmentosa.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the phrase exactly once in the whole module, and it prints it under hypertensive retinopathy.** The deck's extravascular signs of hypertension are three: \"Flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages · **Cotton-wool spots and macular star** · Disc oedema\". Their appearance together — haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, a macular star and a swollen disc — is the malignant (accelerated) phase, in which the arteriolar autoregulation has broken down completely.\n\n**Why the exudates make a star and not a blob.** Severely raised pressure breaks the inner blood–retinal barrier, and lipid-rich fluid leaks out of the damaged perifoveal capillaries. It collects in the **outer plexiform layer**, and at the macula that layer is not arranged like the rest of the retina: the photoreceptor axons — Henle's fibres — run *radially outward* from the fovea, almost parallel to the retinal surface. The exudate is channelled along them and settles between them, so as the water is reabsorbed the residual hard lipid is left in radial spokes pointing at the fovea. The result is a star or a partial fan. It appears **after** the oedema, and it clears over months, long after the blood pressure has been controlled. *(The Henle's-fibre explanation is **not taken from the course material**; the deck prints the sign and its disease, not the histology.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it looks like instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Wet age-related macular degeneration.** | `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`: \"Wet type 10 % — RPE detachment and **choroidal neovascularization**\". The macula shows a grey-green subretinal membrane, subretinal fluid, haemorrhage and eventually a disciform scar. Hard exudate can occur around a leaking membrane, but it is irregular and circinate, not a radial star, and the presentation is sudden **metamorphopsia** rather than the systemic illness of malignant hypertension. |\n| **Macular hole.** | A full-thickness dehiscence at the fovea — a small round red defect with a cuff of subretinal fluid and a central scotoma, from vitreomacular traction. Nothing is deposited. ⚠️ Not taught: \"macular hole\" returns **0 hits across all thirty cached lecture decks**. |\n| **Retinitis pigmentosa.** | A pigmentary **degeneration**, not an exudative disease. Its fundus signs are bone-spicule pigment in the mid-periphery, attenuated arterioles and a waxy pale disc. There is no leakage, so there is no exudate to arrange. |\n\n**The clinical instruction hidden in the answer:** a macular star means take the blood pressure. `opqb-t13-547` builds the same instruction into a vignette — a young man in renal failure with bilateral swollen discs and haemorrhages, where the first line of management is a sphygmomanometer, not a scan.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A macular star is radial hard exudate laid down along Henle fibres and belongs to malignant hypertension, alongside flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and disc oedema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.84 (Q539), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-540', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Sectorial or altitudinal visual field defect** can be seen in all of the following conditions **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Branch retinal vein occlusion.','Retinal detachment.','Branch retinal artery occlusion.','Commotion retinae.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A sectorial or altitudinal defect means a sharply bounded wedge or half of the field is gone while the rest is normal — so the lesion must have a sharp anatomical edge.** Three of the four options do; the fourth does not.\n\n| Option | The edge that makes the defect |\n|---|---|\n| **Branch retinal vein occlusion.** | A retinal vein drains one **quadrant**, and the territory it drains is watertight against its neighbours. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the acute signs — \"Venous tortuosity and dilatation · Flame-shaped and 'dot-blot' haemorrhages · Cotton-wool spots and retinal oedema\" — and every one of them stops at the horizontal raphe. The field defect follows the same wedge, inverted. |\n| **Retinal detachment.** | The subretinal fluid has a **front**, and everything behind it has stopped working. `L20`'s symptom table calls it a \"'**curtain**' or shadow in the peripheral vision\" and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` reproduces the patient's own words — \"like a **curtain coming down**\". A superior detachment gives an inferior (altitudinal) defect, because the retinal image is inverted. |\n| **Branch retinal artery occlusion.** | The sharpest edge of all: one arteriolar territory infarcts and turns white, and the rest of the retina is untouched. `L19.2`: \"VA - variable · APD mild or absent · **Retina whitening** · Arteriolar narrowing\". `opqb-t13-576` works the geometry out — a lower **nasal** branch occlusion gives an upper **temporal** field defect. |\n\n**Why commotion retinae is the exception.** Commotion retinae (Berlin's oedema) is the concussive retinal whitening that follows blunt trauma to the eye. It is not a vascular territory and not a fluid front: it is a **contusion**, the shock wave from the impact disrupting photoreceptor outer segments over an area centred on the point struck — most often the **posterior pole and macula**, and often patchy or diffuse rather than bounded. So the field loss it produces is a **central or generalised blur**, or a paracentral scotoma if the macula is involved, without the crisp respect for a horizontal or vertical meridian that defines an altitudinal or sectorial defect. It is also usually **transient** — the retina looks white for days and then recovers, with vision returning unless the macula was badly hit.\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE NOTE — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. \"Commotion\" returns 0 hits across all thirty cached lecture decks and all twelve cached book chapters** (case-insensitive, in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`). Neither `L25) Trauma I` nor `L26) Trauma II` names it. **The description above is therefore not taken from the course material.** What *is* sourced is the field behaviour of the other three, from `L19.2` and `L20` as quoted.\n\n**How to use the rule generally:** ask what shape the disease has. Vascular occlusions are wedges; detachments are fronts; optic nerve disease is altitudinal at the horizontal meridian (`L31,32`'s AION case gives \"**inferior** visual field loss in the right eye\"); chiasmal and retro-chiasmal disease respects the *vertical* meridian. A contusion has no shape at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Sectorial and altitudinal defects need a sharp anatomical boundary — a vascular territory or a detachment front; commotion retinae is a diffuse contusion of the posterior pole and produces no such edge.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q540), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-541', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'The most appropriate method to examine **retinal circulation** is through:',
    options:['Slit lamp biomicroscope.','Direct ophthalmoscopy.','Fluorescein angiography.','Retinoscopy.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Read the word \"circulation\", not \"retina\".** The other options let you look at retinal *vessels*; only fluorescein angiography lets you watch **blood moving through them in time**.\n\nThe dye is injected into an arm vein and photographed as it arrives, so the study is a sequence rather than a picture: choroidal flush, then arterial filling, then the capillary phase, then venous laminar flow, then the late frames. That time axis is what answers questions no still image can:\n\n| What you need to know | What the angiogram shows | `L19.2`'s own caption |\n|---|---|---|\n| Is a vessel filling at all? | Arm-to-retina and arteriovenous transit times | CRAO: \"**Early filling of cilioretinal artery** · Non-filling of other vessels\" |\n| Is it filling *late*? | Delayed arterial phase | BRAO: \"**Extreme delay of arterial phase**\" |\n| Is the capillary bed alive? | Perfusion versus dropout | Ischaemic CRVO: \"**Extensive capillary non-perfusion**\"; non-ischaemic: \"Good retinal capillary perfusion\" |\n| Is it leaking, and where? | Hyperfluorescence increasing over the late frames | BRVO: \"Late - **hyperfluorescence** due to diffuse oedema\"; macroaneurysm: \"Immediate uniform filling · **Late leakage**\" |\n| Is blood hiding the view? | Blocked background fluorescence | BRVO: \"Early - **blocked background fluorescence** due to haemorrhage\" |\n\nThose five distinctions decide treatment. `L19.2` makes chronic macular oedema after BRVO turn on one of them — \"Wait 6-12 weeks and perform FA. **Macular non-perfusion — no treatment.** Good macular perfusion and VA 6/18 or worse after 3 months — consider **laser photocoagulation**\" — and `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` separates the three maculopathies the same way, by \"Focal leakage on FA\", \"Generalized leakage on FA\" and \"Capillary non-perfusion on FA\".\n\n**Why the other three lose:**\n\n| Option | What it can and cannot do |\n|---|---|\n| **Slit lamp biomicroscope.** | With a fundus lens it gives an excellent stereoscopic view of vessel **calibre, sheathing, crossings and collaterals** — `L19.2` shows exactly those in its \"Signs of old branch retinal vein occlusion: **Vascular sheathing and collaterals**\". But it is a still, structural view; it cannot tell a patent vessel from an occluded one that still holds a column of stationary blood. |\n| **Direct ophthalmoscopy.** | High magnification of the disc and posterior pole, monocular, tiny field. It shows the vessels; it shows nothing about flow. |\n| **Retinoscopy.** | Not an examination of the fundus at all — it measures refraction from the moving reflex. Wrong answer here for the same reason as at `opqb-t13-525`, `opqb-t13-548` and `opqb-t13-556`. |\n\n**A limitation worth knowing:** fluorescein images the **retinal** circulation well and the choroidal circulation poorly, because the dye that leaks freely from the fenestrated choriocapillaris and the pigment of the RPE together mask what lies behind. Indocyanine green is the dye used for the choroid. *(That limitation and the ICG alternative are **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fluorescein angiography shows blood flowing over time — filling, delay, non-perfusion and leakage — which no still view of the vessels can give.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q541), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-542', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'In **retinitis pigmentosa** all of the following are true **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['The earliest symptom is night blindness.','ERG can detect early cases.','In late cases we may find posterior cortical cataract.','In the sex-linked inheritance mode, visual loss occurs at the age of 50 - 60 years.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The X-linked form is the WORST and the EARLIEST of the three inheritance patterns, not the latest.** That is the whole answer, and it inverts the option.\n\nRetinitis pigmentosa is inherited in three ways, and they differ sharply in severity:\n\n| Inheritance | Course |\n|---|---|\n| **Autosomal dominant** | Mildest and latest. Onset often in adult life, slow progression, useful central vision frequently retained into old age. |\n| **Autosomal recessive** | Intermediate. |\n| **X-linked (sex-linked)** | **Most severe and earliest.** Affected males are symptomatic in childhood, with legal blindness commonly reached by the third or fourth decade. Female carriers show a patchy tapetal reflex and may have mild changes. |\n\nA man whose visual loss begins at 50 to 60 is describing the *dominant* pattern, so the option attaches the wrong tempo to the wrong gene. `opqb-t13-527`'s printed box supports the general point from the other direction, giving night blindness as \"**the main and the earliest** complaint\", and this bank's own vignette at `opqb-t13-560` puts a father-in-law with established disease into a pre-marital consultation — a scenario that only makes sense in a disease that declares itself young.\n\n**Why the other three are true:**\n\n| Option | Why it holds |\n|---|---|\n| **The earliest symptom is night blindness.** | Rods carry scotopic vision and rods die first. `opqb-t13-527`'s box prints it as symptom 1, with \"decreased peripheral vision 'tunnel vision' (**due to rod atrophy**)\" as symptom 2 — the same cells, further along. |\n| **ERG can detect early cases.** | The electroretinogram is a mass response of the photoreceptor layer, so it falls as soon as a large number of rods are dysfunctional — **before** the patient notices anything and before the fundus shows bone spicules. That is why it is the test for a presymptomatic relative, and why `opqb-t13-560` and `opqb-t13-561` key ERG for the fiancée. |\n| **In late cases we may find posterior cortical cataract.** | The complicated cataract of chronic retinal disease. ⚠️ **The bank contradicts itself here**: this option says posterior **cortical**, while `opqb-t13-527`'s own printed box says \"**posterior subcapsular** cataracts. (Common in longstanding cases)\". Posterior subcapsular is the correct term. Both are keyed exactly as printed and neither key moves; the discrepancy is recorded, not disputed. |\n\n**Transcription note:** option D is printed \"In the sex-linked inheritance **mood**\" — a misprint for *mode*. Repaired here.\n\n**⚠️ Source note:** retinitis pigmentosa is taught in none of the thirty cached decks — measured, exactly two hits, both opened, neither teaching (a family-history bar chart in `L1,2` slide 96 and a congenital-cataract cause list in `L35,36`:167). ERG is named once in the whole module, `L18) Cataract II`:262, \"Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected\", in the pre-operative cataract work-up. **The inheritance table and the ERG rationale above are not taken from the course material.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'X-linked retinitis pigmentosa is the earliest and most severe form, blinding by early adult life; night blindness comes first, ERG detects presymptomatic cases, and a complicated posterior subcapsular cataract appears late.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q542), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-543', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'The sign of **long-standing retinal detachment** is:',
    options:['The retina is slightly opaque & corrugated in appearance.','The presence of retinal tears.','The presence of subretinal demarcation lines.','The presence of "tobacco dust" in the anterior chamber.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The question is asking which sign takes TIME to develop, and only one of the four does.**\n\nA **subretinal demarcation line** (a high-water mark) is a pigmented ridge at the posterior edge of a chronic detachment. It forms where the retinal pigment epithelium at the boundary of the detached area, chronically irritated and no longer covered by attached retina, proliferates and lays down pigment along the line the fluid has reached. Because it takes **weeks to months** to appear, its presence dates the detachment — and it is one of the few things in a fresh clinical examination that tells you how long the retina has been off. Practically it also marks how far the fluid extended: if the detachment later progresses, a second line forms further back, so several parallel lines mean several episodes.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is a sign of detachment, but not of a long-standing one:**\n\n| Option | When it appears |\n|---|---|\n| **The retina is slightly opaque & corrugated in appearance.** | **Immediately**, and it is the discriminating feature of a *fresh rhegmatogenous* detachment. `L20)`'s differentiation table gives the retinal surface as \"Rhegmatogenous — **Corrugated/Wrinkled**\" against \"Tractional — Smooth/Taut\" and \"Exudative — Smooth/Bullous\". Note the reverse: a **long-standing** detachment loses its corrugation and becomes thin, smooth and immobile, often with intraretinal cysts — so this option is not merely undated, it is the opposite of the answer. |\n| **The presence of retinal tears.** | Present **from the outset**, by definition. `L20`: \"Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (RRD) … occurs when a physical **tear or break** develops in the retina.\" The break precedes the detachment; it dates nothing. |\n| **The presence of \"tobacco dust\" in the anterior chamber.** | Two faults. First, **the location is wrong**: tobacco dust (Shafer's sign) is pigment granules liberated from the RPE through a fresh break and floating in the **anterior vitreous**, not in the anterior chamber. Second, it is a sign of a **RECENT** break — it is one of the most useful acute findings in a patient with new flashes and floaters, and it disperses with time. ⚠️ `L20`'s own NOT-COVERED list records \"**TOBACCO-DUST / SHAFER'S SIGN — not named**\", so the sign is absent from the course material; the correction of its location is **not taken from the course material**. |\n\n**Other features of chronicity worth carrying alongside the demarcation line**, none of them printed in the decks: intraretinal cysts in the detached retina, a thinned and atrophic retinal surface, secondary proliferative vitreoretinopathy with fixed folds, and — because the retina has been off for months — a poor visual prognosis even after successful reattachment. `L20`'s NOT-COVERED list also confirms that \"MACULA-ON versus MACULA-OFF, and the URGENCY/TIMING of surgery\" are absent from the deck, so no time window is available from the source.\n\n**Transcription note:** option A is printed \"The **retinal** is slightly opaque\". Repaired here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A subretinal demarcation line is pigment laid down at the fluid boundary over weeks to months, so it dates a detachment; corrugation, a break and tobacco dust are all signs of a fresh one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q543), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-544', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'A **60 years old male** suffering from **diabetes mellitus for the last 20 years** presented with **bilateral retinal hemorrhages and exudates, with neovascularization on the optic disc**. The **retina is still attached**. The most suitable management is:',
    options:['Panretinal photocoagulation.','Medical control of diabetes.','Vitrectomy','Follow-up.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**NPDR** with neovascularization is treated with **PRP** to prevent progression.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE BOX MISLABELS THE DISEASE, AND THE STEM IS THE PROOF.** Neovascularisation on the optic disc is *proliferative* diabetic retinopathy by definition — `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` captions the sign \"**Neovascularization of disc = NVD**\" on its slide headed \"**Proliferative** diabetic retinopathy\". There is no such thing as \"NPDR with neovascularization\"; the letter N in NPDR means non-proliferative. **The KEY (A, panretinal photocoagulation) is correct and is not in doubt** — the error is in the box's label only, and it is recorded here rather than corrected in the transcription.\n\n**Why PRP, and why now.** `L19.1` gives the indications for treating proliferative disease as three pictures — \"**NVD > 1/3 disc in area** · Less extensive NVD **+ haemorrhage** · NVE > 1/2 disc in area + haemorrhage\" — and this patient has NVD *with* haemorrhages in both eyes, which meets the second at least. The treatment is prescribed in detail: \"**Laser panretinal photocoagulation** — initial treatment is **2000-3000 burns**, spot size 200-500 µm depending on contact lens magnification, gentle intensity burn (0.10-0.05 sec)\", with \"follow-up 4 to 8 weeks\" and re-treatment if involution is poor (\"persistent neovascularization · haemorrhage\").\n\n**The logic behind burning healthy-looking retina:** the new vessels are a response to hypoxia in the **mid-peripheral** retina (`opqb-t13-533`'s box: \"Retinal ischemia in diabetes mainly affects mid-peripheral retina, causing neovascularization\"). Ablating that ischaemic tissue removes the demand and the angiogenic signal, and the vessels regress — \"good involution: **regression of neovascularization**, residual 'ghost' vessels or fibrous tissue, disc pallor\". PRP sacrifices peripheral field and some night vision to save the macula.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails here |\n|---|---|\n| **Medical control of diabetes.** | Essential lifelong and it slows progression — `L19.1`'s first two adverse risk factors are \"long duration of diabetes\" and \"poor metabolic control\" — but it does not make an existing frond of new vessels disappear, and those vessels will bleed into the vitreous or contract into a tractional detachment while the HbA1c is being brought down. Necessary, not sufficient. |\n| **Vitrectomy** | The right operation at the wrong time. `L19.1` lists its indications as \"**severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage** · dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage · **progressive proliferation despite laser therapy** · **retinal detachment involving macula**\". The stem excludes the last of those in so many words — \"**the retina is still attached**\" — and there is no vitreous haemorrhage and no failed laser. |\n| **Follow-up.** | Correct for *severe NPDR*, where `L19.1` says \"**Treatment - not required but watch for proliferative disease**\". That watching ends the moment new vessels appear, and they have. Observing proliferative disease with NVD is how eyes are lost. |\n\n**The one distinction that decides this question:** watch NPDR, **treat PDR**. The dividing line is neovascularisation, and this patient is over it. Only 5–10 % of diabetics reach it, \"IDD at increased risk (**60% after 30 years**)\".",
    objective:'Neovascularisation on the disc is proliferative disease and is treated with panretinal photocoagulation; glycaemic control alone will not regress it and vitrectomy is reserved for haemorrhage, failed laser or a detached macula.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q544), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-545', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A **young male known to be a high myope** complains of **flashes of light in the temporal field of his right eye** of **two days’ duration**. The most expected cause:',
    options:['Corneal edema.','Rise of intraocular pressure.','Crystalline lens opacities.','Retinal break'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three facts in the stem converge on one answer: photopsia, high myopia and two days.**\n\n**Flashes are a retinal symptom and nothing else.** The retina has no pain fibres and no other output — this bank prints the reasoning itself in the option it keys correct at `opqb-t13-565`: \"the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light\". So *any* stimulus applied to the retina, including mechanical traction, is perceived as light. `opqb-t13-565`'s printed box gives the mechanism in three words: \"Flashes = **traction on photoreceptors**\".\n\n**High myopia is the specific risk factor.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives the causes of rhegmatogenous detachment as \"Aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia (nearsightedness)**, or eye trauma\", and its differentiation table lists \"**Myopia**, Aging\" as the common risk for the rhegmatogenous type. A long eye has a thin, stretched peripheral retina and a vitreous that liquefies and separates decades earlier than normal, which is why this patient is young and still at risk.\n\n**Two days matters because it puts him in the window in which something can be done.** He has flashes but no floaters, no curtain and no drop in acuity — traction, and possibly a break, but no detachment yet. `L20`'s symptom table gives the full rhegmatogenous set as \"**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a 'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\", progressing \"often sudden and rapid\". He is at the beginning of that list. A break found now can be sealed with laser in an outpatient clinic — `opqb-t13-571` keys exactly that — where a detachment tomorrow needs an operating theatre.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it cannot flash |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal edema.** | An anterior, media problem. It scatters light, so the patient reports **haloes around lights, glare and misty vision** — a steady disturbance of the whole image, not brief flashes in one part of the field. |\n| **Rise of intraocular pressure.** | Angle closure gives haloes (from corneal oedema), **pain**, redness, a hazy cornea and nausea; chronic open-angle glaucoma gives no symptom at all until the field is far gone. Neither produces photopsia, and neither is silent and localised the way this is. |\n| **Crystalline lens opacities.** | A cataract sits in the light path and **subtracts** — dimming, glare, monocular diplopia, a myopic shift. An opacity cannot generate a percept; it can only block one. It also has no reason to project to one quadrant. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the localisation, because it is the opposite of the intuitive answer.** Flashes in the **temporal** field arise from traction on the **nasal** retina — the retinal image is inverted left-to-right as well as up-and-down. The examiner must therefore look hardest at the retina *opposite* to where the patient points. `opqb-t13-576` builds a whole question on the same inversion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Photopsia is vitreoretinal traction reported by a retina that can only speak in light; in a high myope it means a retinal break until the periphery has been examined.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q545), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-546', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'The **nutrition to the fovea** is through:',
    options:['Long posterior ciliary arteries.','Artery circle of Zinn.','Choriocapillaris.','Anterior ciliary arteries.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The fovea is the one part of the retina with no blood vessels in it at all, and it is fed entirely from behind.**\n\nThe retina has a dual supply. The **central retinal artery** and its branches supply the inner two-thirds through the retinal capillary network; the **choriocapillaris** — the innermost, fenestrated capillary layer of the choroid — supplies the outer third, the photoreceptors and the retinal pigment epithelium, by diffusion across Bruch's membrane. At the fovea, the inner layers are simply not there. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 5 shows it directly: panel D is \"a low-power section through the **FOVEA** — the retina thins to a pit with the **inner layers absent** and the **photoreceptor layer thickened**\". No inner layers means no retinal capillaries; the foveal avascular zone is transparent precisely so that nothing lies between the incoming light and the cones. What remains — a dense monolayer of cones — is outer retina, and outer retina is choroidal territory.\n\n**Two consequences that this topic keeps returning to:**\n\n| Consequence | Where it shows |\n|---|---|\n| **The cherry-red spot** | When the central retinal artery occludes, the inner retina infarcts and whitens, but the fovea has no inner retina to swell and its choroidal supply is untouched — so the intact orange choroid shows through the thin window. `L19.2` prints \"'**Cherry-red spot' at macula**\" as a CRAO sign; `opqb-t13-530` explains it in full. |\n| **Wet AMD is a choroidal disease** | `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`: wet AMD is \"RPE detachment and **choroidal neovascularization**\", and drusen are deposited \"in **Bruch's membrane**, which separates the inner **choroidal vessels** from the retinal pigment epithelium\". The macula is the part of the retina most dependent on the choroid, which is why choroidal disease destroys central vision first. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually supplies |\n|---|---|\n| **Long posterior ciliary arteries.** | Two vessels that run forward in the horizontal meridian between sclera and choroid **without branching** to reach the ciliary body, where they join the anterior ciliary arteries to form the major arterial circle of the iris. They supply the **anterior** uvea — iris and ciliary body — not the posterior pole. (The *short* posterior ciliary arteries are the ones that supply the choroid, and hence the choriocapillaris.) |\n| **Artery circle of Zinn.** | The circle of Zinn–Haller is an anastomotic ring of short posterior ciliary branches **within the sclera around the optic nerve head**, supplying the prelaminar and laminar optic disc. It is the vessel whose failure causes anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy — `L31,32`'s Case 3 — not a macular supply. |\n| **Anterior ciliary arteries.** | Run in the **rectus muscle tendons** and reach the eye at their insertions, supplying the anterior sclera, episclera, limbus and anterior uvea. They are at the opposite end of the globe from the fovea. |\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option C as \"Choriocapillaries\". Repaired here.\n\n*(The dual-supply anatomy, the ciliary artery distributions and the circle of Zinn–Haller are **not taken from the course material** — `L20` gives the foveal histology and `L35,36` gives Bruch's membrane, but no deck sets out the ocular arterial supply.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The fovea has no retinal capillaries — its inner layers are absent — so it is nourished by diffusion from the choriocapillaris, which is why it survives as a cherry-red spot in CRAO.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q546), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-547', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **30-year-old patient with renal failure** presents for routine fundus check. Examination reveals a **raised optic nerve head with retinal hemorrhages in BOTH eyes**. The **first line of management** is:',
    options:['Ultrasonography','Measurement of the blood pressure','CT scan','Fluorescein angiography.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Bilateral disc swelling with haemorrhages in a patient with renal failure is malignant hypertension until the cuff says otherwise — and the cuff takes thirty seconds and costs nothing.**\n\n**Why bilateral is the word that decides it.** A single swollen disc is a local problem — papillitis, AION, a vein occlusion. **Both** discs swollen means something systemic is acting on both optic nerves at once, and there are only two common answers: raised intracranial pressure and accelerated hypertension. This patient has a kidney disease, which is one of the commonest causes of severe secondary hypertension in a thirty-year-old, and the fundus he has is the one `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints under hypertensive retinopathy: the extravascular signs are \"**Flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages** · Cotton-wool spots and macular star · **Disc oedema**\".\n\n**Why measuring first is not merely cheap but clinically right.** Malignant hypertension is an emergency for the brain, the heart and the kidneys long before it is an emergency for the eye — and the *treatment* is to lower the blood pressure, which is available immediately. Every other option delays that by hours while adding nothing: none of them can diagnose hypertension, and none changes what is done next. There is also a two-way relationship here worth noticing: hypertension damages the kidney and renal disease raises the blood pressure, so the fundus is reporting on the systemic disease as much as on the eye. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists both \"**Hypertension**\" and \"**Renal disease**\" among its adverse risk factors for exactly that reason.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not first |\n|---|---|\n| **Ultrasonography** | An ocular B-scan is for **opaque media** or an intraocular mass. The discs and haemorrhages are already visible, so nothing is hidden. Optic nerve sheath diameter can be measured on ultrasound, but that is a surrogate for raised intracranial pressure and it comes after, not before, a blood pressure. |\n| **CT scan** | Reasonable **only if** the blood pressure is normal — then bilateral disc swelling means raised intracranial pressure and imaging is indicated, as at `opqb-t13-523` and `opqb-t13-575`. Ordering it first is doing the expensive investigation for the second diagnosis before excluding the first. |\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | Characterises leakage in retinal vascular disease; it would confirm disc leakage and tell you nothing you did not already know from looking. It also takes a dye injection and a clinic slot. |\n\n**⚠️ The pattern this question teaches, and it recurs:** an ocular sign can be the presenting sign of a systemic emergency, and the correct \"investigation\" is often a bedside measurement rather than a machine. `opqb-t13-539` asks the same disease from its most specific sign (the macular star), and `opqb-t13-573` puts a hypertensive patient in front of you with an acute drop in vision.\n\n*(That papilloedema is bilateral and hypertensive disc oedema likewise, and the differential of bilateral disc swelling, are **not taken from the course material** — `L19.2` prints disc oedema as a hypertensive sign, and `L21) Optic Nerve` covers papilloedema, but no deck sets the two side by side.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Bilateral disc swelling with retinal haemorrhages in renal failure is accelerated hypertension — measure the blood pressure before ordering any imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.85 (Q547), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-548', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'Which of the following is **not a test for macular function**?',
    options:['Color discrimination.','Visual acuity.','Retinoscopy.',"Amsler's grid."],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the list this question is drawn from, and retinoscopy is not on it.** Under macular degeneration:\n\n> **Test for macular function:** V/A · Pupillary light reaction · Color vision · Ophthalmoscopy · Amsilar grid [sic] · Phtosterss test [sic] · Laser inferometry [sic] · Flourescine angiography [sic]\n\nThree of the four options appear in that list verbatim — visual acuity, colour vision and the Amsler grid (printed \"Amsilar\"). **Retinoscopy does not, and could not**: it measures the eye's **refraction** by neutralising the reflex that comes back from the fundus, and it is completely indifferent to what that fundus can *see*. A patient with a destroyed macula and a patient with a normal one give identical retinoscopy findings if their refractive errors match. It is the wrong answer for the same reason at `opqb-t13-525`, `opqb-t13-541` and `opqb-t13-556`.\n\n**Why the other three genuinely test the macula.** The same deck sets out what the macula is for — \"**Sharp clear vision · Color perception · Form vision · Point to point discrimination · UV and blue light protection**\" — and each test probes one of those functions:\n\n| Test | Macular function it probes | What a lesion does to it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Visual acuity** | Sharp central vision, from the highest-density cone packing in the retina | Falls first and furthest; the deck's headline figure for AMD is loss to \"20/200 or worse\" |\n| **Color discrimination** | Colour perception, which is cone-dependent and therefore macular | Acquired dyschromatopsia; the deck lists \"**Color vision defects**\" second among macular symptoms |\n| **Amsler's grid** | Form vision and point-to-point discrimination across the central 10 degrees | Lines bend or break — **metamorphopsia** — and gaps appear where there is a **scotoma**; both are on the deck's macular symptom list, beside micropsia and macropsia. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 123 gives the grid one line: \"Amsler Grid → For the **central 10°** of the visual field\" |\n\n**Why the Amsler grid is the specifically macular one:** ten degrees of central field is almost exactly the macular territory, so the grid tests that area and nothing else. It is also the only self-administered test on the list, which is why patients with drusen are given one to check at home — the change from distortion to a new scotoma is what brings them back before a wet conversion destroys the fovea.\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option C as \"Retinoscopy,\" with a comma. Repaired here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acuity, colour discrimination and the Amsler grid each probe a function of the macula; retinoscopy measures refraction and says nothing about what the retina can see.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q548), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-549', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'A **diabetic patient** complaining of **rapid deterioration of vision overnight**, he suffers most probably from:',
    options:['Macular exudates.','Tractional retinal detachment.','Vitreous haemorrhage.','Macular microaneurysms.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The word doing the work is \"overnight\". Only one diabetic complication can take vision away between going to bed and waking up.**\n\nNew vessels grown in proliferative disease are fragile and unsupported, and they sit on the surface of the retina and the disc where the vitreous can pull on them. When one bleeds, blood enters the vitreous cavity and the eye loses its view of the world in minutes. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` names it among the indications for vitreoretinal surgery: \"**Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage**\" and \"Dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage\". The classical history is exactly the one printed here — a diabetic who wakes up unable to see out of one eye, often with a shower of floaters first, and no pain.\n\n**Why the other three cannot do it overnight:**\n\n| Option | Its actual tempo |\n|---|---|\n| **Macular exudates.** | Lipid deposited from chronically leaking capillaries — \"Hard exudates frequently arranged in **clumps or rings**\" (`L19.1`, mild NPDR). They accumulate over **months** and clear over months. Vision declines gradually. |\n| **Tractional retinal detachment.** | `L20`'s symptom table gives it as \"**Gradual** vision loss and distorted or wavy vision\", progressing \"slow or progressive as scar tissue pulls\". A contracting fibrovascular membrane takes weeks to months to lift the macula. It can *seem* sudden if the macula is finally crossed, but the process is not an overnight one. |\n| **Macular microaneurysms.** | The **earliest** sign of retinopathy — \"Microaneurysms usually temporal to fovea\" (`L19.1`, mild NPDR). Individually they are asymptomatic; they matter because they leak and eventually cause oedema, again over months. |\n\n**The rule to carry:** in diabetic retinopathy, **gradual loss is the macula and sudden loss is blood or traction.** `opqb-t13-515` keys the same three mechanisms — macular oedema, tractional detachment at the macula, vitreous haemorrhage — as the causes of marked visual loss in diabetes.\n\n**What happens next** is the subject of the very next question. `opqb-t13-550` asks the investigation, and the answer follows directly from the mechanism here: an eye full of blood cannot be examined by light, so it is examined by sound.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Overnight visual loss in a diabetic is a vitreous haemorrhage from proliferative new vessels; exudates, traction and microaneurysms all act over months.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q549), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-550', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'In a **diabetic patient who developed rapid deterioration of vision**, the **most valuable diagnostic tool** is:',
    options:['Fluorescein angiography.','Macular electroretinogram.','Ocular ultrasound.','Field examination.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This question is the sequel to `opqb-t13-549`, and the answer follows from that one.** A diabetic who loses vision rapidly has most probably bled into the vitreous — and an eye full of blood cannot be examined with light. Ultrasound is the only tool on the list that works through opaque media.\n\n**What a B-scan answers in this eye, and nothing else can:**\n\n| Question | Why it decides management |\n|---|---|\n| Is the **retina attached**? | `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` makes \"**Retinal detachment involving macula**\" an indication for vitreoretinal surgery. A haemorrhage over an attached retina may be watched while it clears; a haemorrhage hiding a tractional detachment must be operated on. |\n| Is there a **tractional membrane**, and is it pulling? | The other surgical indication, \"progressive proliferation despite laser therapy\". |\n| Is there a **retinal break**? | Changes the operation. |\n| How **dense** is the blood, and is it settling? | Determines whether to wait or to intervene. |\n\n**Why the other three fail here, and each fails for a slightly different reason:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | The most tempting wrong answer, because FA is the diabetic investigation par excellence — `L19.1` classifies all three maculopathies by it (\"Focal leakage\", \"Generalized leakage\", \"Capillary non-perfusion\"). But it is a **photographic** technique: the camera must see the retina to record the dye, and blood in the vitreous blocks both the exciting light and the returning fluorescence. `L19.2` names the effect in its BRVO caption — \"Early - **blocked background fluorescence due to haemorrhage**\". FA is the right test once the media clear. |\n| **Macular electroretinogram.** | Tests **function**, not structure, and tests the wrong thing: it would say whether the macula still responds, but not why the vision has gone or what to do about it. ERG is named once in the whole module (`L18) Cataract II`: \"Electroretinogram (ERG) **if macular disease suspected**\") and is a pre-operative test, not an emergency one. |\n| **Field examination.** | Needs a cooperative patient who can see the target. In an eye that has just lost vision to a dense haemorrhage there is no field to plot, and even a plottable defect would not distinguish blood from detachment. |\n\n**The general principle:** ultrasound is the investigation for an eye you cannot look into. This bank keys the same principle from the other side at `opqb-t13-536`, where the fundus **can** be seen clearly and ultrasound is therefore the false statement, and in the Lens topic at `opqb-t9-371`, where a mature cataract is the obstruction.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A vitreous haemorrhage blocks every light-based test, so B-scan ultrasound is what shows whether the retina behind it is attached — the finding that decides surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q550), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-551', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'Which of the following investigations is **mostly required** in an **elderly patient with a history of central retinal vein occlusion**?',
    options:['Fluorescein angiography.','Serum lipids.','Field of vision plotting.','Sedimentation rate'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem says \"a history of\" — the occlusion has already happened. What remains to be decided is whether this eye is ISCHAEMIC, and that is an angiographic question.**\n\n`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` splits CRVO into two diseases with different futures, and it splits them on perfusion:\n\n| | Non-ischaemic | Ischaemic |\n|---|---|---|\n| Acuity | VA > CF | **VA < 6/60** |\n| APD | mild | **marked** |\n| **FA** | \"**Good retinal capillary perfusion**\" | \"**Extensive capillary non-perfusion**\" |\n| Prognosis | Guarded; \"may subsequently **convert to ischaemic**\" | \"Very poor\"; \"**Rubeosis irides in 50 %**\" |\n\n**The 50 % rubeosis figure is why this matters.** An ischaemic CRVO threatens the eye a second time, months later, through neovascular glaucoma — and that outcome is preventable. The deck prescribes the follow-up in the same breath: \"**Check every month for 6 months** · look for rubeosis and angle new vessels · **treat neovascularization by panretinal photocoagulation**\". Establishing which type this is therefore sets the whole surveillance plan.\n\nFA also answers the other outstanding question in a resolved occlusion — what is limiting the vision now. `L19.2` gives chronic macular oedema as \"the **most common cause of persistent poor VA**\" after a vein occlusion, and makes the treatment decision explicitly angiographic: \"Wait **6-12 weeks and perform FA**. **Macular non-perfusion — no treatment.** Good macular perfusion and VA 6/18 or worse after 3 months — consider **laser photocoagulation**.\" That single sentence is the strongest support for the printed key: the deck says, in so many words, perform FA.\n\n**Why the other three are weaker here:**\n\n| Option | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Serum lipids.** | Part of the systemic risk-factor screen, but `L19.2` does not list hyperlipidaemia among the predisposing factors for vein occlusion at all — its systemic list is \"increasing age · hypertension · diabetes · abnormalities of coagulation\". Lipids matter more in **arterial** disease (\"Atherosclerosis — most common cause of central artery occlusion\"). |\n| **Field of vision plotting.** | Documents the damage rather than guiding treatment. It will not distinguish ischaemic from non-ischaemic, and it will not tell you whether to laser. |\n| **Sedimentation rate** | The test for **giant cell arteritis**, and the deck attaches that to a different vessel — \"Cilioretinal artery occlusion … combined with anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy · **elderly patients with giant cell arteritis** · very poor prognosis\". An urgent ESR in an elderly patient with sudden loss is right; in an established *venous* occlusion it is off target. |\n\n**Note the age term in the stem:** \"increasing age\" is `L19.2`'s first systemic predisposing factor, so an elderly patient needs no further explanation for the occlusion — which is precisely why the useful question shifts from *why did it happen* to *what will it do next*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'After a central retinal vein occlusion, fluorescein angiography separates ischaemic from non-ischaemic by capillary perfusion — the finding that sets the rubeosis surveillance and the laser decision.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q551), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-552', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'Following **cataract surgery**, a patient complains of seeing **floaters and veiling** in his visual field. The probable cause of such complaint is:',
    options:['Intraocular inflammation.','Forward movement of the vitreous body.','Prolapse of the iris.','Postoperative corneal edema.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS A, AND IT IS KEYED AS PRINTED — but option B describes the mechanism that classically produces this exact pair of symptoms, and the divergence is recorded rather than argued away.**\n\n**The case for the printed key.** Every intraocular operation produces some degree of postoperative uveitis. Inflammatory cells and protein released into the anterior vitreous are seen as **floating specks** and, when dense enough, as a **veil** or haze across the field — the two words the stem uses. Anterior uveitis after cataract surgery is common, expected and usually mild, and it is the diagnosis that fits a patient whose complaint is dominated by *seeing things in the way* rather than by loss of acuity. `L18) Cataract II` lists postoperative inflammation among the complications of cataract surgery.\n\n**Why option B is nevertheless the mechanism worth understanding.** Removing the lens takes away the posterior support of the vitreous face, so the vitreous moves forward and, in a proportion of eyes, separates from the retina — a posterior vitreous detachment. The separation itself is seen as floaters and a veil, and it is the reason pseudophakic eyes are at increased risk of retinal breaks and rhegmatogenous detachment. This is exactly the symptom pattern that must not be dismissed: **new floaters and a veil after cataract surgery demand a dilated peripheral examination**, because the alternative to a benign vitreous separation is a retinal tear. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives \"Aging (**vitreous shrinkage**)\" as an RRD cause and its NOT-COVERED list confirms that posterior vitreous detachment is never named in the deck as an entity, so **the mechanism in this paragraph is not taken from the course material**.\n\n**The two are not mutually exclusive**, which is probably why the question is awkward: the same eye can have postoperative inflammation and a fresh vitreous separation at once, and the examination — cells in the anterior chamber, pigment or cells in the anterior vitreous, and a search of the periphery — settles which.\n\n**Why the other two are clearly wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it would actually cause |\n|---|---|\n| **Prolapse of the iris.** | A wound complication, and a visible one: a peaked pupil drawn towards the wound, a dark knuckle of tissue at the limbus, a shallow or leaking anterior chamber, pain and a red eye. It is a surgical emergency because of the endophthalmitis risk. It does not produce floaters. |\n| **Postoperative corneal edema.** | An **anterior** media problem — the whole image goes misty and haloes appear round lights, uniformly and steadily. `L18` lists corneal decompensation among the postoperative complications, with \"retained viscoelastic, raised IOP\" among its causes. A diffuse mist is not a floater; nothing appears to *move* in the field. |\n\n**Distinguish this from the emergency it is not:** `opqb-t9-355`, in the Lens topic, is the postoperative patient who must be assumed to have endophthalmitis — pain, a red eye with ciliary injection, corneal oedema and an **absent red reflex** within 24 to 72 hours. Floaters and veiling with a comfortable white eye are a different presentation entirely.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Floaters and veiling after cataract surgery are keyed to postoperative intraocular inflammation; the forward movement of the vitreous is the competing mechanism, and either way the periphery must be examined for a break.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q552), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-553', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'**Cotton wool spots**:',
    options:['Indicate poor venous retinal outflow.','Are micro infarcts of the nerve fiber layer.','Are commonly seen in normal individuals.','Are seen with retinitis pigmentosa.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A cotton-wool spot is a small infarct, and everything about its appearance follows from that.** A precapillary **arteriole** in the nerve fibre layer closes; the axons it supplied stop transporting material along themselves; and the axoplasm dams up at the two ends of the ischaemic segment. That swollen, stagnant axoplasm is what is seen — a small, fluffy, white patch with soft indistinct edges, always superficial, always in the nerve fibre layer, and usually within the posterior pole where the layer is thickest.\n\n**Why the answer is arteriolar and not venous** — the trap in option A. Cotton-wool spots appear in vein occlusion, but that does not make them a sign of poor venous outflow: what damages the retina in a vein occlusion is the **hypoxia** downstream of the stagnation, and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints exactly that chain — \"Venous Occlusion → **Stagnation** → increased extravascular pressure **and hypoxia** → oedema and haemorrhage\". Oedema and haemorrhage come from the pressure; the cotton-wool spot comes from the hypoxia. That is why the deck lists them as **variable** in both forms of CRVO, and why they appear in diseases with no venous obstruction at all.\n\n**Where the decks put them, which is the differential in one column:**\n\n| Source | Disease |\n|---|---|\n| `L19.2` | BRVO acute signs: \"**Cotton-wool spots** and retinal oedema\" |\n| `L19.2` | CRVO, both types: \"Variable cotton-wool spots\" |\n| `L19.2` | Hypertensive retinopathy, extravascular signs: \"**Cotton-wool spots** and macular star\" |\n| `L19.2` | Papillophlebitis: \"Variable cotton-wool spots and haemorrhages\" |\n| `L19.2` | Blood dyscrasias — anaemias and leukaemias: \"Haemorrhages, **cotton-wool spots** and venous tortuosity\" |\n| `L19.1` | **Severe NPDR**: \"**Cotton-wool spots** · venous irregularities · dark blot haemorrhages · IRMA\" |\n| `L21) Optic Nerve` | Papilloedema photograph: the disc \"strewn with fluffy white-yellow material (**cotton-wool patches**)\" |\n\n**Why the other two are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Are commonly seen in normal individuals.** | The opposite of the truth, and clinically important: even **one** cotton-wool spot in an otherwise normal fundus is abnormal and demands a search for hypertension, diabetes, anaemia, a haemoglobinopathy, a vasculitis or HIV. It is never a normal variant. |\n| **Are seen with retinitis pigmentosa.** | RP is a **degeneration**, not an ischaemic or exudative disease. Its fundus signs are bone-spicule pigment in the mid-periphery, attenuated arterioles and a waxy pale disc. Nothing infarcts and nothing is deposited. |\n\n**A caution about severe NPDR**, since that is the commonest place a student will meet the sign: cotton-wool spots there are a marker that the retina is becoming ischaemic and that proliferation may be coming — `L19.1` says of severe NPDR, \"Treatment - not required but **watch for proliferative disease**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A cotton-wool spot is a precapillary arteriolar microinfarct of the nerve fibre layer seen as dammed axoplasm; it is always pathological and never a normal finding.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q553), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-554', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'Which of the following is **not found** with **central retinal artery occlusion**?',
    options:['No light perception.','A cherry red spot.','Non-reactive pupil to both direct and indirect reaction.','Attenuation of the artery.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The pupil in CRAO loses its DIRECT response and keeps its CONSENSUAL one. An option that takes both away is describing a different lesion.**\n\n`L31,32) Acute visual Loss` sets it out patient by patient in its Case 1 — a 70-year-old with two hours of profound loss, a white retina and a cherry-red spot:\n\n> \"The **right pupil does not respond to light directly but does react consensually.** The left pupil reacts to light directly but not consensually. The swinging-flashlight test confirms a right **relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)**.\"\n\n**Why both halves of that are necessary.** The lesion is on the **afferent** side: the retina cannot send a signal, so light shone into the bad eye produces no constriction anywhere — neither in that eye (direct) nor in the fellow (consensual response *of the fellow eye*). But the **efferent** limb is untouched. The third nerve and the sphincter of the affected eye work perfectly, so when light is shone into the *good* eye, the bad pupil constricts briskly along with it. That preserved response *is* the consensual (indirect) reaction of the affected pupil, and it is the finding option C denies. This bank reproduces the same pupil pair almost verbatim in its own vignette at `opqb-t13-566`.\n\n**What a pupil unreactive to both direct and indirect light would mean:** an **efferent** lesion — a third nerve palsy, pharmacological dilatation, iris damage from trauma or previous surgery, or a globe so distorted that the sphincter cannot act. Those are motor problems, and CRAO is a sensory one.\n\n**Why the other three are found:**\n\n| Option | `L19.2`'s wording |\n|---|---|\n| **No light perception.** | \"VA < 6/60\" and \"very poor prognosis\". A complete central retinal artery occlusion with no cilioretinal sparing can and does end at no perception of light — `opqb-t13-566`'s patient has exactly that. Note the hedge in the deck's own figure, though: *less* than 6/60 is a range, and an eye with a cilioretinal artery keeps a central island (`opqb-t13-516`). |\n| **A cherry red spot.** | \"'Cherry-red spot' at macula\" — the intact choroidal supply showing through the thin, inner-layer-free fovea against a white infarcted background. Written out in full at `opqb-t13-530`. |\n| **Attenuation of the artery.** | \"**Arteriolar and venular narrowing**\", with \"sludging and segmentation of blood column (**cattle-trucking**)\". Unperfused vessels collapse, and the residual blood column breaks into separated segments. |\n\n**One thing to notice about the wording:** the stem says \"not found *with*\", which is a weaker claim than \"never occurs\". A CRAO patient can certainly have a fixed dilated pupil from some *other* cause. The question is asking which of the four is not part of the syndrome.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'CRAO is an afferent lesion — the affected pupil has no direct response but retains its consensual one; loss of both would mean an efferent (third nerve) problem.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q554), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-555', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'The **etiology of rhegmatogenous retinal detachment** is in most conditions due to:',
    options:['The traction by a vitreous hemorrhage.','The development of retinal breaks.','Effusion in the choroidal vessels.','Intra ocular tumors.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The answer is in the name.** *Rhegma* is Greek for a rent or tear, so a rhegmatogenous detachment is by definition the detachment caused by a break. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` defines it in one sentence:\n\n> \"**1. Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (RRD).** This is the **MOST COMMON** type. It occurs when a physical **TEAR OR BREAK** develops in the retina. **Mechanism:** vitreous fluid seeps through the tear and collects in the **subretinal space**, physically pushing the retina away from its underlying blood supply. **Common causes:** aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia, or eye trauma.\"\n\n**Note the two-step causality**, because it is what the other options get wrong. The *cause* is the break; the *consequence* is fluid tracking through it into the subretinal space. Something must first make the hole — vitreous traction as the gel shrinks with age, a thin stretched retina in a high myope, or trauma — and only then does the detachment follow.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and each names a different type of detachment:**\n\n| Option | Which detachment it actually describes |\n|---|---|\n| **The traction by a vitreous hemorrhage.** | Points at **tractional** detachment, and even then not quite: the pulling structure is fibrovascular membrane or scar tissue, not blood. `L20`: \"scar tissue contracts on the surface of the retina, creating 'traction' that lifts it. **Unlike RRD, there is typically no initial tear.**\" That last clause is the direct contradiction — a tractional detachment is the one *without* a break. |\n| **Effusion in the choroidal vessels.** | **Exudative** detachment: \"fluid **leaks** and builds up behind the retina **without any tears or traction** … inflammatory or vascular conditions cause fluid from the choroid to seep into the subretinal space.\" |\n| **Intra ocular tumors.** | Also exudative — `L20` names \"tumors (such as **choroidal melanoma**)\" among the causes, and its management slide adds \"radiation/surgery for ocular tumors\". A tumour lifts the retina by leaking beneath it, not by tearing it. |\n\n**How the three are told apart at the slit lamp**, from the deck's own differentiation table — this is the single most examinable slide in `L20`:\n\n| Feature | Rhegmatogenous | Tractional | Exudative |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Main cause | **Retinal tear/break** | Scar tissue pulling | Fluid leakage |\n| Retinal surface | **Corrugated/wrinkled** | Smooth/taut | Smooth/bullous |\n| Common risk | **Myopia, aging** | Diabetes | Inflammation, tumours |\n\n**Consequence for treatment:** because the break is the cause, sealing it is the cure — `L20` gives vitrectomy with gas or oil, scleral buckling, and pneumatic retinopexy followed by laser or cryotherapy, and states that \"**surgical intervention is almost always required** to seal the retinal break\". Where the same break is found *before* the retina comes off, laser alone suffices (`opqb-t13-571`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Rhegmatogenous means caused by a break — vitreous fluid passes through the tear into the subretinal space; traction and choroidal leakage define the other two types.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q555), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-556', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'Which of the following is a **retinal function test**?',
    options:['Retinoscopy.','Field of vision.','Fundoscopy.','Tonometry.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Divide the four into what they MEASURE, and only one measures what the retina can do.**\n\n| Test | What it measures | Category |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Retinoscopy.** | The eye's refractive error, from the movement of the reflex | **Refraction** |\n| **Field of vision.** | The retinal sensitivity to light, point by point, across the whole seeing area | **Function** |\n| **Fundoscopy.** | The appearance of the retina | **Structure** |\n| **Tonometry.** | Intraocular pressure | **Pressure** |\n\n**Why perimetry is a retinal function test.** The retina is a sheet of receptors, and every point on it maps to a point in the visual field. Plotting the field is therefore plotting the sensitivity of that sheet — it asks each area of retina, in turn, how dim a light it can still see, and records the answer. That is a direct measurement of retinal function, and it is topographic, which nothing else on the list is. It is also the test that detects loss the patient has not noticed: the constricted, tubular field of advanced retinitis pigmentosa (`opqb-t13-572`), the annular scotoma of `opqb-t13-560`, the sectorial defect of a branch occlusion (`opqb-t13-576`), and the curtain of a detachment.\n\n**Why a normal-looking fundus does not settle the question**, which is the real point of the contrast with fundoscopy: structure and function come apart in both directions. A retina can look perfectly normal and see nothing — `opqb-t13-580`'s patient has \"both fundi showed no abnormal finding\" with bilateral no perception of light — and a retina can look terrible and still see, as in the ischaemic maculopathy `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` describes: \"Macula appears **relatively normal** · **poor visual acuity** · capillary non-perfusion on FA\". Looking is not testing.\n\n**Why the other two are wrong beyond their category:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Retinoscopy.** | The reflex it watches comes off the retina, which is what makes the option superficially plausible — but the retina is only acting as a mirror. A blind eye with a clear media refracts exactly like a seeing one. Wrong here for the same reason as at `opqb-t13-525`, `opqb-t13-541` and `opqb-t13-548`. |\n| **Tonometry.** | Measures the pressure inside the globe. It is essential in glaucoma and it is one of the criteria in retinal detachment (`opqb-t13-520`: \"simple RD usually has low or normal IOP\"), but it reports on aqueous dynamics, not on photoreception. |\n\n**The other true retinal function tests**, none of them offered here: visual acuity, colour vision, the Amsler grid (`opqb-t13-548`) and the electroretinogram (`opqb-t13-542`, `opqb-t13-560`). What they share is that the patient — or the retina itself, in the case of the ERG — has to *respond*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Perimetry measures retinal sensitivity point by point and is therefore a function test; fundoscopy shows structure, retinoscopy shows refraction and tonometry shows pressure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q556), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-557', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'A **diabetic patient** came complaining of difficulty in far and near sight. **Glasses were done but the vision did not improve more than 6/12**. The **most important next step** is:',
    options:['Examination of his fundus.','Electro-retinogram.','Electro-oculogram.','Ultrasonography.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Vision that will not correct with glasses is not a refractive problem, and in a diabetic the first place to look is the retina.**\n\nThe logic of the vignette runs in two steps. First, the refraction has already been done and has failed — best-corrected acuity is 6/12, so whatever is limiting vision lies **behind the optics**. Second, the patient is diabetic, and diabetes has a specific, common and treatable way of limiting best-corrected acuity: **maculopathy**. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` devotes half its slides to it, and the criteria for clinically significant macular oedema are geometric and macular — \"retinal oedema within **500 µm** of the centre of the fovea\", \"hard exudates within 500 µm of the centre with adjacent oedema\", \"retinal oedema **one disc area or larger**, any part of which is within one disc diameter (**1500 µm**) of the centre of the fovea\". None of that can be suspected without looking.\n\n**Why fundoscopy and not a machine.** It is immediate, free, available in the same consultation, and it answers the question outright — a macula thickened with exudate, or a fundus full of new vessels, ends the enquiry. Everything else on the list is a second-line test that only becomes sensible once the fundus has been seen or once seeing it has proved impossible.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the next step |\n|---|---|\n| **Electro-retinogram.** | Tests photoreceptor and inner retinal **function** as a mass response. It is reserved for eyes where the fundus cannot be seen or where a widespread dystrophy is suspected. `L18) Cataract II` gives its only appearance in the whole module — \"Electroretinogram (ERG) **if macular disease suspected**\" — and even there it is a pre-operative test used when the view is obstructed. Nothing here obstructs the view. |\n| **Electro-oculogram.** | Tests the standing potential across the **retinal pigment epithelium**, and its use is in a narrow set of RPE dystrophies (classically Best disease). It has no role in diabetic visual loss. This bank prints EOG only twice, here and in `opqb-t13-560`'s box, and it is never taught in any deck. |\n| **Ultrasonography.** | For **opaque media** or an intraocular mass. The stem gives no reason to think the media are opaque; if the patient had a dense cataract or a vitreous haemorrhage the answer would change, which is exactly the situation at `opqb-t13-550`. |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the stem does not say.** \"Difficulty in far and near sight\" in a diabetic can also be the fluctuating refraction of poor glycaemic control — hyperglycaemia swells the lens and shifts the refraction, so glasses prescribed on a bad day are wrong on a good one. That is another reason not to trust the spectacle result, and another reason to look at the fundus rather than to re-refract. *(The osmotic lens-swelling mechanism is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** EOG and VEP are named in **none** of the thirty cached lecture decks — measured, case-insensitively, with every hit opened; the only occurrences of \"VEP\" in the corpus are inside three decks' own NOT-COVERED lists. The descriptions of ERG and EOG above are **not taken from the course material** beyond the single `L18` line quoted.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Best-corrected 6/12 in a diabetic means the problem is behind the optics — look at the fundus for maculopathy before ordering any electrophysiology or imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.86 (Q557), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-558', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Middle-aged patient with myopia of -19.00 diopters**, complained of **muscae volitanes, flashes of light and a lower black veil** in the field of vision. The **key to diagnosis** is:',
    options:['Electroretinogram.','Visual evoked potential.','Field of vision.','Fundus examination.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Myopic patient with floaters → **fundus exam confirms retinal pathology.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The diagnosis is already made by the history; the fundus is where it is confirmed and where the treatment is planned.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` prints the rhegmatogenous symptom triad and the stem reproduces it item for item:\n\n| `L20` slide 11, rhegmatogenous row | What this patient reports |\n|---|---|\n| \"**Floaters**\" | muscae volitantes |\n| \"flashes of light\" | flashes of light |\n| \"a '**curtain**' or shadow in the peripheral vision\" | a lower black veil |\n\nAnd the risk factor is printed too: \"Common causes: aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia** (nearsightedness), or eye trauma\". Minus nineteen dioptres is very high myopia — a long eye with a thin, stretched peripheral retina and a vitreous that liquefied years ago.\n\n**⚠️ Note which way round the veil and the detachment lie.** The retinal image is inverted, so a **lower** field defect means the detachment is **superior**. That matters clinically as well as anatomically: a superior detachment is the one that spreads downwards under gravity and reaches the macula fastest, so it is the more urgent.\n\n**Why fundus examination and not the others.** Only looking can answer the three questions that decide what happens today: is the macula still attached, how far has the fluid spread, and **where is the break**? The break has to be found before anything can be sealed, and it will be in the periphery, which means indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation or a three-mirror lens (`opqb-t13-536` names the Goldmann lens for exactly this).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Electroretinogram.** | A mass response of the photoreceptor layer. It would be reduced over a detached area, but it gives no location, no extent and no break — and it is reserved for eyes whose fundus **cannot** be seen. `L18) Cataract II` gives its only appearance in the module: \"Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected\". |\n| **Visual evoked potential.** | Tests the whole pathway from retina to occipital cortex, so it is used for optic nerve and pathway disease — this bank keys it at `opqb-t13-581`, in a patient whose fundi are normal. Here the fundus is abnormal and the VEP would only confirm that something is wrong somewhere. **VEP is named in no cached lecture deck at all** (measured; the only occurrences of the abbreviation are inside three decks' own NOT-COVERED lists), so this row is **not taken from the course material**. |\n| **Field of vision.** | Would map the veil accurately and might even suggest the quadrant. But it takes a cooperative patient, it takes time this eye does not have, and it still would not show the break. It documents; it does not diagnose. |\n\n**Siblings in this topic:** `opqb-t13-545` (flashes only, no veil — a break before detachment), `opqb-t13-569` (same symptoms after trauma, same answer), `opqb-t13-571` (a break found and lasered), and `opqb-t13-564`/`opqb-t13-565` for the mechanisms of the floaters and the flashes.",
    objective:'Floaters, flashes and a veil in a high myope are a rhegmatogenous detachment; only fundus examination locates the break and the extent, which is what decides the operation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q558), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-559', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'Which of these **senile changes** can lead to **permanent affection of vision**?',
    options:['Senile enophthalmos.','Arcus senilis.','Senile cataract.','Senile macular degeneration.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The word that decides this is PERMANENT.** Three of the four are either harmless or reversible; only one destroys tissue that cannot be replaced.\n\n`L35,36) Chronic visual loss` states it directly: \"In the United States, age-related macular degeneration is the leading cause of **irreversible central visual loss** (20/200 or worse) among people aged 52 or older.\" The same deck explains why the distinction is worth drawing at all: \"It is important to distinguish between the possible causes of visual loss, whether **cataract (surgically correctable)**, glaucoma (medically or surgically treatable), or **macular degeneration** (potentially laser treatable).\"\n\n**Why AMD is permanent and cataract is not.** A cataract is an opacity in the light path; take it out and put a lens in its place, and the retina behind it sees again — the eye is optically restored. AMD kills the retina itself. `L35,36`: \"**Dry type 90 % — slow progressive atrophy of RPE and photoreceptors.** Wet type 10 % — RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization.\" Photoreceptors and retinal pigment epithelium do not regenerate, so once a patch of macula has atrophied or scarred, the central scotoma over it is permanent. Treatment can only stop further loss — \"For dry AMD dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins especially **Vit. E**. For wet AMD injections of **Anti VEGF**.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why vision survives it |\n|---|---|\n| **Senile enophthalmos.** | The globe sinks slightly as orbital fat atrophies with age. It changes the **appearance** of the eye — a deeper superior sulcus, an apparent ptosis — and nothing else. It does not touch the optics or the retina. |\n| **Arcus senilis.** | A grey-white lipid ring deposited in the **peripheral** corneal stroma, separated from the limbus by a clear zone. It is peripheral by definition, so it never reaches the visual axis, and it has no effect on vision at all. Its only significance is systemic: in a patient under about 50 it raises the question of hyperlipidaemia. |\n| **Senile cataract.** | The commonest cause of *reversible* blindness in the world. `L35,36`'s own management list is a series of operations — \"ICCE · ECCE · ECCE IOL · **PHACO IOL**\" — and a successful phacoemulsification with an intraocular lens restores the vision the lens was blocking. It is the deck's own example of \"surgically correctable\". |\n\n**⚠️ The exception worth naming, so the rule is not applied blindly:** a cataract left until it is hypermature can cause **permanent** loss by a different route — phacolytic or phacomorphic glaucoma damaging the optic nerve. That is a complication of neglect, not a property of the cataract, and the Lens topic asks it directly (`opqb-t9-351`, `opqb-t9-404`, `opqb-t9-405`).\n\n**Two local numbers from the same deck**, worth carrying because they are specific to this course: \"The overall prevalence of AMD in **Alexandria** was 6.6 %\", early 5.3 % and late 2.4 %, \"significantly higher … among those aged at least **75 years**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Senile macular degeneration destroys photoreceptors and RPE and is irreversible; enophthalmos and arcus do not affect vision and a cataract is surgically correctable.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q559), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-560', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'A **healthy male** engaged to his **healthy cousin**. **His father-in-law** is suffering from **night blindness, annular scotoma, and marked defective vision**. Choose **one procedure you want to do before marriage**.',
    options:['Fundus examination.','Color vision test.','Field of vision.','Electroretinogram.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Investigations of AMD:\n\nAlthough diagnosis **is mainly clinical**, yet, some electrophysiological tests assist in diagnosis:\n\n**1. ERG (electroretinography).**\n**2. EOG (electrooculography).**\n\n---\n\n*(The block above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — including its heading, which is not labelled \"Explanation\" as the other boxes are. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE BOX IS HEADED \"AMD\" AND THE VIGNETTE IS RETINITIS PIGMENTOSA.** Night blindness plus an annular (ring) scotoma plus marked visual loss is the RP triad — `opqb-t13-527`'s own printed box gives it as \"1. Night blindness (**the main and the earliest** complaint) 2. Decreased peripheral vision 'tunnel vision' (due to rod atrophy) 3. Decreased central vision (macular affection)\" — and a **consanguineous marriage** is only a question at all in an inherited disease. Age-related macular degeneration is not inherited in a way that makes a pre-marital test meaningful, and it does not cause night blindness or a ring scotoma. The box's heading is wrong; **the key (D, electroretinogram) is right for the RP reading and is not in doubt.** Recorded, not disputed.\n\n**Read the genetics carefully — the person at risk is the FIANCÉE, not the man.** \"His father-in-law\" is *her* father, and he has the disease. She is therefore either affected herself, still presymptomatic, or a carrier. The man is described as healthy and is not the father-in-law's blood relative — but he **is her cousin**, so he may carry the same recessive allele from their shared grandparents. That is the whole reason the question exists: consanguinity turns a recessive carrier state in one family branch into a real risk of affected children.\n\n**Why the ERG.** It is the only test on the list that detects the disease **before it declares itself**. The electroretinogram is a mass response of the photoreceptor layer, so it falls as soon as a large number of rods are dysfunctional — earlier than the patient notices night blindness, earlier than the field constricts, and earlier than bone-spicule pigment appears in the fundus. Asked to choose *one* procedure before a marriage, you choose the one that can detect subclinical disease in an apparently healthy young woman.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it comes too late |\n|---|---|\n| **Fundus examination.** | Shows bone-spicule pigment, attenuated arterioles and a waxy pale disc — but only once the degeneration is **established**. A normal fundus in an early case is falsely reassuring. |\n| **Color vision test.** | Cone-dependent, and cones are affected **last** in RP. Normal colour vision excludes nothing. |\n| **Field of vision.** | Detects the ring scotoma and the constriction, which is the *second* symptom, not the first. It is also subjective and needs a cooperative patient; the ERG needs neither. |\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** Retinitis pigmentosa is taught in **none** of the thirty cached decks (measured — exactly two hits, both opened, neither teaching), and **EOG is named in no deck at all**; ERG appears once, `L18) Cataract II`: \"Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected\". The genetics and the electrophysiology above are **not taken from the course material**.\n\n**This is the antecedent for `opqb-t13-561`**, which asks the same question with a budget attached.",
    objective:'In a consanguineous marriage with retinitis pigmentosa in the father of the bride, the ERG is the one test that detects the disease before the fundus, the field or colour vision change.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q560), key p.91 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-561', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'A healthy man is engaged to his healthy cousin, and **her father** has night blindness, an annular scotoma and marked defective vision. If you want to **cut short the expenses to the minimum required**, which **one** procedure must you do before the marriage?',
    options:['Field of vision for him.','Field of vision for her.','Electroretinogram for him.','Electroretinogram for her.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE RESTATED. The bank prints only \"In the Previous case…\", and the antecedent is `opqb-t13-560` — verified rather than assumed** (the question before that one, `opqb-t13-559`, is a one-line senile-changes item with no patient in it). The vignette above is `opqb-t13-560`'s, restated because the options here are meaningless without knowing who \"him\" and \"her\" are.\n\n**Two decisions, and the question forces you to make both.**\n\n**Decision one — which person.** The disease is in **her** family: the affected man is her father. She is therefore at direct risk of being affected or of carrying the gene. The fiancé is healthy and is not the affected man's blood relative; he is only at risk because he is **her cousin**, so he might carry the same recessive allele from a shared ancestor. If you can afford one test, you test the person with the affected parent — a normal result in her is far more informative than a normal result in him. She is the one who could be presymptomatic *right now*.\n\n**Decision two — which test.** The same reasoning as `opqb-t13-560`. The electroretinogram is a mass response of the photoreceptor layer and falls **before** symptoms, before the field constricts and before bone-spicule pigment appears. A perimetry field in an early case is normal, so a normal field proves nothing; a normal full-field ERG in a young adult at risk is genuinely reassuring.\n\n**Why the other three lose:**\n\n| Option | Why it is the wrong single test |\n|---|---|\n| **Field of vision for him.** | Wrong person and wrong test — a double miss. |\n| **Field of vision for her.** | Right person, but the field is the **second** symptom, not the first (`opqb-t13-527`'s box: night blindness is \"the main and the earliest complaint\", tunnel vision comes after). It is also subjective and effort-dependent. |\n| **Electroretinogram for him.** | Right test, wrong person. He has no affected first-degree relative; his risk is second-order, through consanguinity, and it only becomes relevant **if she is shown to be a carrier**. |\n\n**⚠️ The economics clause is not decoration.** \"Cut short the expenses to the minimum required\" is the examiner asking you to rank, not to list — and the ranking rule is the same one used everywhere in screening: test the person with the highest prior probability, with the test that has the earliest sensitivity. Doing all four is better medicine and a worse answer to this question.\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** Retinitis pigmentosa, its inheritance and the ERG's presymptomatic sensitivity are **not taken from the course material** — measured: two hits for the disease across all thirty cached decks, neither of them teaching, and one hit for ERG (`L18) Cataract II`, in the pre-operative cataract work-up). The symptom triad is the bank's own, printed at `opqb-t13-527`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Test the person with the affected parent, with the test that turns abnormal earliest — an ERG for the fiancée, not a field and not the fiancé.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q561), key p.91; stem restated from Q560 (printed as "In the Previous case")' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-562', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'**Macular edema** is a finding in the following **except**:',
    options:['Renal hypertension.','Diabetic maculopathy.','Central retinal vein occlusion','Central retinal artery occlusion'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Oedema needs blood to arrive and leak. In an arterial occlusion no blood arrives, so nothing leaks.**\n\nThat is the whole discrimination, and it separates the last option from the first three cleanly:\n\n| Option | Mechanism | `L19.1` / `L19.2` wording |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Renal hypertension.** | Raised arterial pressure breaks the inner blood–retinal barrier; lipid-rich fluid escapes at the macula | Extravascular signs: \"Cotton-wool spots and **macular star**\" — the star *is* resolved macular exudate (`opqb-t13-539`) |\n| **Diabetic maculopathy.** | \"**Microvascular leakage**\", one of the two pathogenetic routes | The whole \"clinically significant macular oedema\" slide, with its 500 µm and 1500 µm criteria and the three maculopathies — focal, diffuse (\"frequent **cystoid macular oedema**\") and ischaemic |\n| **Central retinal vein occlusion** | Blood keeps arriving and cannot leave; pressure rises in the capillary bed until fluid is forced into the tissue | \"Venous Occlusion → Stagnation → **increased extravascular pressure** and hypoxia → **Oedema** and haemorrhage\"; and, for both CRVO types, \"**Chronic macular oedema**\" — \"the **most common cause of persistent poor VA**\" |\n| **Central retinal artery occlusion** | Supply cut off; the bed is empty and collapsing | \"Retinal whitening · '**Cherry-red spot**' at macula · **Arteriolar and venular narrowing** · sludging and segmentation of blood column (cattle-trucking)\" — no oedema anywhere on the list |\n\n**⚠️ The trap is the word \"whitening\", because it looks like swelling.** The retina in CRAO does turn white and it *is* thickened — but that is **intracellular cytotoxic swelling** of dying ischaemic cells, not extracellular fluid leaked from vessels. It is an infarct, not an oedema. Clinically the two behave completely differently: cytotoxic whitening clears over weeks as the dead tissue is resorbed, leaving a thin atrophic retina and a permanent scotoma, whereas true macular oedema thickens the retina, distorts the image, shows leakage on angiography and can be treated by stopping the leak.\n\n**And the macula is the one part of the CRAO retina that is NOT swollen at all** — that is exactly why the cherry-red spot exists. At the fovea the inner layers are absent (`L20` slide 5: \"the retina thins to a pit with the inner layers absent\"), so there is nothing there to infarct, and the intact choroidal circulation shows through. `opqb-t13-530` and `opqb-t13-546` develop the same anatomy.\n\n**A useful pair to hold together:** venous occlusion → oedema; arterial occlusion → infarction. `opqb-t13-514` and `opqb-t13-519` test the same divide from the CRVO side.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Macular oedema is leaked extracellular fluid and needs perfusion — hypertension, diabetes and vein occlusion all produce it; artery occlusion produces cytotoxic infarction instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q562), key p.91' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-563', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'A **50-year-old man** noted **decreased visual acuity in the right eye yesterday** while **accidentally occluding his left eye**. He is wearing **glasses of -10.00 diopters**. His best-corrected visual acuity is **6/18 in the right eye and 6/6 in the left**. What is your **first recommended investigation**?',
    options:['Fluorescein angiography.','Ultrasonography.','Electroretinogram.','Visually evoked potential.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Three facts in the stem point at one place: a high myope, a unilateral central acuity drop, and a fundus that can be seen (nothing in the stem obstructs the view).** The lesion is at the **macula**, and the investigation for a macular lesion in a myope is an angiogram.\n\n**What is being looked for.** Pathological myopia at -10.00 dioptres stretches the posterior pole: the sclera thins, Bruch's membrane develops lacquer cracks, and choroidal new vessels can grow through those breaks into the subretinal space — **myopic choroidal neovascularisation**. It is a classic cause of a sudden or subacute central drop in a middle-aged high myope, and it is treatable with anti-VEGF injections if it is caught. Fluorescein angiography is what demonstrates it: a small area of early hyperfluorescence that **leaks progressively** through the late frames. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints the mechanism for the age-related version of the same lesion — wet AMD is \"RPE detachment and **choroidal neovascularization**\", treated with \"injections of **Anti VEGF**\" — and `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` shows FA doing exactly this job on the macula, separating \"**Focal** leakage on FA\", \"**Generalized** leakage on FA\" and \"**Capillary non-perfusion** on FA\".\n\n**⚠️ Note how the loss was discovered, because it is the give-away for a monocular macular lesion.** He found it \"accidentally occluding his left eye\" — the good eye had been covering for the bad one, which means the deficit is **central and unilateral** and has probably been there longer than a day. Peripheral disease and detachment announce themselves with flashes, floaters or a curtain; this patient has none of those.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not first |\n|---|---|\n| **Ultrasonography.** | The test for **opaque media** or a mass. Nothing here is opaque — best-corrected acuity of 6/18 means the media transmit — so the retina can simply be looked at, and then photographed. Ultrasound would show posterior staphyloma in a myope this long, which is interesting and not the answer. |\n| **Electroretinogram.** | A **mass** response of the whole photoreceptor layer, so it is dominated by the peripheral retina and is relatively insensitive to a small macular lesion. It is used for widespread dystrophies or when the fundus cannot be seen. `L18) Cataract II` gives its only appearance in the module — \"Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected\" — as a pre-operative test behind a cataract. |\n| **Visually evoked potential.** | Tests conduction along the whole pathway from retina to occipital cortex, so it is the optic-nerve and pathway test — this bank keys it at `opqb-t13-581`, in a patient with **normal fundi**. Here the abnormality is expected to be visible, and a VEP would only report that something, somewhere, is wrong. |\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option A as \"**Fluoresceln** angiography\", and the stem prints \"glasses of-10.00\" and \"6/6 **In** the left eye\". Repaired here.\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** Myopic choroidal neovascularisation is named in no cached deck — `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` mentions myopia only as an RRD risk factor, and its NOT-COVERED list records \"MYOPIC degenerative changes beyond the bare words 'high myopia'\" as absent. **The mechanism above is not taken from the course material**; the FA captions and the anti-VEGF treatment are printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A unilateral central acuity drop in a high myope with clear media is a macular lesion — most likely myopic choroidal neovascularisation — and fluorescein angiography is what demonstrates the leak.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q563), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-564', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Retinal tears cause muscae volitantes** due to:',
    options:['The retina is getting detached.','Myopic vitreous degeneration throwing shadows on the retina.','Red blood cells in the vitreous from the retinal tear.','Irritation of the photoreceptors from the tearing process.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Floaters = **vitreous hemorrhage from tear.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**A floater is a shadow. Something opaque has to be sitting in the vitreous, in front of the retina, for the patient to see one — and the question is asking what that something is when the cause is a tear.**\n\nThe retina is a vascular membrane. When the vitreous pulls hard enough to tear it, the tear crosses a retinal vessel, and blood escapes into the vitreous cavity. Red cells suspended in gel cast moving shadows on the retina behind them, and the patient reports a sudden shower of dark specks that drift with eye movement and settle when the eye is still. That is why the symptom is **sudden and new** rather than long-standing, and why a *shower* of floaters is the finding that matters.\n\n**This is the diagnostic value of the symptom.** A few chronic floaters are common and harmless. New floaters after a tear mean there is blood in the vitreous, and blood in the vitreous after a tear means the tear crossed a vessel — which is why every patient with sudden floaters needs a dilated peripheral examination (`opqb-t13-536`, `opqb-t13-558`, `opqb-t13-569`). If the shower is dense enough to obscure the fundus, the eye is examined by ultrasound instead (`opqb-t13-550`).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **The retina is getting detached.** | Confuses the sequence. A detachment produces a **field defect** — `L20`'s \"'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\" — which is a region that has gone dark and *stays* dark in that part of the field. A floater moves, drifts and is seen against every background. Detachment may follow the tear, but it is not what makes the specks. |\n| **Myopic vitreous degeneration throwing shadows on the retina.** | A real mechanism, and the reason many myopes have floaters for years — but it is not what the stem asks. The question specifies floaters caused by a **tear**, which is an acute event with blood in it. Chronic degenerative floaters are gradual and stable; the ones that matter are the new ones. |\n| **Irritation of the photoreceptors from the tearing process.** | That is the mechanism of the **flashes**, not the floaters — and this bank keys it as such in the very next question, whose box reads \"Flashes = **traction on photoreceptors**\" (`opqb-t13-565`). The two questions are a deliberate pair and each other's distractors. |\n\n**Hold the pair together:** *traction* is felt as **light**, because a stimulated retina can only report light; *blood* is seen as a **shadow**, because it blocks light before it reaches the retina. One is the retina responding, the other is the vitreous obstructing.\n\n*(That new floaters specifically indicate a vessel torn along with the retina, and that chronic myopic floaters are benign, is a reading of the bank's own two boxes rather than a separately printed rule; `L20`'s NOT-COVERED list confirms that posterior vitreous detachment and break morphology are absent from the deck.)*",
    objective:'Floaters after a retinal tear are red cells in the vitreous casting shadows; traction on photoreceptors gives the flashes and a detachment gives the field defect.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q564), key p.92 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-565', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Retinal tear causes flashes of light** due to:',
    options:['Stimulation of photoreceptors by the tear since the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light.','Presence of the blood in the vitreous from the tear.','Axoplasmic transport delay in the optic nerve.','Field defect corresponding to the tear itself.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Flashes = **traction on photoreceptors**.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The retina has one output, and that output is light.** It has no pain fibres, so it cannot report a mechanical stimulus as an ache; it reports it the only way it can, as a **photopsia**. The option itself states the principle — \"the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light\" — and it is worth carrying beyond this question, because it is also why a retinal detachment is painless (`opqb-t13-531`) and why a tear that is destroying an eye can produce no discomfort at all.\n\n**⚠️ The box says \"traction\" and the option says \"stimulation by the tear\". They are the same event described from two ends.** As the vitreous gel liquefies and shrinks, it stays adherent at points on the peripheral retina and tugs on them with every eye movement. That tug **is** the stimulus; when it finally exceeds what the retina can withstand, the retina tears — and the traction continues on the edges of the tear. There is no discrepancy of substance, and the key is not in doubt.\n\n**Clinical features that follow from the mechanism:**\n\n| Feature | Why |\n|---|---|\n| The flashes are **brief and repeated** | Each eye movement produces one tug |\n| They are worse **in the dark** | No competing visual input |\n| They are **peripheral**, usually temporal | Vitreous adhesions are strongest at the peripheral retina |\n| The **field location is inverted** | Flashes in the temporal field come from the **nasal** retina — `opqb-t13-545` builds the same inversion into its stem, and `opqb-t13-576` works it out formally |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually explains |\n|---|---|\n| **Presence of the blood in the vitreous from the tear.** | The **floaters** — the very next fact this bank keys, at `opqb-t13-564`, whose box reads \"Floaters = vitreous hemorrhage from tear\". Blood is opaque, so it **subtracts** light and is seen as a moving shadow. An opacity cannot generate a percept. |\n| **Axoplasmic transport delay in the optic nerve.** | The mechanism of **disc swelling**, not of photopsia. Stasis of axoplasm at the lamina cribrosa is what makes the disc swell in papilloedema, and dammed axoplasm in the nerve fibre layer is what a cotton-wool spot is made of (`opqb-t13-553`). Neither produces flashes. |\n| **Field defect corresponding to the tear itself.** | A field defect is a **loss** of vision in an area, which is the opposite of a positive phenomenon like a flash. It is also the symptom of the *detachment* that may follow — `L20`'s \"'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\" — not of the tear. |\n\n**The distinction the pair teaches, in one line:** positive phenomena (flashes) mean the retina is being **stimulated**; negative phenomena (shadows, curtains) mean light is being **blocked** or the retina has stopped working.\n\n*(The eye-movement timing, the worse-in-the-dark observation and the peripheral localisation are **not taken from the course material** — `L20` prints the symptom list but no mechanism, and its NOT-COVERED list records that the vitreoretinal traction mechanism for a break is absent from the deck.)*",
    objective:'Vitreoretinal traction stimulates photoreceptors and a retina that has no pain fibres reports it as light; blood explains the floaters and axoplasmic stasis explains disc swelling.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.87 (Q565), key p.92 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-566', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A **67-year old man** experienced **sudden loss of vision in the left eye three hours ago**. His best-corrected visual acuity is **6/6 in the right eye and no perception of light in the left**. The **right pupil responds to light directly but not consensually**. The **left pupil responds to light consensually but not directly**. What is your **most probable diagnosis**?',
    options:['Central retinal vein occlusion.','Central retinal artery occlusion','Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.','Diabetic maculopathy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**This vignette is `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` Case 1, re-dressed. The deck prints the same pupil pair almost word for word:**\n\n> \"A **70-year-old** man notes **sudden profound loss of vision** in his right eye, with onset **2 hours ago** … the visual acuity is OD hand motion and OS 20/20. **The right pupil does not respond to light directly but does react consensually. The left pupil reacts to light directly but not consensually.** The swinging-flashlight test confirms a right **relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)** … Retinal examination reveals a **white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot**. **Diagnosis: Central Retinal Artery Occlusion.**\"\n\n**Decode the pupils, because they are the whole answer.** The description is a **total afferent defect on the left**:\n\n| Light shone into | Left pupil | Right pupil | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Left eye** | no constriction (**no direct**) | no constriction (**no consensual** in the right) | Nothing is getting *in* on the left |\n| **Right eye** | constricts (**consensual present**) | constricts (**direct present**) | The left sphincter and third nerve are intact |\n\nSo the efferent limb of the left eye works perfectly and the afferent limb is dead. That is a **retinal or optic nerve** lesion, and the depth — **no perception of light within three hours**, in an eye that was normal this morning — narrows it to a catastrophic vascular event. `opqb-t13-554` keys the same pupil logic in one line: the finding that would be *wrong* for CRAO is a pupil unreactive to both direct **and** indirect light, because that would be an efferent problem.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Central retinal vein occlusion.** | Wrong severity and wrong tempo. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives even the ischaemic form \"VA **< 6/60**\" with a marked APD — bad, but not usually no perception of light — and it develops over hours to days, not in one step. The fundus would be full of blood, not white. |\n| **Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.** | Wrong symptoms and wrong pupil. A detachment announces itself with flashes, floaters and a **curtain** (`L20` slide 11), the loss is progressive rather than instantaneous, and unless the whole retina is off it does not extinguish light perception. A macula-off detachment gives at best a mild RAPD, not a total one. |\n| **Diabetic maculopathy.** | Wrong everything. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` describes a **gradual** central blur from oedema or exudate; the patient is not stated to be diabetic; maculopathy affects the central field and leaves peripheral vision, so it cannot produce no perception of light; and it produces no afferent pupillary defect of this magnitude. |\n\n**⚠️ The clock in the stem is the point of the question.** Three hours is inside the window in which anything might be attempted — but the deck that teaches this case leaves its management slide **completely blank**, and this bank's own answer to *what to aim for* is the next question, `opqb-t13-567`: \"CRAO has poor prognosis; **focus on prophylaxis**.\" The systemic implication is the real emergency: a 67-year-old with a retinal embolus has had a stroke in miniature, and the carotids and heart need investigating today (`L19.2` gives a whole slide to duplex scanning, MR angiography and carotid endarterectomy for \"symptomatic carotid stenosis > 70%\").\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A pupil that has lost its direct but kept its consensual response, with sudden total loss of vision, is a complete afferent lesion — in an elderly patient over three hours, central retinal artery occlusion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q566), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-567', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A 67-year-old man lost the vision of his **left eye three hours ago**. Acuity is **6/6 right and no perception of light left**; the **right pupil reacts directly but not consensually** and the **left pupil reacts consensually but not directly**. In this case the **aim of the management** is:',
    options:['Complete visual recovery','Complete visual recovery and prophylaxis against second attack.','Moderate visual recovery and prophylaxis against second attack.','Poor visual recovery with detection of the underlying causes for prophylaxis.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**CRAO** has poor **prognosis; focus on prophylaxis.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE RESTATED. The bank prints only \"In the Previous case…\", and the antecedent is `opqb-t13-566` — verified rather than assumed** (`opqb-t13-565` is a mechanism one-liner with no patient in it). The stem above restates `opqb-t13-566`'s **findings only** and deliberately does **not** name the diagnosis: the printed box names it, and putting the diagnosis into the stem would hand the reader the answer.\n\n**Why the honest answer is the pessimistic one.** The inner retina is neural tissue and it infarcts fast. Once the central retinal artery has been occluded for hours, most of what it supplied is dead, and no procedure brings it back — which is why `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` ends its CRAO sign list with the flat phrase \"**Very poor prognosis**\". Anything offered acutely (ocular massage, carbogen, anterior chamber paracentesis — see `opqb-t13-524`) is an attempt worth making because the cost is low, not a treatment with a good expected yield.\n\n**Why prophylaxis is not a consolation prize but the main event.** A retinal artery occlusion is an **embolic or thrombotic event in a cerebral-territory artery**. The eye is the organ that reported it; the next one may be the brain. `L19.2` devotes several slides to exactly this work-up, and none to treating the eye:\n\n| Step | What the deck prints |\n|---|---|\n| Where the embolus came from | \"Types of emboli: **Cardiac · Carotid**\" — cholesterol (Hollenhorst plaques), fibrinoplatelet (\"may cause **amaurosis fugax**\"), calcific (\"may cause permanent obstruction\") |\n| Other causes | \"**Atherosclerosis** — most common cause of central artery occlusion\"; periarteritis; and in the young, \"protein S deficiency, protein C deficiency, antithrombin III deficiency, sticky platelet syndromes and antiphospholipid antibody syndrome\" |\n| Imaging the carotid | \"**Duplex scanning** — high resolution real-time B-scan ultrasonography with Doppler flow analysis\"; digital intravenous subtraction angiography; MR angiography (\"also images ischaemic cerebral lesions\"); intra-arterial angiography (\"excellent visualization · potential morbidity · seldom performed\") |\n| Treatment of the carotid | Medical: \"**Aspirin 75 mg daily** · aspirin + dipyridamole · clopidogrel 75 mg daily · anticoagulants if antiplatelet therapy ineffective\". Surgical: \"**Carotid endarterectomy** — patients with other risk factors for stroke · **symptomatic carotid stenosis > 70 %**\" |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** each promises more recovery than the disease allows. **A** and **B** claim *complete* recovery, which contradicts \"very poor prognosis\" outright — and A additionally forgets the fellow eye and the brain. **C** is the closest of the three and still overstates: \"moderate visual recovery\" is what you might hope for in a *branch* occlusion (`L19.2`: BRAO, \"VA - variable\") or where a **cilioretinal artery** spares the macula (`opqb-t13-516`), and this patient has neither — he has no perception of light.\n\n**⚠️ Source note.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` diagnoses this exact case and then leaves its management slide **entirely blank**, so the acute treatment is unsourced (see `opqb-t13-524`). The prognosis and the whole prophylactic pathway above **are** printed, in `L19.2`, and are quoted.",
    objective:'Central retinal artery occlusion has a very poor visual prognosis, so management aims at finding the embolic source and preventing the next event in the fellow eye or the brain.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q567), key p.92 (explanation box printed); stem restated from Q566 (printed as "In the Previous case")' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-568', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **female patient** is receiving **contraceptive pills for a long time**. She complained lately of a **rapid painless drop of vision in one eye**. The **most important diagnostic sign** is:',
    options:['Double vision.','Abnormal pupillary reactions.','Posterior synechia','Corneal edema'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS C, \"POSTERIOR SYNECHIA\". IT IS KEYED EXACTLY AS PRINTED AND IT IS NOT DISPUTED — but it does not fit the vignette, and the discrepancy is set out here so that the question can still be learned from.**\n\n**What the vignette is.** A woman on the combined pill with a rapid, painless, monocular drop of vision is a **central retinal vein occlusion** — the bank keys that diagnosis itself at `opqb-t13-522` and keys fundus examination as the investigation at `opqb-t13-574`. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` lists \"**Abnormalities of coagulation**\" as the fourth systemic predisposing factor for retinal vein occlusion, and the combined oral contraceptive is the only thing in this history that puts her there.\n\n**Why option B is the sign the deck actually gives.** `L19.2` prints an afferent pupillary defect in the sign list of **both** forms of CRVO, and makes it the discriminator between them:\n\n| | Non-ischaemic CRVO | Ischaemic CRVO |\n|---|---|---|\n| VA | > CF | < 6/60 |\n| **APD** | **mild** | **marked** |\n\nThat is not a minor detail. The APD is what separates a guarded prognosis from one carrying \"**Rubeosis irides in 50 %**\" and monthly review for six months, so it is the sign that changes management. \"Abnormal pupillary reactions\" is a fair description of it.\n\n**What posterior synechia actually is.** Adhesions between the posterior iris surface and the anterior lens capsule, formed by inflammatory exudate in **anterior uveitis**. Their presence means the eye is or has been inflamed — an irregular pupil that dilates poorly, sometimes festooned. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` is the deck that teaches them. A vein occlusion is not an inflammatory disease of the anterior segment; it produces no synechiae, and the eye is quiet and white.\n\n**Why the other two are also wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Double vision.** | Binocular diplopia is a **motility** problem — a cranial nerve palsy or a restrictive orbitopathy. It has nothing to do with a retinal vascular occlusion, and a patient who has lost the vision of one eye is in fact **less** likely to be diplopic, because there is no second image to fuse. |\n| **Corneal edema** | An **anterior** media problem, and it belongs to the disease this vignette is written to exclude: acute angle-closure glaucoma, which is **painful**. `opqb-t13-522` offers angle closure as its option D for the same reason. |\n\n**⚠️ A note on the shape of the error, recorded as an observation and nothing more:** this is the third printing of the same vignette in the topic, and all three key the letter **C** — Q522 C (central retinal vein occlusion, correct), Q574 C (fundus examination, correct) and this one C (posterior synechia, which fits nothing). The pattern is consistent with a key copied down a column. **The `answer` field carries C as printed; a defective key is noted, never disputed.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The vignette is a contraceptive-associated central retinal vein occlusion, whose printed sign in the deck is an afferent pupillary defect; the bank keys posterior synechia, which belongs to anterior uveitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q568), key p.92 — key recorded as printed; see explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-569', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A patient **wearing glasses since childhood** observed **flashes of light and muscae volitantes following a trauma to his eye**. The **first thing to do** is:',
    options:['Change his glasses.','Fundus examination.','Intra ocular pressure measurement.','Field of vision.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every element of the stem is a risk factor, and together they name one diagnosis to exclude today: a retinal break.**\n\n`L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives the causes of rhegmatogenous detachment as \"aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia** (nearsightedness), or **eye trauma**\" — and this patient has two of the three at once. \"Wearing glasses since childhood\" is the bank's shorthand for a myope who needed correction before school age, which means significant myopia. Then the symptoms: `L20`'s rhegmatogenous row is \"**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a 'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\", and he has the first two.\n\n**Why fundus examination is \"the first thing\".** The break is peripheral, and nothing except looking will find it. That means a **dilated** examination with indirect ophthalmoscopy, scleral indentation, or a three-mirror lens at the slit lamp (`opqb-t13-536` names the Goldmann lens for exactly this). What is found decides everything that follows:\n\n| Finding | Consequence |\n|---|---|\n| No break | Reassure; warn about the curtain and review |\n| **Break, retina still attached** | **Laser retinopexy in clinic** — this bank keys it at `opqb-t13-571` |\n| Break **with** detachment | Theatre — vitrectomy with gas or oil, scleral buckle, or pneumatic retinopexy (`L20` slide 14) |\n\n**Note what he does NOT have**: no curtain, no field defect, no drop in acuity. That is the good news and the reason for urgency in the same breath — the retina is probably still on, and the window in which a laser will do instead of an operation is open now.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not first |\n|---|---|\n| **Change his glasses.** | Treats the least important thing in the history as if it were the complaint. He has not asked for glasses and a refractive change does not produce photopsia — a new spectacle prescription would send a patient with a fresh retinal tear home. |\n| **Intra ocular pressure measurement.** | Part of any post-traumatic examination, and it should be done — blunt injury can cause angle recession or a hyphaema with raised pressure. But it does not address the symptoms, and a **low** pressure would in fact be a *clue to* a detachment rather than a diagnosis of one (`opqb-t13-520`: \"simple RD usually has low or normal IOP\"). |\n| **Field of vision.** | Would map a defect **once there is one**, which is to say once the retina has already detached. It is a late test in this sequence, subjective, and it cannot show a break. |\n\n**Compare the neighbours in this topic**, all four built on the same set-up: `opqb-t13-545` (flashes only, asks the cause), `opqb-t13-558` (flashes, floaters **and** a veil, asks the key to diagnosis — the same answer), `opqb-t13-571` (a break found, asks the treatment), and `opqb-t13-536` (a diabetic myope with a clear fundus, where ultrasound is the false statement).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Flashes and floaters after trauma in a lifelong myope mean a retinal break until the dilated periphery has been examined; nothing else on the list can find one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q569), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-570', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A patient in the **recovery of an open-heart surgery** complained of **sudden painless loss of vision of one eye**. On **fundus examination** the expected finding is:',
    options:['Dilated engorged central retinal vein.','Cherry red spot.','Optic nerve head edema.','Wide spread retinal hemorrhages.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Open-heart surgery → **embolic CRAO likely → cherry red spot.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Cardiac surgery is an embolic event by construction, and the retina is the one place an embolus can be seen with the naked eye.**\n\n`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives the causes of retinal artery occlusion as \"**Embolism**\" and \"Vaso-obliteration\", and names the embolic sources first: \"Types of emboli: **Cardiac · Carotid**\". Cardiopulmonary bypass supplies every mechanism at once — the aorta is cross-clamped and cannulated, so atheromatous plaque is dislodged from the arch; valves are handled or replaced, so calcific and thrombotic material is released; the circuit itself generates platelet–fibrin aggregates and gas microbubbles. Anything that reaches the ascending aorta can travel up the carotid, into the ophthalmic artery, and lodge in the central retinal artery, which is the first vessel of that calibre downstream. The deck names all three particle types — \"cholesterol emboli (**Hollenhorst plaques**) … often located at arteriolar bifurcations\"; \"**fibrinoplatelet** emboli … may cause amaurosis fugax and occasionally permanent obstruction\"; \"**calcific** emboli … usually single, white and close to disc · **may cause permanent obstruction**\".\n\n**And the fundus finding of CRAO is the cherry-red spot**, printed in the deck's sign list — \"Retinal whitening · '**Cherry-red spot**' at macula · arteriolar and venular narrowing\" — and reproduced in `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` Case 1: \"a **white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot**\". It is not a lesion but the *normal* macula seen against the infarcted white background, because the fovea has no inner layers to swell and its choroidal supply is intact. Written out in full at `opqb-t13-530`; the anatomy is at `opqb-t13-546`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each belongs to a different disease:**\n\n| Option | Which disease |\n|---|---|\n| **Dilated engorged central retinal vein.** | **CRVO** — `L19.2`, ischaemic form: \"**Marked venous tortuosity and engorgement**\". The opposite hydraulics. In CRAO the veins **narrow** along with the arterioles, and the residual column breaks into segments (\"cattle-trucking\"). |\n| **Optic nerve head edema.** | Papilloedema, papillitis, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, or the disc oedema of CRVO and malignant hypertension. Bypass can cause a **perioperative ischaemic optic neuropathy**, which is a genuine complication of long procedures with hypotension and anaemia — but the bank's box names embolic CRAO, and an embolic occlusion of the retinal artery does not swell the disc. |\n| **Wide spread retinal hemorrhages.** | Vein occlusion again — \"**Extensive** retinal haemorrhages\" in ischaemic CRVO, or \"Superficial hemorrhage all over the fundus\" as `opqb-t13-519` puts it. An arterial occlusion cannot bleed, because there is no pressure head arriving to force blood into the tissue. |\n\n**The pattern worth carrying:** ask what has been done to the patient. Cardiac surgery, carotid instrumentation and angiography all mean **emboli**; the pill and thrombophilia mean **venous thrombosis** (`opqb-t13-522`); systemic hypertension and renal failure mean **leakage and disc oedema** (`opqb-t13-547`).",
    objective:'Open-heart surgery showers emboli, so sudden painless monocular loss afterwards is embolic central retinal artery occlusion — a white retina with a cherry-red spot.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q570), key p.92 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-571', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A **high myopic patient** received **eye trauma** followed by **muscae volitantes and flashes of light**. On examination there was a **retinal tear WITHOUT detachment**. What of the following is **best for treatment**?',
    options:['Glasses.','Contact lens.','Laser photocoagulation of the tear.','Buckling of the tear.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The four words that decide this are \"without detachment\". Catch a break before the retina comes off and the whole problem shrinks from an operation to an outpatient laser.**\n\n**What the laser does.** Rows of burns are placed in the retina **around** the tear, deliberately not in it. Each burn produces a small chorioretinal adhesion — a controlled scar welding the sensory retina to the retinal pigment epithelium and choroid. Within about a week those adhesions are strong enough to form a watertight barrier, so vitreous fluid passing through the tear can no longer track outwards under the surrounding retina. The tear stays; what is prevented is the detachment. It is done at the slit lamp or with an indirect delivery system, takes minutes, needs only topical anaesthesia, and the eye is not opened.\n\n**Why buckling is the wrong operation here, not merely a bigger one.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives scleral buckling as a treatment for an established rhegmatogenous **detachment** — \"a **silicone band** is placed around the eye to push the eye wall inward, helping the retina **reattach**\", alongside vitrectomy with gas or oil and pneumatic retinopexy. It is an intraocular-adjacent procedure with real morbidity: induced myopia and astigmatism, diplopia from disturbed muscle function, infection or extrusion of the explant, and, in a **high myope**, a sclera that is already thin. There is nothing here to reattach. The option is also loosely worded — one buckles an **eye**, not a tear.\n\n**Why the first two are wrong:** glasses and contact lenses are **refractive** devices. They change where the image forms; they do not touch the retina. He is a high myope, so he needs correction — but correcting his refraction while leaving a retinal break unsealed is treating the wrong problem, and a well-corrected eye can detach as easily as a blurred one.\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE NOTE — MEASURED. Prophylactic laser retinopexy for a break WITHOUT detachment is on `L20`'s own NOT-COVERED list**, which records \"**LASER RETINOPEXY / prophylactic barrage laser for a break WITHOUT detachment**\" among the things the deck never delivers, along with break morphology and macula-on/macula-off timing. The deck does print laser and cryotherapy, but only as an adjunct **after** pneumatic retinopexy in an established detachment: \"often followed by **laser or cryotherapy (freezing)**\". **The barrier mechanism and the outpatient setting above are therefore not taken from the course material.** What is sourced is the risk factor set — \"aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia**, or **eye trauma**\" — and the buckling description quoted.\n\n**The sequence this topic builds across four questions:** `opqb-t13-545` (flashes in a myope — suspect a break) → `opqb-t13-569` (flashes and floaters after trauma — examine the fundus) → **this question** (break found, retina on — laser) → `opqb-t13-558` (a veil has appeared — the retina is off, and now it is surgery).\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints \"**musca** volitanes\" here, singular, where `opqb-t13-558`, `opqb-t13-564` and `opqb-t13-569` print \"muscae\". Repaired here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A retinal break with the retina still attached is walled off with laser retinopexy in clinic; buckling is for an established detachment and refractive correction treats nothing.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q571), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-572', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'The following conditions can cause **tubular field except**:',
    options:['Open angle Glaucoma.','C.R.V.O with macular edema.','C.R.A.O. with cilioretinal artery.','Advanced case of retinitis pigmentosa.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A tubular (tunnel) field is PERIPHERAL field lost with a CENTRAL island preserved. Three of the four options destroy the periphery and spare the centre; the fourth does the exact opposite.**\n\n| Option | What it destroys | Field result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Open angle Glaucoma.** | Ganglion cell axons in the arcuate bundles, progressively, from the periphery inwards; the papillomacular bundle is spared until the end | `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` prints it directly: \"**Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)**\", and lists \"**Tubular field** / temporal\" among the late findings. The classic end-stage glaucomatous field. |\n| **C.R.A.O. with cilioretinal artery.** | The whole inner retina *except* the macular wedge fed by the cilioretinal artery, which arises from the posterior ciliary circulation and is **upstream of the block** (`L19.2`: \"derived from posterior ciliary circulation · present in about 30 % of individuals\"; and on FA, \"**Early filling of cilioretinal artery** · non-filling of other vessels\") | Peripheral field gone in one stroke, central island kept — the mechanism `opqb-t13-516` keys. A tubular field made in seconds. |\n| **Advanced case of retinitis pigmentosa.** | Rods first, and rods are concentrated in the mid-periphery; the cone-rich macula survives longest | `opqb-t13-527`'s own printed box: \"Decreased peripheral vision '**tunnel vision**' (due to rod atrophy)\", with \"decreased central vision (macular affection)\" listed *after* it. The textbook tunnel field. |\n\n**Why CRVO with macular oedema is the exception.** Macular oedema is a **central** lesion: fluid accumulates at the fovea, blurs and distorts central vision and produces a **central scotoma**. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` calls chronic macular oedema \"the **most common cause of persistent poor VA**\" after a vein occlusion, and `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` defines the whole of clinically significant macular oedema by distances **from the centre of the fovea** — 500 µm, 1500 µm. That is the inverse of a tubular field: the island in the middle is the part that has been lost, and the periphery is what remains.\n\n**The rule underneath the question:** ask **which photoreceptors, and where**. Rods and the arcuate ganglion cell bundles are peripheral, so diseases that attack them tunnel the field. Cones and the papillomacular bundle are central, so diseases that attack them scotomatise it. A CRVO can certainly constrict the field if it is severely ischaemic — but the *macular oedema* named in the option is a central problem by definition, and the option names it deliberately.\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank points the abbreviations inconsistently, \"C.R.V.O\" without a final stop and \"C.R.A.O.\" with one. Kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A tubular field is peripheral loss with a central island — glaucoma, cilioretinal-spared CRAO and advanced retinitis pigmentosa all produce one; macular oedema takes the centre instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q572), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-573', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **hypertensive patient** developed an **acute attack of marked diminution of vision**. On fundus examination all the following can be present **except**:',
    options:['Macular star.','Macular hole.','Retinal vein occlusion,','Retinal artery occlusion.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hypertension damages the eye through its blood vessels. A macular hole is a mechanical, vitreoretinal disease with no vascular component at all — nothing about a high blood pressure makes one.**\n\nA macular hole is a full-thickness dehiscence at the fovea produced by **anteroposterior and tangential traction** from the vitreous as it separates. It gives a slowly progressive central scotoma and metamorphopsia, not an acute attack, and it is treated surgically by vitrectomy with membrane peeling and a gas tamponade. Nothing in that chain involves arterial pressure.\n\n**Why the other three can all appear in this patient — `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` supplies each of them:**\n\n| Option | Where the deck puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Macular star.** | Hypertensive retinopathy, extravascular signs: \"Flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages · **Cotton-wool spots and macular star** · Disc oedema\". The star is radial hard exudate at the macula and is a sign of the **malignant** phase — `opqb-t13-539` keys it to malignant hypertension directly. |\n| **Retinal vein occlusion,** | \"Ocular associations of hypertension: **Retinal vein occlusion** · retinal artery macroaneurysm · anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy · ocular motor nerve palsies.\" And hypertension is the second entry on the deck's own systemic predisposing-factor list for vein occlusion. A CRVO is a classic cause of acute marked visual loss in a hypertensive. |\n| **Retinal artery occlusion.** | Hypertension accelerates atherosclerosis, and \"**Atherosclerosis** — most common cause of central artery occlusion\". Also embolic, from a hypertensive heart or carotid. |\n\n**Two more hypertensive causes of an acute drop that the option list does not offer**, from the same slides, so the pattern is not read too narrowly: **anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy** (named among the ocular associations) and a **ruptured retinal artery macroaneurysm** — \"typically affects **elderly hypertensive females** · localized dilatation of retinal arteriole · most frequently along temporal arcades\", whose rupture gives \"pre-retinal and retinal haemorrhage · subretinal haemorrhage\".\n\n**⚠️ SOURCE NOTE — MEASURED. \"Macular hole\" returns 0 hits across all thirty cached lecture decks and all twelve cached book chapters** (case-insensitive, every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`). The entity is not taught anywhere in this course, and **the description above is not taken from the course material**. This bank nevertheless uses it as a distractor three times — here, at `opqb-t13-537` and at `opqb-t13-539`.\n\n**Transcription note:** option C is printed \"Retinal vein occlusion,\" with a comma. Kept as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hypertension acts through vessels — macular star, vein occlusion and artery occlusion all follow; a macular hole is vitreoretinal traction and has no vascular cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.88 (Q573), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-574', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **female patient on contraceptive pills for 5 years** developed **painless rapid marked diminution of vision in one eye**. What is the **most diagnostic clinical examination** you would do?',
    options:['Tonometry.','Angle of the anterior chamber.','Fundus examination.','Field of vision.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Painless vision drop on contraceptives → **CRVO** → **fundus exam is diagnostic.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The disease is behind the eye, so the examination has to be of the back of the eye.** This is the third printing of the same vignette in this topic — the full account of why the combined pill causes a central retinal vein occlusion is written at `opqb-t13-522`, and the family is Q522 (the diagnosis) ‖ `opqb-t13-568` (the sign) ‖ this question (the examination).\n\n**Why fundoscopy is *diagnostic* and not merely suggestive.** A CRVO does not need a test; it needs to be looked at. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints a fundus picture so characteristic that the diagnosis is made on sight, and grades the severity on the same view:\n\n| Sign | Non-ischaemic | Ischaemic |\n|---|---|---|\n| Veins | Mild tortuosity and dilatation | **Marked tortuosity and engorgement** |\n| Haemorrhages | Mild to moderate | **Extensive** — all four quadrants |\n| Cotton-wool spots | Variable | Variable |\n| Disc | Mild to moderate oedema | **Severe disc oedema** |\n| Macula | Chronic oedema | Macular ischaemia |\n\nA fundus scattered with flame and dot-blot haemorrhages in every quadrant, tortuous engorged veins and a swollen disc is a picture no other disease produces. `opqb-t13-519` calls it \"superficial hemorrhage **all over the fundus**\" — the phrase that separates a central from a branch occlusion, which bleeds in one sector only.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and note that the first two both belong to the disease the word \"painless\" has already excluded:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Tonometry.** | Measures intraocular pressure. It is the examination for **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, which is the painful sudden loss, and it would be normal here. There is a real connection in the other direction — `L19.2` lists \"**Raised intraocular pressure**\" as an *ocular* predisposing factor for vein occlusion, and `opqb-t13-578` keys open-angle glaucoma as a predisposing factor — but a normal pressure does not diagnose a CRVO and a raised one would not either. |\n| **Angle of the anterior chamber.** | Gonioscopy, again the angle-closure examination. It becomes relevant to *this* patient months later, if the occlusion proves ischaemic: `L19.2` prescribes \"check every month for 6 months · look for rubeosis and **angle new vessels**\", because of \"Rubeosis irides in **50 %**\". That is surveillance for a complication, not the diagnostic examination today. |\n| **Field of vision.** | Would show a defect and tell you nothing about its cause. Subjective, slow, and it cannot distinguish a vein occlusion from an artery occlusion, a detachment or an optic neuropathy — all of which the fundus separates at a glance. |\n\n**⚠️ The word \"clinical\" is doing work in the stem.** It rules out fluorescein angiography, which is an *investigation* and which this bank keys for a different question at `opqb-t13-551` — after the diagnosis is made, to decide whether the eye is ischaemic.",
    objective:'A central retinal vein occlusion is diagnosed by looking: four-quadrant haemorrhages, engorged tortuous veins and a swollen disc — a picture tonometry, gonioscopy and perimetry cannot give.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q574), key p.92 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-575', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'A **55-year-old female** presented with **severe headache and blurring of vision**. Best-corrected visual acuity was **6/9 right and 6/6 left**. Fundus examination showed **ill-defined optic disc edges and flame shaped hemorrhages around the disc**. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Raised blood pressure.','Raised blood sugar.','Raised intracranial pressure.','Optic neuritis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two features in the stem are doing all the work, and they pull in the same direction: a SEVERE HEADACHE, and acuities that are almost NORMAL despite grossly swollen discs.**\n\n**Why near-normal acuity points at raised intracranial pressure.** Papilloedema is disc swelling caused mechanically — raised cerebrospinal fluid pressure is transmitted along the optic nerve sheath, axoplasmic transport stalls at the lamina cribrosa, and the axons swell where they are dammed. The **conducting** function of those axons is preserved for a long time, so central acuity stays good until the swelling has been present for months. What is lost early is the **blind spot**, which enlarges, and the peripheral field, which constricts. A patient who sees 6/9 and 6/6 with discs like this has a mechanical problem, not a destructive one. `L21) Optic Nerve` prints a papilloedema-versus-papillitis table for exactly this contrast, and its papilloedema photographs show the same appearance.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Raised blood pressure.** | The closest rival, and it must be excluded before anything else is done — malignant hypertension produces the same three signs. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives its extravascular signs as \"**Flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages** · cotton-wool spots and macular star · **disc oedema**\". What is missing here is the rest of the hypertensive fundus: no cotton-wool spots, no **macular star**, no haemorrhages away from the disc, and no arteriolar attenuation or arteriovenous crossing changes. The haemorrhages are described as **around the disc**, which is where papilloedema puts them. Compare `opqb-t13-547`, where the same signs in a patient with **renal failure** key blood-pressure measurement as the first step. |\n| **Raised blood sugar.** | Diabetic retinopathy is a disease of the **retinal capillary bed**, not of the disc, and it does not cause headache. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` gives its signs as microaneurysms, dot-and-blot haemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots, venous beading, IRMA and neovascularisation — none of them a swollen disc with a normal-looking retina beyond it. (Diabetic papillopathy exists but is rare and not taught here.) |\n| **Optic neuritis.** | Wrong acuity, wrong laterality, wrong pupil. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` Case 2 is a papillitis, and the profile is the opposite of this: a **24-year-old** woman, **unilateral**, \"OD 20/20, **OS 20/100**\", with \"a relative afferent pupillary defect OS\". Optic neuritis drops acuity early and severely and gives a marked RAPD; this patient has 6/9 and 6/6 and no pupil abnormality is mentioned. Its headache is also different in kind — pain **on eye movement**, not a severe generalised headache. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the source overlap that could mislead.** `L31,32` slide 22 describes an **AION** fundus in wording almost identical to this stem: \"the right optic disc is swollen, and there area [sic] **flame-shaped hemorrhages around the disc**\". But that patient is **90 years old**, with a right-sided headache, weight loss, fatigue, an RAPD and **inferior altitudinal field loss** — the giant-cell arteritis picture — and her disease is **unilateral**. This patient is 55, has near-normal acuity in both eyes and no altitudinal defect. The overlapping description is a reminder that a swollen disc with peripapillary flame haemorrhages is a *sign*, and the diagnosis comes from the company it keeps.\n\n**What follows:** measure the blood pressure at the bedside, then image the brain and, if imaging is normal, measure the CSF pressure. `opqb-t13-523` asks the other ocular consequence of the same disease — the sixth nerve palsy that raised intracranial pressure produces as a false localising sign.\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option D as \"Optic neuritis,\" with a comma. Repaired here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Severe headache with bilateral blurred disc margins, peripapillary flame haemorrhages and near-normal acuity is papilloedema from raised intracranial pressure, not hypertensive retinopathy or optic neuritis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q575), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-576', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro',
    stem:'Occlusion of the **lower nasal branch of the central retinal artery** results in one of the following **field defects**:',
    options:['Lower nasal sector field defect.','Upper nasal sector field defect.','Upper temporal sector field defect.','Lower temporal sector field defect.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The retinal image is inverted on BOTH axes, so a retinal quadrant maps to the diagonally opposite field quadrant. Flip up-down AND nasal-temporal, and the answer follows in one step.**\n\n| Step | Retina | Field |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vertical flip | **lower** | **upper** |\n| Horizontal flip | **nasal** | **temporal** |\n| Result | lower nasal retina | **upper temporal field defect** |\n\n**Why both flips happen.** The eye is a single convex lens system with the nodal point behind the pupil, so every ray crossing it is reversed in both meridians — exactly like a camera. Light from the upper field lands on the lower retina; light from the temporal field crosses the nodal point and lands on the **nasal** retina. Destroy a patch of retina and the patient loses the part of the field that used to project onto it, which is the diagonally opposite quadrant.\n\n**Why it is a SECTOR and not a scotoma.** A retinal arteriole supplies a wedge-shaped territory with essentially no collateral, and the wedge boundaries are respected absolutely — that is what makes a branch occlusion the sharpest field defect in ophthalmology. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives branch retinal artery occlusion as \"VA - variable · APD mild or absent · **Retina whitening** · Arteriolar narrowing\", and the whitening stops dead at the territory edge. `opqb-t13-540` uses the same logic to key sectorial and altitudinal defects to branch vein occlusion, branch artery occlusion and retinal detachment — all conditions with a sharp anatomical boundary — and to exclude commotion retinae, which has none.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** each performs the wrong number of flips.\n\n| Option | Error |\n|---|---|\n| **Lower nasal sector field defect.** | No flip at all — reads the retinal quadrant straight off as a field quadrant. The commonest mistake. |\n| **Upper nasal sector field defect.** | Vertical flip only; forgets that nasal retina sees the temporal world. |\n| **Lower temporal sector field defect.** | Horizontal flip only; forgets that lower retina sees the upper world. |\n\n**Where the same inversion turns up elsewhere in this topic:** `opqb-t13-545` places flashes in the **temporal** field, which means traction on the **nasal** retina; `opqb-t13-558` gives a **lower** black veil, which means a **superior** detachment — and a superior detachment is the more urgent one, because gravity carries the fluid down towards the macula.\n\n**⚠️ Do not confuse this with a chiasmal or retro-chiasmal defect.** Those respect the **vertical** meridian and are defined in *field* terms from the outset (bitemporal, homonymous). Retinal defects respect vascular territories and have to be translated. `opqb-t13-517` covers the projection that makes the chiasmal rules work.\n\n*(The optics of the inversion and the absence of collateral supply between arteriolar territories are **not taken from the course material**; the BRAO sign list is printed in `L19.2`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A retinal quadrant projects to the diagonally opposite field quadrant, so a lower nasal branch artery occlusion produces an upper temporal sector field defect.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q576), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-577', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **50-year-old female, diabetic for 5 years**, presented with a **sudden marked drop of vision of the left eye**. On examination the **right fundus was normal**; the **left fundus showed edema of the optic disc and retinal hemorrhages**. This picture is suggestive of:',
    options:['Non proliferative diabetic retinopathy.','Proliferative diabetic retinopathy.','Left central vein occlusion.','Left central artery occlusion.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One word excludes both diabetic options, and it is not a fundus finding: the right fundus is NORMAL.**\n\n**Diabetic retinopathy is a bilateral, symmetrical, metabolic disease.** It affects the retinal capillary bed of both eyes at once, because both eyes are exposed to the same blood. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists its adverse risk factors as systemic throughout — \"long duration of diabetes · poor metabolic control · pregnancy · hypertension · renal disease\" — and every sign it prints is a capillary sign found in both eyes. A completely normal fellow fundus in a diabetic of five years is therefore strong evidence that whatever is happening in the left eye is **not** diabetic retinopathy.\n\n**The other two words are \"sudden\" and \"disc oedema\".** Diabetic vision loss is gradual when it comes from the macula and sudden only when there is **blood in the vitreous** or a **tractional detachment** (`opqb-t13-515`, `opqb-t13-549`) — neither of which is described here, and neither of which swells the optic disc.\n\n**What is described is `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`' CRVO panel, item for item:**\n\n| Finding in the stem | `L19.2`, central retinal vein occlusion |\n|---|---|\n| Sudden marked drop, one eye | Ischaemic: \"VA < 6/60\", \"APD - marked\" |\n| **Oedema of the optic disc** | \"**Severe disc oedema**\" (ischaemic) / \"mild to moderate disc oedema\" (non-ischaemic) |\n| **Retinal haemorrhages** | \"**Extensive retinal haemorrhages**\" / \"mild to moderate retinal haemorrhages\" |\n| Unilateral | The occlusion is of one vessel in one eye |\n\nDiabetes is not incidental to the story, though — it is a **cause**. The deck's systemic predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion are \"increasing age · hypertension · **diabetes** · abnormalities of coagulation\", and this woman has two of the four. The diabetes explains why she got a vein occlusion; it does not explain the fundus.\n\n**Why the last option is wrong:** a **central retinal ARTERY** occlusion gives the opposite fundus — \"Retinal whitening · '**Cherry-red spot**' at macula · **arteriolar and venular narrowing** · sludging and segmentation of blood column\". No disc oedema, and **no haemorrhages**, because an occluded artery delivers no pressure head to force blood into the tissue (`opqb-t13-530`, `opqb-t13-562`). Its onset is instantaneous rather than sudden-over-hours, and its acuity loss is usually more profound.\n\n**What happens next.** The distinction that matters now is ischaemic versus non-ischaemic, because that decides the surveillance: `L19.2` prescribes \"check every month for 6 months · look for **rubeosis** and angle new vessels · treat neovascularization by **panretinal photocoagulation**\" for the ischaemic form, which carries \"Rubeosis irides in **50 %**\" and a very poor prognosis. Fluorescein angiography makes that call (`opqb-t13-551`), and the pupil gives an immediate clue at the bedside — mild APD in the non-ischaemic form, marked in the ischaemic.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A normal fellow fundus excludes diabetic retinopathy, which is bilateral; unilateral sudden loss with disc oedema and retinal haemorrhages is a central retinal vein occlusion, for which diabetes is a predisposing factor.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q577), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-578', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'Each of the following can be a **predisposing factor for central retinal vein occlusion except**:',
    options:['Hypertension','Open angle glaucoma.','Hyper mature senile cataract.','Diabetes mellitus.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the predisposing factors as a two-column slide, and three of the four options are on it verbatim:**\n\n| Systemic | Ocular |\n|---|---|\n| Increasing age | **Raised intraocular pressure** |\n| **Hypertension** | Periphlebitis |\n| **Diabetes** | |\n| Abnormalities of coagulation | |\n\n**Hypertension** and **diabetes** are the second and third systemic entries. **Open-angle glaucoma** is \"raised intraocular pressure\", the first ocular entry — and the mechanism is worth understanding rather than memorising, because it is mechanical. The central retinal vein and the central retinal artery leave the eye together through the **lamina cribrosa**, sharing a common adventitial sheath in a rigid opening. A chronically raised intraocular pressure compresses the vein at that point, where it is thin-walled and cannot resist; flow slows, the endothelium is damaged, and thrombosis follows. `L19.2`'s own pathophysiology chart takes it from there: \"Venous Occlusion → **Stagnation** → increased extravascular pressure and hypoxia → oedema and haemorrhage\".\n\n**Why hypermature cataract is the exception.** A cataract is an opacity **inside the lens capsule**. Its complications are all anterior-segment and all lens-derived, and the Lens topic of this bank sets them out: **phacolytic glaucoma**, where liquefied cortex leaks proteins that clog the trabecular meshwork (`opqb-t9-351`, `opqb-t9-405`), **phacomorphic glaucoma**, where a swollen intumescent lens shuts the angle (`opqb-t9-404`), lens-induced uveitis, and subluxation as the zonules degenerate. None of these acts on the retinal venous outflow at the optic nerve head. A cataract also does not raise the pressure by simply being there.\n\n**⚠️ The near-miss worth noticing, because it is the reason the distractor was chosen.** A hypermature cataract *can* cause a secondary glaucoma — and a secondary glaucoma *is* a raised intraocular pressure, which *is* an ocular predisposing factor for vein occlusion. So there is a chain. But it is two steps long and it runs through a complication that may never occur; the cataract itself predisposes to nothing. The question asks what predisposes to CRVO, and open-angle glaucoma does so **directly and chronically**, over years, which is exactly the exposure a slowly thrombosing vein needs.\n\n**A second reason the option is wrong in practice:** a hypermature cataract makes the fundus **unexaminable**, so it hides retinal disease rather than causing it — which is why an eye with dense cataract is scanned before surgery (`opqb-t9-371`) and why ERG is offered \"if macular disease suspected\" (`L18) Cataract II`).\n\n**Elsewhere in this topic:** `opqb-t13-522` builds the coagulation-abnormality row into a vignette, `opqb-t13-577` builds the diabetes row into one, and `opqb-t13-551` and `opqb-t13-574` follow the same disease through investigation and examination.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hypertension, diabetes and raised intraocular pressure from open-angle glaucoma all predispose to central retinal vein occlusion; a hypermature cataract acts only inside the lens and on the anterior segment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q578), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-579', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:'The **electroretinogram** is useful in assessing the function of the **outer layers of the retina**, especially where the **media are opaque**. It is **not of value** in the case of:',
    options:['Chorioretinal degeneration.','Open angle glaucoma.','Retinitis pigmentosa.','Macular degeneration.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem gives you the rule and then asks you to apply it: the ERG reports on the OUTER retina. Glaucoma is a disease of the INNER retina, so the ERG has nothing to say about it.**\n\n**Where each disease sits in the retinal layers.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 4 lists them from inner to outer — nerve fibre, ganglion cell, inner plexiform, inner nuclear, outer plexiform, outer nuclear, rods and cones, retinal pigment epithelium:\n\n| Disease | Layer affected | ERG |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Open angle glaucoma.** | **Ganglion cells and their axons** — the innermost layers | **Normal**, even in advanced disease. The photoreceptors are healthy and keep generating the response the ERG records. |\n| **Chorioretinal degeneration.** | Choroid, RPE and photoreceptors — the outermost layers | Reduced, often extinguished |\n| **Retinitis pigmentosa.** | Rods first, then cones, with RPE migration | Reduced or flat, and reduced **before symptoms** — which is why it is the test at `opqb-t13-560` and `opqb-t13-561` |\n| **Macular degeneration.** | RPE and photoreceptors at the macula | Full-field ERG can be near normal because the macula is a small fraction of the retinal area, but a **focal or multifocal** ERG is abnormal — and `L18) Cataract II` prescribes it in exactly this situation: \"**Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected**\" |\n\n**Why the ERG is blind to glaucoma.** The full-field electroretinogram is a **mass potential** generated by the photoreceptors (the a-wave) and by the bipolar and Müller cells (the b-wave). The ganglion cells contribute very little to it. In glaucoma those ganglion cells are dying while everything outer remains intact, so the trace stays normal while the field is being destroyed. The tests that *do* detect glaucoma are the ones that look at the inner retina and the disc — perimetry and disc/RNFL assessment. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` builds its diagnosis on precisely those: field constriction \"(→ **tubular field**)\" and the cupped disc.\n\n**Why the \"opaque media\" clause is in the stem.** It is the ERG's principal practical indication. When a dense cataract or a vitreous haemorrhage prevents any view of the retina, the ERG answers the one question that matters before surgery — *is there a retina worth operating on?* — because it needs light to reach the photoreceptors but needs no image at all. That is the setting `L18` describes and the reason `opqb-t13-557` and `opqb-t13-563` key ERG **wrong** in patients whose media are clear.\n\n**⚠️ Source note — measured.** \"Electroretinogram\" occurs **once** in all thirty cached lecture decks, at `L18) Cataract II`:262 in the pre-operative cataract work-up; **EOG and VEP are named in no deck at all** (the only occurrences of \"VEP\" in the corpus are inside three decks' own NOT-COVERED lists). Retinitis pigmentosa is likewise taught nowhere — exactly two hits across the decks, neither of them teaching. **The layer-by-layer table and the a-wave/b-wave origins above are not taken from the course material.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The ERG is a mass response of the photoreceptors and bipolar cells, so it is normal in glaucoma, which destroys ganglion cells; it is abnormal in the outer-retinal diseases and works through opaque media.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.89 (Q579), key p.92' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-580', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A **young lady** presented with **bilateral sudden loss of vision**. On examination, visual acuity was **bilateral no PL**. The **pupillary reactions were normal** and **both fundi showed no abnormal finding**. In such a case we always suspect:',
    options:['Central retinal artery occlusion.','Retrobulbar neuritis.','Hysterical blindness.','Retinal detachment.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Bilateral no PL with normal fundi → **functional vision loss.** **(not Correlated symptoms with signs)**\n\n---\n\n*(The block above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including the bracketed line. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box's second line is the whole diagnostic principle: the symptoms and the signs do not correlate, and in this case they cannot.**\n\n**Why normal pupils and no perception of light are physiologically incompatible.** The pupillary light reflex and conscious vision share the same afferent limb as far as the optic tracts: retina → optic nerve → chiasm → tract, where the pupil fibres branch off to the pretectal nuclei before the rest of the signal continues to the lateral geniculate body and the cortex. If **no light at all** is reaching consciousness from either eye because of disease anywhere from the retina to the tracts, then the pupil fibres are equally cut off and the pupils **must** be unreactive. Reactive pupils prove that light is reaching the midbrain from both retinas. Either the lesion is behind the lateral geniculate — cortical blindness, which is a different presentation entirely and not on the list — or there is no organic lesion.\n\n**Why the other three are excluded, each by a specific finding in the stem:**\n\n| Option | The finding that excludes it |\n|---|---|\n| **Central retinal artery occlusion.** | Two exclusions. The **fundus is normal**, and CRAO produces a fundus that could not be more abnormal — `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`: \"Retinal whitening · '**Cherry-red spot**' at macula · arteriolar and venular narrowing\". And the **pupil**: CRAO gives a marked afferent defect, exactly as `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` Case 1 describes and `opqb-t13-566` reproduces. It is also almost never simultaneously bilateral. |\n| **Retrobulbar neuritis.** | The classic teaching phrase is that \"the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing\" — a normal fundus is expected, because the inflammation is **behind** the disc. So the fundus does **not** exclude it; the **pupils** do. A retrobulbar neuritis severe enough to abolish light perception bilaterally would give profoundly unreactive pupils. `L31,32`'s own optic-nerve case makes the pupil the finding: \"a relative afferent pupillary defect OS\". It would also usually be unilateral and painful on eye movement. |\n| **Retinal detachment.** | Bilateral simultaneous total detachment is vanishingly rare, and a detachment is **visible** — `L20`'s corrugated mobile retina, and `L31,32`'s ultra-widefield image of a raised rippled retina behind a demarcation line. A normal fundus excludes it outright. |\n\n**⚠️ How this diagnosis must be made, because it is the part the question does not ask.** Functional visual loss is a **diagnosis of exclusion**, never of first resort, and never made because a patient is young, female or distressed. It requires a normal examination *and* positive evidence that the visual system works — reactive pupils, as here, and objective tests such as optokinetic nystagmus elicited with a rotating drum, a mirror tracking test, or a normal VEP. That last is the subject of the very next question, `opqb-t13-581`.\n\n**Transcription note:** the bank prints option B as \"**Retrobulhar** neuritis\" and option C ends in a comma. Both repaired here.\n\n*(The anatomy of the pupillary afferent pathway and the objective bedside tests are **not taken from the course material** — no deck covers functional visual loss; `L31,32`'s NOT-COVERED list names it explicitly among the causes of sudden painless loss that get no slide.)*",
    objective:'Reactive pupils prove light is reaching the midbrain from both retinas, so bilateral no perception of light with normal pupils and normal fundi is functional visual loss.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.90 (Q580), key p.92 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t13-581', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
    stem:'A young lady presents with **bilateral sudden loss of vision**, acuity **bilateral no PL**, **normal pupillary reactions** and **no abnormal finding in either fundus**. In this case the **most useful investigation** is:',
    options:['Ultrasonography.','Computerized tomography.','Fluorescein angiography.','Visual evoked potential'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**VEP** detects retinal/optic nerve pathway function in opaque media.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE RESTATED. The bank prints only \"In the previous case…\", and the antecedent is `opqb-t13-580` — verified rather than assumed** (`opqb-t13-579` is a one-line ERG question with no patient in it). The vignette above is `opqb-t13-580`'s findings, restated because none of these options can be judged without them.\n\n**⚠️ THE BOX'S LAST THREE WORDS ARE COPIED FROM THE PREVIOUS QUESTION AND DO NOT APPLY HERE.** \"In opaque media\" is `opqb-t13-579`'s stem — this patient's media are clear, and the whole point of the case is that everything visible is normal. The key (D) is right; the justification printed with it is borrowed. Recorded, not disputed.\n\n**The real reason the VEP is the answer: it is the only OBJECTIVE test of the visual pathway on the list.** Everything else about this patient is a *statement* — she says she cannot see. The examination has already produced one piece of objective evidence against that (reactive pupils, which prove light reaches the midbrain from both retinas), and the VEP produces the second and stronger piece: a cortical potential recorded over the occipital scalp in response to a pattern-reversal or flash stimulus. It requires no cooperation and no report from the patient. A **normal VEP** in a patient claiming no perception of light demonstrates that the pathway from retina to occipital cortex conducts normally — which, taken with the normal pupils and normal fundi, establishes that the loss is **functional**. An abnormal VEP would send you looking for organic disease instead, which is why the test is worth doing rather than simply asserting the diagnosis.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is useless here |\n|---|---|\n| **Ultrasonography.** | Images **structure**, and only when the media are opaque or a mass is suspected. Both fundi are normal, so there is nothing structural to find and nothing blocking the view. |\n| **Computerized tomography.** | Would be the right test if the pupils were **abnormal** — bilateral visual loss with unreactive pupils, or with any neurological sign, demands imaging of the chiasm and the visual pathway. Here the pupils are normal, which is precisely the finding that argues against an organic lesion anywhere from retina to tract. CT is also poor at imaging the optic nerve and chiasm compared with MRI. |\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | Images the **retinal circulation**, and the retinae are normal. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` uses FA to show filling, non-perfusion and leakage; there is no vascular question in this case. |\n\n**The ERG/VEP division, which this pair of questions is really teaching:** the **ERG** tests the retina, mostly the outer layers, and is the test when the media are opaque (`opqb-t13-579`, and `L18) Cataract II`: \"Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected\"). The **VEP** tests the whole pathway through to the occipital cortex, and is the test when the eye looks normal but the patient cannot see.\n\n**⚠️ Source note — measured. VEP is named in NO cached lecture deck**; the only occurrences of the abbreviation across the corpus are inside `L21) Optic Nerve`, `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss`'s own NOT-COVERED lists, all of which record it as absent. Functional visual loss is likewise on `L31,32`'s absent list by name. **This entry is a gap-fill and none of the VEP description above is taken from the course material.**\n\n**Last question of the topic** — Q581 is the final printed number of Grade Gain topic 13.",
    objective:'A visual evoked potential objectively tests the whole pathway to the occipital cortex without the patient cooperating, and a normal trace confirms functional visual loss where pupils and fundi are already normal.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.90 (Q581), key p.92 (explanation box printed); stem restated from Q580 (printed as "In the previous case")' }
